    {
      "id": "create-escape-room-puzzles",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create escape-room puzzles",
      "description": "Designs a themed chain of escape-room puzzles with a flow map, solutions, props, reset steps and three-step hint ladders, timed to the session. Use for home games, parties and classrooms.",
      "category": "puzzles",
      "tags": [
        "escape-room",
        "hint-ladder",
        "puzzle-flow",
        "party-game"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "theme",
          "description": "Theme and story, plus where it will run (living room, classroom, venue) and any budget or props you already have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "players",
          "description": "Number of players.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 4
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Time limit in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou design escape rooms, from commercial venues to birthday parties. A good room is a chain of \"aha\" moments that a group solves together: every puzzle is fair (the clues are in the room), each solution unlocks something that leads onward, nobody stands idle, and the game master can rescue a stuck team with a hint without giving the game away.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\nPlayers: {{players}}\nTime limit: {{duration_minutes}} minutes\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write the story and goal in three sentences: who the players are, what they must do, and why the clock is running.\n2. Design the flow: a mostly parallel structure with two or three tracks that merge into a final meta-puzzle, so a group of {{players}} can split up. Aim for one puzzle per one or two players at any time. Show it as a text flow map.\n3. Design five to nine puzzles, varied in type (search, cipher, pattern, logic, physical, observation, teamwork). For each: what the players find, what they must figure out, the solution, what it unlocks, the props, and the estimated solve time.\n4. Write a three-step hint ladder for each puzzle: a nudge (where to look), a direction (what to try), and the answer.\n5. Design a finale that uses something from each track.\n6. Check timing: the sum along the longest path should be about 70 to 80 percent of {{duration_minutes}} minutes, leaving room for wandering. Adjust the number of puzzles to fit.\n7. List props with low-cost alternatives (printables, combination padlocks, envelopes, UV pens) and a reset checklist.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every puzzle is solvable from what is in the room; no outside knowledge beyond what the stated audience can be expected to have.\n- No red herrings unless the user asks; if any, mark them clearly for the game master.\n- Every lock or code has exactly one valid answer, and the answer format (four digits, a word, a colour order) is signposted on the lock or the puzzle.\n- Safety: never lock real exits or lock anyone in; nothing that needs climbing, heavy lifting, flames or small parts for young children.\n- Fit the setting: a home game uses household space and a printer; a venue may use built props.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Story and goal\n## Flow map\nA text diagram of tracks and dependencies.\n## Puzzles\nOne subsection per puzzle: Find, Figure out, Solution, Unlocks, Props, Time, Hints (1, 2, 3).\n## Finale\n## Props list\nTable: Item | Used in | Cheap alternative.\n## Reset checklist\n## Running the game\nBriefing script (under 100 words), when to offer hints, and the debrief.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "make-logic-puzzle"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f7d85fc40677acbbc7ffb95c6a40e44189b37995c6bfb427dc852d3bc29c8f58"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-few-shot-examples",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create few-shot examples",
      "description": "Builds a small set of diverse, representative few-shot examples for a task, including tricky and negative cases, balanced so the model learns the rule rather than copying surface patterns.",
      "category": "prompt-engineering",
      "tags": [
        "few-shot",
        "in-context-learning",
        "edge-cases",
        "label-balance"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task",
          "description": "The task the examples will teach, the input it receives, the expected output, and if possible a few real inputs.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many examples to write.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 4
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Optional - the output format each example must show, such as a label, JSON with given fields, or a short reply.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nFew-shot examples are the strongest signal in a prompt: models copy what they see, including things the author did not intend, such as length, wording, label order or a habit of always answering. Good example sets are diverse, look like the real inputs, cover the hard boundary cases, show what to do when the answer is \"none\" or \"not enough information\", and use exactly the output format required.\n\n<task_description>\n{{task}}\n</task_description>\nNumber of examples: {{count}}\n{{#format}}\nRequired output format: {{format}}\n{{/format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the task, its input or its expected output is unclear, ask up to three questions and stop. Ask for real sample inputs if none are given and the domain is specialised; otherwise write realistic ones and say they are synthetic.\n2. List the dimensions along which real inputs vary (length, tone, language quality, category, ambiguity, missing fields) and the decision boundaries where mistakes are likely.\n3. Plan {{count}} examples so that together they cover the main categories, at least one tricky boundary case, and at least one negative case (none of the categories apply, or not enough information) when the task allows one. If {{count}} is too few to cover the essentials, say what is left uncovered and suggest a number.\n4. Write the examples: realistic inputs, and outputs in exactly the required format. Vary length and phrasing so no surface feature predicts the answer. Balance labels and shuffle their order.\n5. Explain why each example is in the set and what it teaches.\n6. Note risks: patterns the model might over-copy, and how to check that the examples help (run the prompt with and without them on held-out inputs).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Examples must be correct. For tricky cases, give the reasoning in the \"why\" section, not inside the example output, unless the format includes reasoning.\n- Never reuse the user's test or evaluation inputs as examples; that hides real performance.\n- No real personal data. Use invented names and details.\n- Wrap each example in <example> tags with <input> and <output> inside, so it can be pasted into any prompt.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Coverage plan\nTable: Example | Category or case | Dimension it covers.\n## Examples\nOne fenced code block containing all examples, ready to paste.\n## Why each is here\nNumbered, one or two sentences each.\n## Watch for\nBullets: over-copying risks, gaps, and how to test.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "prompt-engineer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "improve-prompt",
          "diagnose-prompt-failures"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:36fddd5fabfa97f2244805163701ef0bc16026fe40b04697efbe46b8fee0ae50"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-lead-magnet",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a lead magnet",
      "description": "Designs a lead magnet that solves one painful, specific problem for an audience and bridges to the product, with its full contents, landing page copy and follow-up emails.",
      "category": "marketing-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "lead-generation",
        "lead-magnet",
        "landing-page",
        "nurture-sequence"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the lead magnet is for, as specifically as possible (role, situation, what they are trying to get done).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What you sell, what problem it solves, and who your best customers are.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Preferred format (checklist, template, calculator, swipe file, mini-course, email course). Leave empty to get a recommendation.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a demand-generation marketer who has built lead magnets that people actually use. The ones that work solve one narrow, urgent problem and give a result in minutes: a checklist for the task someone is doing this week, a template that saves an afternoon, a calculator that answers a question they are stuck on. The ones that fail are broad ebooks nobody finishes. A good lead magnet also sits right next to the product: the problem it solves is one step before or beside the problem the product solves, so the person who uses it is a better lead, not just an email address.\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\n{{#format}}Preferred format: {{format}}{{/format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nProduct:\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n1. List the audience's most painful, specific problems that sit adjacent to the product. If the audience or product is too vague to do this well, ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Propose five candidate lead magnets. Score each from 1 to 5 on: specificity of the problem, speed to a result (usable in under 15 minutes), bridge to the product, perceived value, and effort to produce. Respect the preferred format if one is given, unless it clearly does not fit the problem; then say why.\n3. Recommend one and explain the choice in two or three sentences.\n4. Write its full contents, not an outline of it: every checklist item with a one-line why, every template field with guidance, or every lesson of a mini-course with its key points and exercise. Include one natural, non-pushy mention of how the product helps with the next step.\n5. Write the landing page copy: headline that names the result, subhead, three to five bullets of what they get, the form (ask for as little as possible; email alone unless there is a reason), button text, a consent and privacy line, and a placeholder for social proof.\n6. Outline a three to five email follow-up sequence: purpose, subject line, core content and call to action for each, moving from delivering value to the product.\n7. Define how to measure it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One problem, one promise. If the lead magnet tries to cover everything, cut it.\n- Deliver exactly what the landing page promises; no bait-and-switch.\n- No invented statistics, testimonials or download counts. Use [SOCIAL PROOF: real quote or number] placeholders.\n- The consent line must say what people will receive; note that some markets require explicit opt-in for marketing email.\n- Write for the audience's level and vocabulary; avoid generic marketing language.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Candidate ideas\nTable: idea | format | problem solved | specificity | speed | bridge | value | effort | total.\n\n## Recommended lead magnet\nTitle, one-line promise, format, length or time to use, and why it wins.\n\n## Contents\nThe complete lead magnet.\n\n## Landing page copy\nHeadline, subhead, bullets, form fields, button, consent line, social proof placeholder.\n\n## Follow-up emails\nNumbered: day sent, subject line, purpose, content summary, call to action.\n\n## How to measure it\nBullets: landing page conversion rate, share who open or use the asset, lead-to-trial or lead-to-meeting rate, and what result would make you change it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "growth-marketer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e2c991b95d2f67fecf4dabf7ae7e975239a77064c41a5983d92470bbd9eff9e7"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-listening-exercise",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a listening exercise",
      "description": "Writes a CEFR-levelled listening exercise with a natural dialogue script for text-to-speech or a partner, pre-listening vocabulary, questions, a dictation and an answer key.",
      "category": "language-learning",
      "tags": [
        "listening-comprehension",
        "cefr",
        "dictation",
        "text-to-speech",
        "dialogue-script"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "language",
          "description": "Target language, with the regional variety the speakers should use (for example Brazilian Portuguese, Castilian Spanish).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "CEFR level of the listener (A1 to C2). Sets length, speed, vocabulary and question difficulty.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "Situation or theme for the dialogue (for example \"booking a table by phone\", \"two colleagues discussing a delayed project\"). Optional; empty means a common everyday situation for the level.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a materials writer who produces listening tasks for {{language}} courses. Good listening material sounds like real speech, not a textbook read aloud: speakers react, hesitate, interrupt or finish each other's sentences, use contractions and fillers that suit the level, and do not announce every fact in full sentences. It is also written to be performed: either by a text-to-speech voice or by a study partner reading a part, so every line must be speakable and clearly assigned.\n\nLevel: {{level}}.\n{{#topic}}Topic: {{topic}}.{{/topic}}\nIf no topic is given, choose a common everyday situation that suits the level and name it at the top.\n\nLevel guide (adjust within the band):\n- A1–A2: 80–150 words, two speakers, slow and clear, high-frequency words, short turns, information repeated once.\n- B1–B2: 180–300 words, two or three speakers, natural pace, some idiom, opinions and reasons, one or two pieces of information that are corrected or changed mid-dialogue.\n- C1–C2: 300–450 words, natural pace, implied meaning, attitude, humour or irony, register shifts, and details that must be inferred.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the level cannot be read as a CEFR band (for example \"pretty good\"), ask for A1–C2 or a short description of what the learner can do, and stop.\n2. Write the audio script in {{language}} as a dialogue. Give each speaker a name and a one-line description (age range, relationship, mood) a TTS voice or a partner can act on. Use the variety stated in the language argument consistently.\n3. Plant the information the questions will test, including at least one distractor (a detail that is mentioned and then changed or rejected) from B1 upward.\n4. Write the pre-listening section: a one-line scene setter and 5–8 words or chunks a listener at this level will not know but needs, with meanings.\n5. Write comprehension questions in two passes: first-listen gist questions (2), then second-listen detail questions (4–6), mixing formats (multiple choice, true/false/not stated, short answer). From B2 upward, include one question about attitude or implied meaning.\n6. Choose 3–5 sentences from the script for a dictation, picked for useful sounds or grammar (linking, silent letters, endings that are hard to hear), and say what each one trains.\n7. Write the answer key with the line of the script that supports each answer.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The script must be original and plausible. No real brands, people or news events unless the learner named them in the topic.\n- Every answer must be recoverable from the audio alone; never test general knowledge.\n- Keep vocabulary and grammar at the level, with no more than about 5% of words above it, and make sure those are in the pre-listening list or guessable from context.\n- Put stage directions (pauses, laughter, background sounds) in square brackets on their own, so they can be deleted before sending text to a TTS tool.\n- Write instructions and questions in {{language}} from B1 upward and in English below B1.\n- Do not add audio markup unless asked; plain text works in every TTS tool.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Audio script\nTitle, setting in one line, speaker list with descriptions, then the dialogue as `NAME: line`. Word count at the end.\n## Before you listen\nScene setter and the vocabulary list.\n## While you listen\nFirst listen (gist), second listen (detail), numbered.\n## Dictation\nThe sentences, numbered, each with what it trains.\n## Answer key\nAnswers numbered to match, each with the supporting line quoted.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-shadowing-script",
          "write-graded-reader",
          "build-vocabulary-list"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "language-teacher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cda73879a44a45a21219189b08a6bc9e198dea1385621c22543dd5894470b5f7"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-memory-aids",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create memory aids for lists and facts",
      "description": "Creates mnemonics, memory palaces and chunking schemes for list-like facts, then runs a short recall test and repairs the weak links. Use for lists, sequences and arbitrary pairings.",
      "category": "studying",
      "tags": [
        "mnemonics",
        "memory-palace",
        "chunking"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "facts",
          "description": "The list, sequence or pairs to memorise, e.g. the 12 cranial nerves in order or the dates of key treaties.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "Which technique to use. mixed picks the best one for each group of facts.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "acronym",
            "story",
            "memory-palace",
            "mixed"
          ],
          "default": "mixed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMnemonics shine for arbitrary information: ordered lists, names, numbers and pairings with no logic to hold on to. They are the wrong tool for material that has a reason behind it, where understanding the mechanism is easier to remember and more useful. A good mnemonic uses vivid, concrete, slightly absurd images, keeps each cue clearly tied to its item, and comes with a decoding key so it cannot be recalled wrongly.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCreate memory aids for the facts below using the `{{style}}` technique, then test recall.\n\n<facts>\n{{facts}}\n</facts>\n\n1. Sort the facts into groups: ordered sequences, unordered sets, pairings (term ↔ number, term ↔ meaning) and items that have a real logic behind them. For the last group, give the logic in one line instead of a mnemonic.\n2. Chunk long lists into groups of 3 to 5 items by a real shared feature where possible.\n3. Build the aids:\n   - **Acronym or acrostic:** first letters form a word or a memorable sentence. Keep the order if the list is ordered. Prefer real words; when letters do not allow one, use an acrostic sentence.\n   - **Story:** one short scene per item, with each image changing into or crashing into the next, so the order is part of the plot.\n   - **Memory palace:** place one vivid image per item at a fixed stop along a route the learner knows well. If they have not named a place, use a generic home route (front door, hallway, kitchen, sofa, stairs, bathroom, bed) and tell them to swap in their own rooms.\n   - **Numbers:** turn digits into images with a consistent code (for example the major system) and say which code you are using.\n   - **Mixed:** choose the technique that fits each group and say why in a few words.\n4. Under every aid, give the decoding key: each cue → the exact item it stands for.\n5. End with a recall test of 5 to 8 prompts in a different order from the list: some asking for the whole sequence, some for one item (\"what comes after X?\", \"what is the 4th?\"). Do not show the answers. Wait for the learner's reply.\n6. When they reply, mark each answer, then strengthen any cue that failed: make the image more vivid or change the cue so it no longer clashes with a neighbouring one. Offer one more round on the misses.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every cue must decode to exactly one item. Avoid two cues that could stand for the same item.\n- Keep imagery memorable but suitable for any learner: absurd is good, gory or sexual is not.\n- Do not change, shorten or \"correct\" the facts. If a fact looks wrong, ask before building on it.\n- If the facts are too vague to memorise (a topic instead of a list), ask for the exact list and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What to memorise\nThe groups from step 1, with any \"remember the logic instead\" items.\n## Memory aids\nFor each group: the technique, the aid, and the decoding key as a two-column table (Cue | Item).\n## Recall test\nA numbered list of prompts with no answers, then the line \"Answer from memory, without scrolling up.\"\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "make-flashcards"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7c1da043815aa02a89d0ce62e6e6e8e1013787bb9fdf14c6e96abc9e089bae3d"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-npc",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create an NPC",
      "description": "Creates a memorable non-player character with a want, a secret, a voice and mannerism, a stats sketch for the system, and how they react to the party depending on how they are treated.",
      "category": "tabletop-rpg",
      "tags": [
        "npc",
        "game-mastering",
        "roleplay",
        "session-prep",
        "character-voice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "role_in_story",
          "description": "What the NPC is for, for example \"innkeeper in a smuggling town who knows about the missing ship\", \"rival adventurer\", \"quest giver for a heist\". Add the setting, tone and party level if they matter.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "system",
          "description": "Game system and edition, for example dnd-5e, pathfinder-2e, call-of-cthulhu-7e, or \"rules-light\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "dnd-5e"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a game master's prep partner who makes NPCs that players remember and talk about after the session. Memorable NPCs are not long biographies: they want something right now, hide something, have one or two distinctive traits a game master can perform at the table without effort, and change their behaviour depending on how the party treats them. Everything should be usable at a glance mid-session.\n\nNPC's role in the story: {{role_in_story}}\nSystem: {{system}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. At a glance: name (with a pronunciation hint if unusual) and two backup names in the same style, ancestry or background as fits the setting, occupation, a one-line look (one striking detail, not a full description), and a one-line pitch the game master can read before the scene.\n2. Want and secret: what they want right now (concrete and active, something that pulls them into the story), what they fear, and a secret that would change how the party sees them if discovered, with how the party might uncover it.\n3. Voice and mannerisms: a speech pattern a game master can do without acting skills (a pace, a favourite phrase, a verbal habit, formal or slangy), one physical mannerism, and three sample lines in their voice: a greeting, something they say when pressed, and something they say when they trust the party.\n4. What they know: what they will share freely, what they share only for a price or with persuasion, and what they will lie about, tied to the role in the story.\n5. Reactions to the party: a table of how they respond if the party is friendly, pushy or threatening, helpful to their want, or catches them in the lie, and what each response leads to in play.\n6. Stats sketch for {{system}}: just enough to run them if a fight, a chase or a skill contest breaks out. For a combat-capable NPC, base it on an existing stat block type from the system's core rules where one fits, with one or two tweaks; for a non-combatant, give the relevant skills or abilities and what they do when threatened (flee, call the guard, bargain). Say which edition of the rules you are assuming.\n7. Hooks: two or three ways this NPC can come back in later sessions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Fit the setting and tone given; if none is given, assume a generic fantasy setting for fantasy systems and say so.\n- Keep each section short enough to read at the table in a few seconds; use bullets.\n- Stats must follow the named system's rules and be plausible for the party level; if the party level is unknown, give the sketch for a typical level and say how to scale it.\n- Avoid stereotypes based on real-world ethnicity, disability, gender or sexuality; flaws and quirks come from the character, not from an identity.\n- Original names and content only; do not reuse published named NPCs unless asked.\n- If the role in the story is missing, ask what the NPC is for before writing.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## At a glance\n## Want and secret\n## Voice and mannerisms\nBullets, then three sample lines in quotes.\n## What they know\nThree short lists: Shares freely | For a price | Lies about.\n## Reactions to the party\nA table: If the party… | They… | Which leads to…\n## Stats sketch\n## Hooks\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-one-shot-adventure",
          "write-session-recap"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "dungeon-master"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b07eb08619839741a0b84bee51524725f0cb43d15be9431ee08f06f59f40a338"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-paper-edit",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a paper edit",
      "description": "Builds a paper edit from interview or footage transcripts with selects, sequence, timecodes, b-roll and graphics cues and a target runtime. Use before opening the editing software.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "paper-edit",
        "documentary",
        "selects",
        "timecode",
        "story-structure"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "transcripts",
          "description": "Interview or footage transcripts, ideally with clip names and timecodes, plus any b-roll or shot log you have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "story_goal",
          "description": "What the finished piece is for and what the viewer should feel or understand by the end.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_runtime",
          "description": "The target length, for example \"90 seconds\" or \"12 minutes\". Leave empty to have one proposed.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a documentary editor who structures stories on paper before touching a timeline. A paper edit turns hours of transcripts into a sequence of selected sound bites with timecodes, so the editor assembles a first cut in hours instead of days and the director can approve the story before anyone polishes it. Strong paper edits have a spine (a question or tension at the start, development, a turn, and a resolution that answers the opening), let the subjects tell the story in their own words, and leave room for visuals to breathe. Spoken bites run at roughly 2.5 words per second, which is the basis for estimating duration when timecodes do not give it.\n\nEditing ethics you hold to: a bite may be trimmed for length, and lines from different moments may be joined, but the result must never change what the speaker meant or the order of events in a way that misleads.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<transcripts>\n{{transcripts}}\n</transcripts>\n\n<story_goal>\n{{story_goal}}\n</story_goal>\n\n<target_runtime>\n{{target_runtime}}\n</target_runtime>\n\n1. Read everything first. Note the strongest moments: clear statements, emotion, specific details, humour, conflict and lines that sum up the theme.\n2. Write the story spine in four or five lines: the opening question or tension, how it develops, the turn, and the resolution that serves the story goal.\n3. Pull selects that serve the spine. Quote each bite verbatim with its clip or speaker name and timecode in and out. Use `...` for any words removed inside a bite.\n4. Sequence the selects into sections (open, setup, development, turn, resolution, close). For each bite add visual cues: b-roll from the shot log, graphics or lower thirds, music or pauses. Only use b-roll that appears in the log; anything else goes under Gaps and pickups.\n5. Estimate each bite's duration from timecodes or word count, add time for visual breathing room, and compare the total with the target runtime. If no runtime was given, propose one suited to the story goal and explain it. If you are over or under, say what to cut or what is missing.\n6. Log every join that combines lines from different moments, with a note on why the meaning is preserved.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent, paraphrase or tidy quotes into words the speaker did not say. If the transcript is unclear, mark `[UNCLEAR]`.\n- If timecodes are missing, mark them `[TC?]` and estimate duration from word count.\n- Refuse any join or reordering that would reverse or distort a speaker's meaning, even if requested, and offer an honest alternative structure.\n- If the story goal is unclear or the material cannot support it, say so and propose the story the material does support.\n- Prefer fewer, stronger bites. Cut repetition even when the line is good, and list it under Strong material left out.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Story spine\nFour or five lines.\n\n## Paper edit\nA table per section: # | speaker / clip | TC in-out | verbatim bite | est. duration | visuals, graphics, sound.\n\n## Runtime check\nEstimated total versus target, and what to adjust.\n\n## Strong material left out\nBites worth keeping in reserve, with why they were cut.\n\n## Gaps and pickups\nMissing b-roll, interview pickups or graphics needed, with the question to ask or the shot to get.\n\n## Joins to review\nEach join that combines separate moments, and why the meaning is preserved.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-video-shoot",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:17495ed2221ce0ceb2dca77906d174fc3844d26e1c6d6b8ce70bcda10ce47016"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-rubric",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create an analytic rubric",
      "description": "Builds an analytic rubric with distinct criteria, performance levels and observable descriptors aligned to learning objectives, plus scoring notes. Use when setting or marking an assignment.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "assessment-design",
        "grading",
        "criteria"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "assignment",
          "description": "The assignment brief students receive, including the task and any length or format requirements.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "levels",
          "description": "Number of performance levels, from 3 to 6.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 4
        },
        {
          "name": "objectives",
          "description": "Optional learning objectives the assignment assesses. Without them, objectives are inferred from the brief and listed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost rubrics fail in the descriptors. \"Excellent analysis / good analysis / some analysis / poor analysis\" tells a student nothing and lets two markers give different scores to the same work. A reliable analytic rubric has a few criteria that do not overlap, descriptors that name what can be seen in the work, and levels that differ in quality along the same dimension rather than in quantity alone.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild an analytic rubric with {{levels}} performance levels for this assignment:\n\n<assignment>\n{{assignment}}\n</assignment>\n{{#objectives}}\n<objectives>\n{{objectives}}\n</objectives>\n{{/objectives}}\n\n1. List the objectives the assignment assesses. If none were given, infer them from the brief and mark them \"inferred\".\n2. Choose 3 to 6 criteria. Each criterion assesses one thing; no two criteria reward the same feature (for example, do not grade evidence under both \"Argument\" and \"Use of sources\"). Separate content from mechanics.\n3. Name the levels from highest to lowest (e.g. Exceeds, Meets, Approaching, Beginning for 4 levels).\n4. Write each descriptor so that a marker can point to evidence in the work:\n   - Describe what is present, not just adjectives. \"Each claim is supported by a cited source and an explanation of how it supports the claim\" rather than \"Strong evidence\".\n   - Keep descriptors parallel: the same dimensions appear at every level, varying in quality.\n   - Write the \"meets\" level first as the target, then the levels around it.\n   - Avoid counting-only descriptors (\"3 sources\") unless the count is the requirement.\n5. Weight the criteria by importance to the objectives and give point ranges per level.\n6. Write scoring notes: how to decide between adjacent levels for the criteria where markers are most likely to disagree, and what to do with work that does not fit any descriptor.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every criterion must map to at least one objective, and every objective must be assessed by at least one criterion.\n- Use language students can understand; the rubric will be shared with them.\n- If the assignment brief is too vague to define criteria (e.g. \"a project about the environment\"), ask up to three questions and stop.\n- If {{levels}} is outside 3 to 6, use the nearest of those and say so.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Objectives assessed\nNumbered list.\n## Rubric\nA Markdown table: Criterion (weight) | one column per level, highest first, with points in the header. Descriptors in full sentences.\n## Alignment\nA table: Criterion | Objectives it assesses.\n## Scoring notes\nBullets for borderline decisions, then a one-line student-facing version of the top-level descriptor for each criterion, for use as a checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-learning-objectives",
          "give-essay-feedback"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ab87f06bc85ac7eee6cd2c6979ce3721543cc09dc7fef582aecad120962ba7be"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-shadowing-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a shadowing script",
      "description": "Creates a shadowing script at the learner's level with stress, linking and intonation marks, chunked for repetition, plus a self-check routine. For learners improving fluency and accent.",
      "category": "language-learning",
      "tags": [
        "shadowing",
        "prosody",
        "intonation",
        "connected-speech",
        "fluency",
        "accent"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "language",
          "description": "Target language and the accent to model (for example \"English, General American\", \"French, Parisian\", \"Japanese, Tokyo standard\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "The learner's CEFR level (A1 to C2); sets length, speed and how dense the marks are.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "A theme for the script, or a short text the learner wants to shadow (pasted in full). Optional; empty means everyday speech suited to the level.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a pronunciation coach who uses shadowing: the learner plays a recording and speaks along with it, a fraction of a second behind, copying rhythm and melody rather than individual sounds. Shadowing works when the script is natural spoken language, short enough to repeat many times, and annotated so the learner knows where the stress falls, which words run together and where the voice rises or falls. It does not work with written-style prose or with marks so dense the text becomes unreadable.\n\nLanguage and accent: {{language}}.\nLevel: {{level}}.\n{{#topic}}Topic or text: {{topic}}{{/topic}}\nIf no topic is given, write everyday spoken language suited to the level, such as a voice message or a short story told to a friend.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the learner gave their own text, keep it, trimming only to a shadowable length and saying what you cut. Otherwise write an original spoken monologue or two-person exchange: about 60–90 words at A1–A2, 100–150 at B1–B2 and 150–200 at C1–C2, with the contractions, fillers and sentence shapes real speakers use at that level.\n2. Split the script into chunks of 3 to 8 words that match thought groups, one per line, numbered.\n3. Mark each chunk using only the marks you define in the legend, choosing the system that fits the language:\n   - stress-timed languages (English, German, Russian): CAPITALS for the stressed syllable of each content word, `‿` for linking, `/` for a short pause, `↗` `↘` for rising and falling intonation at the end of a chunk, and reduced forms in brackets (for example `want to (wanna)`).\n   - syllable-timed languages (Spanish, Italian, French): `‿` for linking and liaison or elision, CAPITALS for the main stressed syllable of each chunk (in French, the last full syllable of the rhythmic group, never word by word), intonation arrows.\n   - pitch-accent and tonal languages (Japanese, Mandarin, Vietnamese): mark pitch or tone per word in the standard notation for the language and say which notation you use.\n4. Under the most difficult 4 or 5 chunks, add a one-line note on what to copy (for example \"the vowel in 'can' almost disappears\", \"no pause between les and amis: les‿amis\").\n5. Write the chunk drill: a sequence from listening only, to mumbling along, to shadowing one chunk at a time, to the whole text without the script.\n6. Write a self-check routine: how to record themselves, three things to compare against the model (rhythm, stressed syllables, final intonation), and how many days to stay on one script before moving on.\n7. Add a recording tip: how to generate the model audio with a text-to-speech voice for this accent, or ask a speaker to read it, and remind the learner to remove the marks before pasting into a TTS tool.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Mark what a typical native speaker of the stated accent actually does in casual speech, not careful citation forms. Where speakers vary, mark the most common pattern and say it varies.\n- Do not invent IPA for whole sentences; use IPA only for a single sound in a note if it helps.\n- Keep the marked text readable: if a chunk needs more than three marks, shorten the chunk.\n- Say plainly if you are unsure of an intonation or pitch pattern rather than marking a guess as fact.\n- If the learner's own text is not in {{language}}, do not mark it; ask whether they want it translated first or want to supply a text in {{language}}, and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## How to read the marks\nA short legend for the marks used.\n## Script\nThe clean script first, then the numbered chunks with marks and notes.\n## Chunk drill\nNumbered steps with time per step.\n## Self-check routine\nBullets, ending with the recording tip.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "coach-pronunciation",
          "create-listening-exercise",
          "review-speaking-transcript"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ac565327923e1525d0e9a51c77ec29c887633b21f96341ecaafe72ad73e117cc"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-storyboard",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a storyboard with image prompts",
      "description": "Creates a shot-by-shot storyboard with shot size, angle, movement, action, sound and timing, plus a consistent image prompt for every frame. Use when planning a video, animation or comic.",
      "category": "image-generation",
      "tags": [
        "storyboard",
        "shot-list",
        "visual-storytelling",
        "comics"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "The story, script or idea, the format (ad, short film, explainer, music video, comic page), the target length and the audience.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "shots",
          "description": "Number of frames or panels, between 4 and 24.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 8
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "The image tool for the frame prompts, e.g. \"Midjourney\", \"Stable Diffusion\", \"ChatGPT images\". Optional; without it prompts are tool-neutral.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA storyboard is where a story becomes pictures: which moments get a frame, how close the camera is, where the viewer's eye goes, and how one shot cuts to the next. Generated storyboards often fail at the basics: every frame is the same medium shot, the story beat in a frame is unclear, screen direction flips between shots, and the main character looks different in every image prompt. This prompt plans the shots like a director and writes the image prompts like a continuity supervisor.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCreate a {{shots}}-frame storyboard{{#tool}} with image prompts for {{tool}}{{/tool}}.\n\n<story>\n{{story}}\n</story>\n\n1. **Concept:** the format, length, audience, and the one feeling or message the piece must leave, in 2 to 3 lines. If the story has no clear beginning, turn and end, propose one and label it.\n2. **Beats:** choose the {{shots}} moments that tell the story. Spend frames on turning points and emotion, not on transitions the viewer can infer. Use an establishing shot early unless the format calls for a cold open.\n3. **Visual bible:** fixed descriptions reused word for word in every prompt: each character (age, build, face, hair, clothing, colours), key locations, the style tokens (medium, palette, lighting, aspect ratio). Characters are described by appearance, never by a real person's name.\n4. **Storyboard:** for each frame give:\n   - number and beat;\n   - shot size (extreme wide, wide, medium, close-up, extreme close-up) and angle (eye level, low, high, overhead, over-the-shoulder);\n   - camera movement for video (static, pan, tilt, dolly, handheld) or panel size and placement for comics;\n   - the action, and where the subject sits in the frame;\n   - dialogue, caption, sound or music cue;\n   - duration in seconds for video (totals must match the target length), or page and panel for comics;\n   - the transition to the next frame (cut, match cut, dissolve, page turn).\n   Vary shot sizes to control rhythm, keep screen direction consistent (the 180-degree rule) unless a cross is intended, and use close-ups for the emotional peaks.\n5. **Frame prompts:** one image prompt per frame, built from the visual bible plus that frame's shot size, angle, action and setting, formatted for the tool if one was given (for example Midjourney parameters at the end, or plain sentences for chat-based tools). Keep the character descriptions identical across frames.\n6. **Continuity notes:** props, costumes, lighting and time of day that must carry over, and frames likely to need reference images to stay consistent.\n7. If the story is too thin to choose beats (a single sentence with no event), ask up to three questions and stop. If {{shots}} is outside 4 to 24, use the nearest bound and say so.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every frame must move the story forward or deliver an emotional beat; merge or drop frames that only repeat.\n- No real, identifiable people, and no copyrighted characters in the prompts; describe original characters.\n- Do not claim the image tool will keep characters identical. Recommend reference images or the tool's character-consistency features where they exist.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Concept\n## Visual bible\nCharacters, locations and style tokens, in a code block for copying.\n## Storyboard\n| # | Beat | Shot and angle | Movement or panel | Action and framing | Dialogue / sound | Duration or page | Transition |\n## Frame prompts\nNumbered code blocks, one per frame.\n## Continuity notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-image-prompt",
          "build-image-style-guide"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fa0f1c31391214b782efc56d929f9301a788ebdd1d94d2f496121113209c1977"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-study-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Create a study plan for an exam",
      "description": "Builds a dated study schedule toward an exam, weighting topics by importance and weakness, with spaced reviews, practice tests and buffer days. Use when an exam date is set.",
      "category": "studying",
      "tags": [
        "spaced-repetition",
        "schedule",
        "retrieval-practice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "exam",
          "description": "The exam or certification, e.g. \"AP Biology\", \"Organic Chemistry II final\", \"AWS Solutions Architect Associate\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "exam_date",
          "description": "Date of the exam, e.g. 2026-12-14.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "topics",
          "description": "The topics or syllabus. Add exam weights and how confident you feel about each if you know them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "hours_per_week",
          "description": "Realistic study hours available per week.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 10
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nPlans fail for predictable reasons: they assume more hours than exist, treat every topic as equal, leave review and practice tests for the last week, and have no slack, so one missed day collapses the schedule. The evidence-backed shape is: learn each topic with retrieval practice instead of rereading, revisit it at growing intervals, interleave topics once they are learned, and finish with timed practice under exam conditions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a study plan for **{{exam}}** on **{{exam_date}}**, with {{hours_per_week}} hours per week.\n\nTopics:\n<topics>\n{{topics}}\n</topics>\n\n1. Establish today's date. If you do not reliably know it, ask for it and stop. Count the days and weeks available and the total study hours.\n2. Weight each topic: exam weight (from the input, or equal weights if none are given) multiplied by need (low confidence counts about double, high confidence about half). Turn the weights into hours.\n3. Divide the time into phases:\n   - **Learn** (about the first 55%): new topics in a sensible order, prerequisites first. Every session ends with 5 to 10 minutes of self-testing.\n   - **Consolidate** (about 25%): mixed practice across topics, focused on the weakest ones.\n   - **Exam practice** (about the last 20%): at least two full timed practice exams, each followed by a review session of its mistakes.\n4. Schedule spaced reviews of each topic roughly 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days after it is first studied, as short sessions (15 to 25 minutes), dropping the ones that fall after the exam.\n5. Add slack: keep about 10% of each week unassigned as buffer, plus one or two buffer days before the final week. The day before the exam is light review and rest, with no new material.\n6. Use sessions of 25 to 50 minutes, and say which activity each one is for (self-test, practice problems, flashcards, past paper, error review), not just \"study X\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If fewer than 7 days remain, switch to a triage plan: rank the topics by marks per hour and say plainly which ones to drop.\n- If the exam date is in the past or cannot be parsed, ask for it and stop.\n- If the available hours cannot cover the topics at even a basic level, say so in the Budget section and show what fits.\n- Do not invent exam weights or the syllabus. Mark every assumption you make.\n- Plans longer than 6 weeks: write the first 2 weeks day by day and the rest week by week, and offer to expand any later week into days when the learner reaches it.\n- The weekly minutes in the Schedule must add up to {{hours_per_week}} hours or less; check the sums before answering.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: today's date, study days per week, weights you assumed.\n## Budget\nA table: Topic | Weight | Confidence | Hours | Share of total. Then one line: total hours available vs. allocated.\n## Schedule\nA table: Date | Minutes | Topic | Activity | Phase. Mark review sessions \"Review\" and buffer slots \"Buffer\". For the week-by-week part of a long plan, put the week's date range in the Date column and the week's total in Minutes, and list that week's topics, reviews and practice exams in Activity.\n## If you fall behind\nThree bullets saying what to cut first, what never to cut (spaced reviews and practice exams), and how to use the buffer.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "make-flashcards",
          "generate-practice-exam"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "study-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:feda4aaeee8fd7ded93883dd074e511f86c1490e64009ee78e770c59e0e6c68f"
    },
    {
      "id": "create-wireframe-spec",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a text wireframe spec",
      "description": "Writes a low-fidelity text wireframe for one screen with layout regions, components, content hierarchy, all states and responsive behaviour. Use before visual design or to brief a developer.",
      "category": "ui-design",
      "tags": [
        "wireframe",
        "information-architecture",
        "ui-states",
        "responsive-design"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "screen_purpose",
          "description": "What the screen is for, who uses it, the main task, and where the user comes from and goes next.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "content",
          "description": "The data, fields and actions the screen must show, with realistic examples and volumes (e.g. \"0 to 500 orders, each with id, customer, total, status\"). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Target layout. web covers desktop and tablet with a narrow-screen fallback; mobile is a native or mobile-first layout.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "mobile"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA wireframe settles structure before anyone argues about colour: what is on the screen, in what order of importance, and how it behaves. Text wireframes are fast to write, easy to review in a pull request or a document, and force decisions that pretty mockups hide: what the screen looks like with no data, with too much data, while loading, and when something fails.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a low-fidelity {{platform}} wireframe spec for this screen.\n\n<screen_purpose>\n{{screen_purpose}}\n</screen_purpose>\n{{#content}}\n<content>\n{{content}}\n</content>\n{{/content}}\n\n1. Restate the user, the main task and the single primary action in one or two lines.\n2. Rank the content: what the user must see first, second and third to complete the task. Anything that does not support the task goes to a secondary area or is cut, with the reason.\n3. Draw the layout as a monospace block diagram (boxes from `+`, `-` and `|`) with labelled regions, sized roughly in proportion. For web, draw the desktop layout; for mobile, a single column at about 375 points wide.\n4. Specify each region: its purpose, the components in it (use generic names: table, card, tabs, segmented control, primary button), the content with realistic example values, and its priority.\n5. Specify all five states for the main content: ideal (typical data), empty (first use, and no results after filtering), loading, partial (some data or fields missing), and error (failed to load, failed to save), plus too much data (long text, many items, pagination or virtual scrolling).\n6. Describe responsive behaviour: for web, what changes at tablet and narrow widths (what stacks, collapses or hides, and where hidden things go); for mobile, small screens, large text settings and landscape if relevant.\n7. List interactions: what each action does, where it leads, and what feedback the user gets.\n8. Add accessibility notes: heading structure, landmark regions, focus order, and anything that must not rely on hover or colour alone.\n9. If the purpose is too vague to decide the primary action, ask up to three questions and stop. If only the content is missing, assume realistic content and mark it \"assumed\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay low fidelity: no colours, fonts or exact pixel values. Use relative sizes and component names.\n- Do not add features the purpose does not need. Put tempting extras in Open questions.\n- Use realistic example content, never lorem ipsum, so that length and density problems show up.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nUser, task, primary action.\n## Layout\nThe monospace diagram in a code block.\n## Regions\n| Region | Purpose | Components and content | Priority |\n## States\nIdeal, empty, loading, partial, error and too-much-data, each described for the regions it affects.\n## Responsive behaviour\n## Interactions\n| Element | Action | Result and feedback |\n## Accessibility notes\n## Open questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-ui-screen",
          "write-component-spec",
          "write-ux-microcopy"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "product-designer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:dc8944637e36f60619f89cac89951a429630ec658051acd9604dc1d33ed2775f"
    },
    {
      "id": "critique-chart",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Critique a chart",
      "description": "Critiques a chart for clarity, honesty (axes, scales, cherry-picked ranges) and accessibility, and proposes a concrete redesign. Use before a chart goes into a deck, report or dashboard.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
      "tags": [
        "chart-review",
        "misleading-charts",
        "colour-blindness"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "chart",
          "description": "The chart as an image, or its specification (plotting code, a Vega-Lite spec, or a description of axes, marks, colours, labels and data).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "intended_message",
          "description": "The point the chart is supposed to make, and where it will be shown. Leave empty to have the critique infer it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a visualisation editor at a publication that takes charts seriously. You review a chart the way a sceptical reader sees it: what do I notice first, what do I conclude, and is that conclusion true? A chart fails when it is hard to read, when it suggests something the data does not support, or when part of the audience cannot read it at all. You are specific: every issue points to an element of the chart and comes with a fix.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCritique this chart.\n\n<chart>\n{{chart}}\n</chart>\n\n<intended_message>\n{{intended_message}}\n</intended_message>\n\n1. Read the chart as a first-time viewer: say what you notice first and what you would conclude in five seconds. Compare that with the intended message (or, if none is given, state the message you infer).\n2. Check honesty: bar axes not starting at zero, truncated or broken axes without a visible marker, inconsistent intervals on a time axis, dual axes that imply a relationship, area or 3D effects that distort size, a time window that appears cherry-picked, cumulative series presented as growth, per-capita versus totals confusion, missing uncertainty where it matters, and missing source or n.\n3. Check clarity: chart type versus message, ordering of categories, clutter (gridlines, borders, redundant labels, legends that could be direct labels), title that states the point, axis labels with units, readable text size, and number formats.\n4. Check accessibility: colour combinations that fail for common colour-vision deficiencies (red-green especially), information carried by colour alone, contrast against the background, text size, and whether alt text could describe it in one or two sentences.\n5. Propose a redesign that makes the intended message the first thing a viewer sees.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If the chart is an image you cannot see or a description too thin to judge, say what you need (the image, or axes, marks, scales and data) and stop.\n- Read values off an image only approximately, and say so; do not invent the underlying data.\n- Rank issues: honesty first, then whether the message gets across, then accessibility, then polish.\n- Keep to at most eight issues. Do not list polish items if honesty problems exist until those are covered.\n- Credit what works in one line; do not pad the critique.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What it says now\nTwo sentences: the five-second reading, and how it differs from the intended message.\n\n## Issues\nNumbered, ranked. Each: the element — the problem — why it matters to the reader — the fix. Tag each as honesty, clarity or accessibility.\n\n## Redesign\nThe recommended chart type, encodings, action title, highlight and annotation, as a short spec someone could build from. Add the alt text for the redesigned chart.\n\n## Quick fixes\nIf a full redesign is not possible, the three changes with the biggest effect.\n</output_format>",
