    {
      "id": "design-classroom-activity",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design an active-learning activity",
      "description": "Designs an active-learning activity such as a jigsaw or gallery walk, with timing, grouping, materials, a teacher script and accountability. Use when lecture alone will not reach an objective.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "active-learning",
        "cooperative-learning",
        "jigsaw",
        "gallery-walk"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "objective",
          "description": "What students should be able to do by the end, plus the content, e.g. \"compare the causes of the American and French revolutions\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "class_size",
          "description": "Number of students.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 25
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Time available for the activity.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 30
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nActive learning works when the structure fits the thinking the objective requires and every student has to do that thinking. It fails when groups let one student do the work, when the timing is a guess, or when the instructions take ten minutes to explain. Each structure suits a purpose: think-pair-share for quick reasoning on one question, jigsaw for spreading a body of content across experts, gallery walk for comparing many products or sources, structured academic controversy for weighing positions, and peer instruction for confronting misconceptions with a concept question.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign an activity for {{class_size}} students in {{duration_minutes}} minutes toward this objective:\n\n<objective>\n{{objective}}\n</objective>\n\n1. Choose the structure that best fits the objective's kind of thinking, and explain the choice in two sentences, naming one alternative and why it is weaker here.\n2. Work out the grouping arithmetic for exactly {{class_size}} students: group size, number of groups, and what to do with the remainder. For a jigsaw, the expert and home group sizes must both work.\n3. Write a minute-by-minute run sheet whose times add up to exactly {{duration_minutes}} minutes, including transitions and a closing synthesis.\n4. Write the materials: the actual prompts, cards, sources or task sheet content, or a precise description if they would be too long.\n5. Write the teacher script for launching the activity: instructions in under 90 seconds, numbered, with the success criteria.\n6. Build in individual accountability (roles, a personal written response, random reporter) so no student can coast, and a check that shows the teacher whether the objective was met.\n7. Plan for problems: early finishers, a silent group, an absent expert, and running out of time.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If {{duration_minutes}} is too short for the best structure, choose a simpler one and say why.\n- Use materials a normal classroom has unless the objective says otherwise.\n- If the objective is too vague to design for (\"learn about plants\"), restate it as a measurable objective, say you did, and design for that.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Choice of structure\nTwo or three sentences.\n## Setup\nGroup size and count, room layout, materials list.\n## Run sheet\nA table: Minutes | Phase | Students do | Teacher does. Times sum to {{duration_minutes}}.\n## Teacher script\nNumbered launch instructions, ready to read aloud.\n## Accountability and check\nBullets.\n## If things go wrong\nProblem → response.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-lesson-plan",
          "generate-discussion-questions"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7724f686e01dd56cf464d6d741081ad732fb57569e391a7d2b0ec55456bfd22b"
    },
    {
      "id": "design-classroom-management-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design a classroom management plan",
      "description": "Designs a classroom management plan with expectations, routines, positive reinforcement, a consistent response ladder and family communication for the grade. For new teachers and class resets.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "classroom-management",
        "behaviour",
        "routines",
        "pbis",
        "restorative-practice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "grade",
          "description": "Grade or age and setting, e.g. \"Grade 2, self-contained classroom\", \"Year 9 science, 5 different classes\", \"adult ESL evening class\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "challenges",
          "description": "Optional current issues, described as observable behaviour, e.g. \"calling out during instruction, slow transitions after lunch, 4 students regularly off task in group work\".",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "school_policy",
          "description": "Optional school behaviour policy, consequence system or required approaches the plan must fit.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost classroom behaviour problems are prevented, not punished away. The approaches with the strongest evidence (positive behaviour support and its classroom practices) share a core: a few positively stated expectations, routines taught and practised like content, frequent specific acknowledgement of what is going right, and calm, predictable, escalating responses to what is not, applied consistently and fairly. Relationships and repair matter as much as rules. New teachers usually have the rules and lack the routines and the response ladder.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign a classroom management plan for {{grade}}.\n{{#challenges}}\n<challenges>\n{{challenges}}\n</challenges>\n{{/challenges}}\n{{#school_policy}}\n<school_policy>\n{{school_policy}}\n</school_policy>\n{{/school_policy}}\n\n1. **Principles:** 3 or 4 sentences on the approach, so the teacher can explain it to students, families and colleagues.\n2. **Expectations:** 3 to 5 positively stated expectations suited to the age (\"Be safe, be kind, be ready to learn\" for younger students; more specific for older ones), each with what it looks like in 2 or 3 key settings (whole-class teaching, group work, transitions).\n3. **Routines:** the routines this class needs, at least entry, getting attention, transitions, asking for help, materials and dismissal{{#challenges}}, plus one for each challenge described{{/challenges}}. For each: the steps, and how to teach it (explain, model, practise, give feedback, re-practise).\n4. **Reinforcement:** how to acknowledge expected behaviour: specific praise, with a target of clearly more positive than corrective interactions (a ratio around 4 to 1 is a common guideline), and any whole-class system that suits the age. Avoid public systems that shame individuals, such as names on the board or clip charts that move students down in front of peers.\n5. **Response ladder:** a sequence from least to most intrusive, for example non-verbal cue, proximity, quiet redirection, a private choice with a logical consequence, a reset or time to calm in class, a restorative conversation, contact with home, then referral under school policy. For each step: what the teacher says or does, and when to move up. Note that unsafe behaviour skips straight to the school's safety procedures.\n6. **Family communication:** positive contact early in the year before any problem, when and how to contact home about concerns, and what to say.\n7. **Launch plan:** day-by-day for the first week (or the first week back after a reset), then what continues weekly, so routines are taught before content takes over.\n8. **Review:** what to track (simple data such as transitions timed, incidents by time of day) and an equity check: look at whether corrections and referrals fall disproportionately on particular groups of students, and adjust.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Fit every part to the school policy when given; if the plan's suggestions conflict with it, follow the policy and note the conflict.\n- Match the age: language, rewards and routines that would feel babyish to teenagers, or too abstract for six-year-olds, are a failure.\n- Address challenges as behaviour to teach and change, not as traits of students; no diagnoses.\n- Keep it runnable by one teacher; prefer a few routines done consistently over many.\n- If the setting is unclear (age, single class or many), state your assumption.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nUse the section headings from the output contract. Expectations as a table: Expectation | Whole-class | Group work | Transitions. Routines as numbered steps. Response ladder as a table: Step | Teacher action | Words to use | Move up when. Launch plan as a short day-by-day list.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-parent-email",
          "plan-substitute-lesson"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "instructional-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e176d9ad1a3e62af94cf81449f47f0018a76cd8d061fca265c8ae84403a56ef1"
    },
    {
      "id": "design-course-outline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design a course outline",
      "description": "Designs a course with backward design, moving from outcomes to assessments to a sequenced, paced module plan with an alignment matrix. Use when building a new course or workshop series.",
      "category": "course-design",
      "tags": [
        "backward-design",
        "syllabus",
        "constructive-alignment"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "subject",
          "description": "What the course teaches, e.g. \"introductory data visualisation with Python\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who it is for and what they already know, e.g. \"second-year biology undergraduates, no coding experience\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "Duration and rhythm, e.g. \"8 weeks\", \"12 weeks, two 90-minute sessions a week\", \"a 3-day workshop\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "8 weeks"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nCourses designed topic-first end up as a list of things to cover, with assessments bolted on at the end that test whatever was easiest to test. Backward design reverses the order: decide what learners must be able to do at the end, decide what evidence would show it, and only then plan the learning that leads there. Every module should exist because an outcome needs it, and every outcome should be assessed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign a course on **{{subject}}** for **{{audience}}**, lasting **{{length}}**.\n\n1. Outcomes: write 4 to 6 course-level outcomes. Each starts with an observable verb, describes what the learner can do after the course, and is achievable in {{length}} for this audience. Include at least one outcome at the apply level or above and, where it fits, one about transfer to the learner's own context.\n2. Evidence: plan the assessments.\n   - One or two summative assessments that require performing the outcomes, preferably an authentic task (a project, a case, a portfolio, a performance), not only a test.\n   - Formative checks in every module, low-stakes, with feedback.\n   - Weightings that reflect the importance of each outcome.\n3. Learning plan: break the course into modules or weeks that fit {{length}}.\n   - Order them by prerequisites: what must be understood before what. Front-load the foundations, and revisit key ideas later (spiral).\n   - For each module: a title, the outcomes it serves, key concepts, learning activities, the formative check, and the estimated learner hours (in session and independent).\n   - Leave slack: a catch-up or consolidation point about two-thirds through, and time to work on the summative task.\n4. Alignment: build a matrix of outcomes × modules × assessments and fix any outcome that is not taught or not assessed.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep the workload realistic for the audience. State the assumed weekly hours; if {{length}} does not give the session pattern, assume one and say so.\n- Do not recommend specific textbooks, courses or URLs unless you are confident they exist; describe the resource type instead (\"an introductory open textbook chapter on…\").\n- If the subject is too broad for {{length}} (\"all of physics in 4 weeks\"), narrow the scope, say what you cut, and why.\n- Make no claims about accreditation or institutional requirements; flag them as things to check.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Course summary\nThree or four sentences: who, what, how long, assumed weekly hours, and the summative task.\n## Outcomes\nNumbered O1, O2…\n## Assessment plan\nA table: Assessment | Type (summative / formative) | Outcomes | Weight | When.\n## Module plan\nA table: Week or module | Title | Outcomes | Key concepts | Activities | Formative check | Hours.\n## Alignment matrix\nA table with outcomes as rows and modules and assessments as columns, marked with ✓.\n## Assumptions and open questions\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-learning-objectives",
          "create-rubric"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "course-design-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4df7e34433d10dc77818f79ed2801d43e9668e792e2f11990ef744a706bdc1fc"
    },
    {
      "id": "design-daily-routine",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design a daily routine",
      "description": "Designs morning and evening routines around your goals, energy and fixed constraints, starting small, with a bad-day minimum version and a plan to grow it. Use when you want more structure.",
      "category": "habits",
      "tags": [
        "morning-routine",
        "evening-routine",
        "habit-stacking",
        "minimum-viable-routine"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "What you want your days to do for you, for example \"write every day, feel less rushed in the morning, sleep by 23:00, read more\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Optional - fixed things to work around - wake and work times, commute, kids, pets, shifts, shared bathroom, when you have energy, what has failed before.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRoutines fail when they are copied from someone else's life, start too big, or have no version for bad days, so one missed morning ends the whole thing. A routine that lasts is built from the person's goals and real constraints, anchors new behaviours to things they already do, starts with far less than they think they can manage, and has a minimum version that still counts.\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{#constraints}}\n<constraints_given>\n{{constraints}}\n</constraints_given>\n{{/constraints}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If you cannot tell roughly when the person wakes, starts work or school, and goes to bed, either from clock times or from a shift pattern, ask for the missing ones in one short question and stop; timing cannot be guessed. For shift work or irregular days, anchor the routines to waking and to the start of the shift rather than to clock times, and give a version for each kind of day.\n2. Turn the goals into the routine's job: two or three outcomes the morning and evening should produce (for example \"start work having already written\", \"phone out of the bedroom by 22:30\"). Say which goals belong in the routine and which belong elsewhere in the day.\n3. Design a morning routine and an evening routine. For each:\n   - a clear start trigger tied to something that already happens (alarm, kettle, kids leave, laptop closes);\n   - three to five steps in order, each with a duration, totalling what fits the constraints with at least 10 minutes of slack;\n   - the steps that serve the goals placed where energy suits them;\n   - preparation the evening does for the morning (clothes, bag, first task chosen).\n4. Write a bad-day version of each: two or three steps, under 10 minutes total, that still count as keeping the routine.\n5. First two weeks: which one or two steps to start with (not the whole routine), and a simple tick-box way to track them.\n6. How to grow it: when and in what order to add the remaining steps, and a rule for missed days (never miss twice; do the bad-day version instead).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Fit the person's real constraints. Never assume a 5 a.m. start, a quiet house or free time they did not mention.\n- No generic filler steps (affirmations, cold showers, journaling) unless they serve a stated goal.\n- Do not give medical, sleep-disorder or diet advice. If they mention serious sleep problems or exhaustion, suggest raising it with a doctor.\n- Keep total routine time modest: a morning routine under 45 minutes and an evening one under 30 unless they ask for more.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What the routine is for\nTwo or three outcomes, plus goals that belong elsewhere.\n## Morning routine\nTrigger, then a numbered list: step (minutes). Total time.\n## Evening routine\nSame format.\n## Bad-day version\nMorning and evening, two or three steps each.\n## First two weeks\nWhat to start with and how to track it.\n## How to grow it\nOrder of additions with rough timing, plus the missed-day rule.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-habit-plan",
          "run-energy-audit",
          "plan-my-week"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "productivity-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4926d9a4f870c1552d4214ed55189c77a0f356e5ae92f0ff37ab07ca6b7126e0"
    },
    {
      "id": "design-dark-mode",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design a dark theme",
      "description": "Designs a dark theme from an existing light palette, covering surface elevation, semantic colour mapping, contrast checks, images, charts and token changes. Use when a design system adds dark mode.",
      "category": "design-systems",
      "tags": [
        "dark-mode",
        "theming",
