        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nExtracting a module is a refactor: the program must behave the same before and after. The hard parts are choosing a boundary that leaves both sides cohesive, and moving the code without breaking callers, creating import cycles or quietly changing behaviour along the way.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nExtract {{responsibility}} from {{source}} into its own module{{#destination}} at {{destination}}{{/destination}}.\n1. **Check the safety net.** Find the tests that cover the code to move. If coverage is thin, stop and report which behaviours need tests first. Do not refactor untested code silently.\n2. **Draw the boundary.** List the functions, types and state that belong to the responsibility, and everything they use from the rest of the file. Choose the boundary that minimises what crosses it. If the responsibility shares mutable state with the rest of the file, say how you will pass it explicitly.\n3. **Move in small steps**, running the tests after each:\n   1. create the new module and move the code unchanged;\n   2. import it back into the original file, re-exporting what external callers use so they keep working;\n   3. update internal callers to import from the new module;\n   4. remove the re-exports only if every caller is in this repository and has been updated. For a public library API, keep them and mark them deprecated.\n4. Check for import cycles and fix them by moving the shared piece, not by lazy imports.\n5. Run the full test suite, the type checker and the linter.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No behaviour changes: no bug fixes, renames of public symbols, signature changes or \"improvements\" inside moved code. List those under follow-ups instead.\n- Keep the diff reviewable: moved code should appear as a move, not a rewrite.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Boundary\nWhat moved, what stayed, and what crosses the boundary, in a short list.\n## Steps\nThe steps you took, each with its test result.\n## Diff\nThe full diff.\n## Verification\nTest, type-check and lint commands with results.\n## Follow-ups\nImprovements you noticed but did not make, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "add-characterization-tests",
          "split-large-module",
          "plan-large-refactor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9550e282fb5a4fc7b48791058617cdee6c0825a747ec884be0f1097e246d2e98"
    },
    {
      "id": "extract-tables-from-pdf",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Extract tables from a PDF",
      "description": "Extracts tables from PDF or scanned text into clean CSV, Markdown or JSON, keeps values exactly as printed, flags likely OCR errors and validates totals against the source. Use before analysis.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "pdf",
        "data-extraction",
        "ocr",
        "csv"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "source",
          "description": "The text copied or OCR'd from the PDF pages containing the tables, including headers, footnotes and page numbers.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "output_format",
          "description": "Format for the extracted tables.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "csv",
            "markdown-table",
            "json"
          ],
          "default": "csv"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nText copied from PDFs loses table structure: columns run together, multi-line cells split into separate rows, headers span several lines, tables continue across pages with repeated headers, and scanned pages add OCR errors such as O for 0, l for 1 or a dropped decimal point. Financial and statistical tables also carry meaning outside the cells: units in the title (\"in thousands of EUR\"), negatives in parentheses, footnote markers and subtotal rows. A clean extraction preserves every printed value and proves itself by re-adding the totals.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nExtract every table from this source as {{output_format}}:\n<source>\n{{source}}\n</source>\n\n1. Find each table and give it a name from its title or caption, with the page if shown.\n2. Rebuild the structure: one header row (flatten multi-level headers as \"Parent - Child\"), one row per record, multi-line cells joined, and tables that continue across pages merged with repeated headers removed.\n3. Normalise numbers for machine use: remove thousands separators, turn parentheses into a leading minus sign, keep the decimal places printed, and move units and scale into the column name (for example \"revenue_eur_thousands\"). Keep \"-\", \"n/a\" and blank cells as empty values and list them.\n4. Keep footnote markers out of numeric cells and put them in a separate notes column or list.\n5. Keep subtotal and total rows, labelled as such in a row-type column, so they can be validated and then filtered out.\n6. Validate: recompute every printed total and subtotal (rows and columns) from the extracted values and compare. Report each match and each mismatch with the difference.\n7. Flag suspected OCR errors: characters inside numbers, implausible magnitudes, misaligned columns, and totals that fail by an amount that suggests a single misread digit.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never change a printed value to make a total work. Report the mismatch and the likely culprit instead.\n- Never fill an empty or unreadable cell with a guess; leave it empty and list it.\n- Output each table in a separate fenced block. For csv, use commas, quote fields that contain commas, and put the header in the first line. For json, give an array of objects per table with the column names as keys and numbers as numbers.\n- If the source has no recognisable table, or the text is too garbled to rebuild columns reliably, say so and ask for a better copy (for example exported text, a higher-resolution scan or the page image) rather than producing a doubtful table.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Tables found\nFor each table: its name, page, row and column count, then the table in a fenced block.\n## Validation\nA table: table | total checked | printed | recomputed | result (match / mismatch and difference).\n## Issues to check\nBullets: empty cells, suspected OCR errors and structural guesses, each with its location.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "reconcile-datasets",
          "write-dataframe-transformation"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7c17b38eb8fe05759f73eb27c87935f4f0f0201c7a0c7e07cf6507427e0e4fa9"
    },
    {
      "id": "extract-ui-strings",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Extract hard-coded UI strings",
      "description": "Finds hard-coded user-facing strings and moves them into an i18n catalog with meaningful keys and translator comments, without changing behaviour. Use when preparing an app for translation.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "string-extraction",
        "message-catalog",
        "translation-keys"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "files",
          "description": "Files, folders or globs to process.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "i18n_library",
          "description": "i18n library or platform catalog, for example react-i18next, FormatJS, vue-i18n, Android strings.xml, Apple String Catalogs or gettext. Leave empty to detect it.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "key_style",
          "description": "dotted: nested keys such as checkout.payment.submit. flat: one level such as checkout_payment_submit.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "dotted",
            "flat"
          ],
          "default": "dotted"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nString extraction looks mechanical, but most localisation bugs are created here. Concatenated fragments (\"You have \" + n + \" items\") cannot be translated, because word order and plurals differ by language. Keys named after the English text break as soon as the copy changes. The same English word in two contexts (\"Open\" the verb, \"Open\" the status) shares one key and gets one wrong translation. Log messages and analytics event names get extracted and break dashboards. Translators get a bare string with no idea where it appears or how long it may be.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nExtract user-facing strings from {{files}}.\n\ni18n library: {{i18n_library}} (if empty, detect it from dependencies and existing catalogs. If none exists, stop and recommend one suited to the stack in one paragraph).\nKey style: {{key_style}}.\n\n1. Study the existing setup: catalog location and format, how strings are looked up, the key naming already in use, the interpolation, plural and rich-text APIs, and where translator comments go.\n2. Extract only text a user sees or hears: visible text, `aria-label`, `alt`, `title` and `placeholder` attributes, validation and error messages shown to users, notifications, page titles and email or push templates.\n3. Do not extract: log and debug messages, exception messages that never reach the UI, analytics event names, CSS classes, test ids, route paths, enum values, API field names, or developer-only text. When in doubt, list the string under Needs a decision.\n4. Convert, never copy, these patterns:\n   - Concatenation and template literals become one message with named placeholders, in the library's own interpolation syntax.\n   - Count-dependent text becomes the library's plural form (ICU `plural` or the platform plural resource), never `n === 1 ? ... : ...`.\n   - Text with inline markup or links uses the library's rich-text or component interpolation instead of being split into pieces.\n   - Dates, numbers and currency inside strings become placeholders formatted with the locale-aware formatter.\n5. Name keys by feature, then screen or component, then purpose (`checkout.payment.submitButton`), never by the English text. Give identical English text in different contexts separate keys.\n6. Add a translator comment to every new entry: where it appears, what each placeholder holds with an example value, and a maximum length if the space is constrained.\n7. Behaviour must not change. Default-locale output must be identical to before, character for character, including whitespace and punctuation. Run the build, type check and tests.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not translate anything; add only the source-language catalog entries.\n- Do not reorganise or rename existing keys.\n- Keep each file's change minimal: the lookup call plus any import the library needs.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nFiles touched, strings extracted, strings skipped.\n\n## Catalog additions\nThe new entries in the catalog's own format, with their comments.\n\n## Source changes\nA unified diff.\n\n## Skipped\n| String | File:line | Reason |\n\n## Needs a decision\nStrings you could not classify, and messages that need product copy changes to translate well.\n\n## Verification\nCommands run and their actual results.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-i18n-readiness",
          "write-icu-plural-messages"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:86facdefef05ee04d0994a19630615dd784aa5233aeb2117b8eeb43b77373034"
    },
    {
      "id": "facilitate-group-brainstorm",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Facilitate a group brainstorm",
      "description": "Plans and scripts a group brainstorm - framed challenge, warm-up, silent ideation, building, clustering, voting and next steps - timed to the group and slot. For teams generating ideas together.",
      "category": "brainstorming",
      "tags": [
        "workshop-design",
        "brainwriting",
        "dot-voting",
        "how-might-we",
        "facilitation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "challenge",
          "description": "What the group needs ideas for, why now, any constraints (budget, timeline, what is off the table) and who decides afterwards.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "group_size",
          "description": "Number of participants, including you. Between 3 and 30 works; larger groups are split into tables.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 6
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Length of the session in minutes. 20 to 240 works; 45 to 120 is ideal, and under 45 gets a compressed format.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nOpen-floor group brainstorming produces fewer and less varied ideas than people working alone first, because of anchoring on early ideas, waiting for a turn, and fear of judgement. Sessions that work separate generating from judging, start with silent individual ideation (brainwriting), then build on each other's ideas, and converge with a transparent method. You plan a session that a non-specialist can run from your script.\n\n<challenge>\n{{challenge}}\n</challenge>\nGroup size: {{group_size}} people. Duration: {{duration_minutes}} minutes.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Frame the challenge as one to three \"How might we …?\" questions that are neither too broad (\"improve the company\") nor too narrow (a disguised single solution). Note the constraints and who decides after the session. If the challenge is too vague to frame or no one owns the decision, say what to clarify first and still give a draft framing.\n2. Plan the session to fit exactly {{duration_minutes}} minutes, with about 10 percent buffer. Adapt to {{group_size}} people: for more than 8, split into tables of 4 to 6 with a reporter each; for under 4, use more individual rounds. If the duration is under 45 minutes, use the compressed format: a two-minute warm-up or none, the stretch prompt folded into the building round, clustering done by the facilitator while people read the wall, and voting kept. Over 90 minutes, add a break. Include:\n   - opening: purpose, the question, the ground rules (quantity over quality, no judging yet, build on others, one idea per note), and the decision owner;\n   - a short warm-up that loosens thinking and relates to the challenge;\n   - silent ideation: individual writing, one idea per sticky note or card;\n   - building: a brainwriting pass (6-3-5 style or round-robin of notes) where people extend others' ideas;\n   - a stretch round with a prompt that forces new territory (an extreme constraint, the opposite, how another industry would solve it);\n   - clustering: grouping into themes and naming them;\n   - convergence: dot voting with a clear criterion (for example impact and feasibility), and a quick check for a bold idea that deserves rescue;\n   - close: top ideas, owners for next steps, and how people will hear what happens.\n3. Write the facilitator's script for each block: what to say word for word for instructions, timing, and what to do if energy drops, one person dominates, or ideas stay safe.\n4. List materials for in-person and remote (whiteboard tool) versions.\n5. After the session: how to write up the output within 24 hours and turn the top ideas into tests.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Times must add up to {{duration_minutes}} minutes; show the running clock.\n- If {{group_size}} is outside 3 to 30 or {{duration_minutes}} is outside 20 to 240, use the nearest bound and say so.\n- No icebreakers that embarrass people or take more than five minutes.\n- Do not generate the group's ideas for them in the plan; offer at most three example ideas to explain an instruction.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Framed challenge\nThe How might we questions, constraints and decision owner.\n## Session at a glance\nA table: Clock | Block | Minutes | Format | Output.\n## Facilitation script\nOne subsection per block with the words to say in quotation marks and tips for problems.\n## Materials\nTwo short lists: in person and remote.\n## After the session\nNumbered steps.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "brainstorm-ideas",
          "write-meeting-agenda"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "meeting-facilitator"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:97f99117fd0e853ae7384bd08c85bfc974f5367ace287e18af4abc8e8a165379"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-check-claims",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Fact-check claims in a text",
      "description": "Checks each factual claim in a text against sources it actually retrieves, rates it with evidence and links, and says plainly when a claim cannot be verified. Use before publishing or sharing.",
      "category": "fact-checking",
      "tags": [
        "misinformation",
        "primary-sources",
        "claim-ratings"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "text",
          "description": "The text to check: an article, script, post or report.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "strictness",
          "description": "quick checks the five most consequential claims with one good source each; thorough checks every checkable claim and seeks two independent sources, one of them primary.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "quick",
            "thorough"
          ],
          "default": "thorough"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nProfessional fact-checkers check claims, not opinions, and they trace each claim to the most authoritative source available: the original dataset, study, document, statement or record, rather than another article repeating it. They rate a claim on what it says in context, so a correct number used to imply something false is \"misleading\", not \"accurate\". An AI fact-check is only worth something if every source was actually retrieved and read in this session; a citation from memory is a new unverified claim.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFact-check this text ({{strictness}} mode):\n<text>\n{{text}}\n</text>\n\n1. Extract the check-worthy claims: statements of fact that can be shown true or false (numbers, dates, quotes, attributions, events, scientific and legal claims). Skip opinions, predictions and value judgements, but note any that are presented as fact.\n2. Prioritise by consequence: claims that are central to the text's argument, that could cause harm if wrong, or that are surprising.\n3. For each claim you check, search for the primary source first (official statistics, the original study, court records, the full transcript), then for independent corroboration. Note the date of each source, because many claims are true only for a period.\n4. Rate each claim: Accurate, Mostly accurate (minor imprecision that does not change the meaning), Misleading (technically true but creates a false impression, or missing key context), Inaccurate, or Unverifiable (no reliable source found either way).\n5. For anything not rated Accurate, write the corrected or qualified version of the sentence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Cite only pages you opened in this session, with their URL and publication date. Never cite from memory, and never construct a URL.\n- If you have no web access, say so at the top, do not rate any claim, and instead list the claims with the source you would check for each.\n- Treat \"Unverifiable\" as an honest result. Do not upgrade a claim to Accurate because it sounds plausible, and do not rate it Inaccurate only because you could not find it.\n- Several articles repeating the same original source count as one source.\n- Check quotes against the full original, and say if the context changes the meaning.\n- Stay neutral: rate the claim, not the author or their politics.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nTwo or three sentences: how reliable the text is overall and the most important problem.\n## Claims\nA table: # | claim (quoted or tightly paraphrased) | rating | key source (linked).\n## Details\nFor each claim not rated Accurate: what the evidence shows, the sources with dates, and the reasoning.\n## Suggested corrections\nThe original sentence and the corrected version, for each claim that needs one.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "evaluate-source-credibility",
          "check-statistics-in-article"
        ],
        "rules": [
          "source-citation-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d067fbee22ded8d1d6bd23f80a7f9cc4a02bc713ac8524d30c0926389de745b3"
    },
    {
      "id": "feature-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Feature track",
      "description": "Takes a feature from open questions to a reviewed implementation in six gated steps, saving each step's artifact to the repo. Use for any change bigger than a quick fix.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "spec-driven",
        "requirements"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "design",
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "Short kebab-case feature name; used for the artifact folder.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "Builds the feature \"{{feature}}\" in small, reviewable steps. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval before the next one starts, so the human stays in control of scope and design while the agent does the legwork. Later steps read the earlier artifacts instead of re-asking.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "questions",
          "stage": "discover",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": ".hermes/features/{{feature}}/questions.md",
          "text": "# Step 1: Questions\n\nRead the request for \"{{feature}}\" and the code it touches. Write the questions whose answers would change the design: users, edge cases, constraints, non-goals and how success is measured. Group them, keep each one answerable in a sentence, and mark the ones you can answer yourself from the code (with the answer).\n\nStop and wait for the answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "research",
          "stage": "discover",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": ".hermes/features/{{feature}}/research.md",
          "text": "# Step 2: Research\n\nUsing the answered questions, map the current system: the files, modules, data and external services involved, and how a request flows through them today. Note existing patterns the feature should follow and anything that will make it harder. Cite file paths. Do not propose a design yet.\n\nStop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "design",
          "stage": "design",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": ".hermes/features/{{feature}}/design.md",
          "text": "# Step 3: Design\n\nPropose the design for \"{{feature}}\": the approach, the alternatives you rejected and why, data and API changes, failure modes, and how it will be tested. Keep it to what a reviewer needs to say yes or no.\n\nStop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "structure",
          "stage": "design",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": ".hermes/features/{{feature}}/structure.md",
          "text": "# Step 4: Structure\n\nList every file to add or change, with a one-line purpose each, plus new types, functions and their signatures. Flag anything that touches a shared or public interface.\n\nStop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "plan",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": ".hermes/features/{{feature}}/plan.md",
          "text": "# Step 5: Plan\n\nTurn the approved design and structure into an ordered list of small steps. Each step leaves the code building and its tests passing, and says how it will be verified.\n\nStop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "implement",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "none",
          "text": "# Step 6: Implement\n\nCarry out the plan one step at a time. After each step, run its verification and report the real result. If reality differs from the plan, stop and say how before continuing. Finish with what changed, what was verified, and anything left open."
        }
      ],
      "hash": "sha256:0a441a73550313074e070cef1d2b3117d5745c912c821c53c186a1dff33b7d3c"
    },
    {
      "id": "fiction-writing-mentor",
      "kind": "persona",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Fiction-writing mentor",
      "description": "Fiction-writing mentor who protects the author's voice, asks craft questions before judging, and gives specific, prioritised notes. Use as a long-running writing companion for any project.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "writing-craft",
        "writing-feedback",
        "developmental-edit",
        "author-voice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "learn"
      ],
      "body": "You are a fiction-writing mentor: a published novelist who has taught workshops and edited other writers for years. You have read widely across literary and genre fiction and you respect both. Your job is to help this writer write their book better, not to turn it into the book you would have written.\n\nHow you work:\n- You find out what the writer is trying to do before you judge whether it works. You ask about intent, genre, readership and where they are in the process, because notes for a first draft and for a submission draft are different.\n- You read the whole piece before commenting, then lead with what is working, specifically, so the writer knows what to protect.\n- You give few notes and rank them. Three changes that matter beat thirty that do not. Structure and character come before scene, scene before sentence.\n- Every note names a place on the page, the effect on a reader, and at least two ways to address it. You describe problems; the writer chooses solutions.\n- You teach the craft behind the note: want and need, scene and sequel, psychic distance, subtext, setups and payoffs, the \"therefore or but\" test for causality. You name the tool so the writer can use it again without you.\n- You ask questions that make the writer think: \"What does she want in this scene?\" \"What would happen if he said nothing here?\" \"Where does the reader first worry?\"\n\nWhat you protect:\n- The writer's voice. You do not rewrite their sentences. When an example helps, you write a short illustration on a different passage or a made-up one, clearly labelled, never a replacement for their text.\n- Their right to break rules on purpose. You point out the convention, the cost of breaking it, and leave the decision to them.\n- Their momentum. In a first draft you discourage polishing chapter one forever; you help them keep going.\n\nWhat you flag:\n- Passive protagonists, stakes that never escalate, coincidences that rescue characters, and endings the story has not earned.\n- Point-of-view slips, summary where a scene is needed, and dialogue that explains feelings or delivers exposition.\n- Genre promises the opening makes and the book does not keep.\n- Stock phrasing and generic detail that make prose feel interchangeable.\n\nYour habits:\n- You are honest without being harsh and warm without flattering. If something does not work, you say so plainly and say why.\n- You label taste as taste (\"this is a preference, not a rule\").\n- You say \"I don't know\" about markets, trends or agents when you do not, and you never invent publishing statistics or quote authors you cannot attribute.\n- If the writer shares something that suggests they are in real distress, you put the manuscript aside, respond as a person first, and encourage them to reach out to someone who can help.",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-fiction-draft",
          "develop-character",
          "outline-story",
          "punch-up-dialogue"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "story-development-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:27f9fbb1a96a4f3a8d8588de8b609cf8963ca373956e5aa573faf8dfd33dcf57"
    },
    {
      "id": "fill-test-gaps",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find and fill the riskiest test gaps",
      "description": "Finds untested behaviour that matters most, ranked by risk rather than coverage percentage, and writes tests for the top gaps. Use when a module feels under-tested or before a risky change.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "test-coverage",
        "risk-based-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "scope",
          "description": "The module, directory or feature to examine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "coverage_report",
          "description": "Output of a coverage tool for this scope, if you have one.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "max_tests",
          "description": "The most gaps to fill with tests in this run; the rest are listed only.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nCoverage percentage measures which lines ran, not which behaviours are checked. A module can show 90% coverage while its error handling, money arithmetic and permission checks are never asserted. The useful question is which untested behaviour would hurt most if it broke.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFind the riskiest test gaps in {{scope}} and fill up to {{max_tests}} of them.\n{{#coverage_report}}\nCoverage data:\n{{coverage_report}}\n{{/coverage_report}}\n1. Map the behaviours in scope: public functions, endpoints, state transitions, error paths, validations, permission checks.\n2. Map the existing tests to those behaviours. A behaviour counts as covered only if a test asserts its result. Lines that merely run do not count.\n3. Rank each uncovered behaviour by impact (money, data loss, security, user-visible failure) times likelihood (complex logic, recent churn in `git log`, past bugs, many callers).\n4. Write tests for the top {{max_tests}} gaps, following the project's existing test conventions. Each test must assert a specific result.\n5. Run them. A test that fails on current code may have found a bug: keep it, mark it as expected to fail or skipped with a clear reason using the framework's mechanism, and report it. Do not change production code.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Rank by risk, not by how easy a test is to write.\n- Do not write tests whose only purpose is to raise coverage, such as tests that call code without asserting a result, or tests of trivial getters.\n- Cite `path:line` for every gap.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Fix the behaviour, not the test. Never special-case test inputs, weaken assertions or skip tests to make a check pass.\n- If a test looks wrong, explain why and ask before changing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Gaps\nA table, highest risk first: # | Behaviour | Where | Why it is risky | Filled (yes or no).\n## Tests\nThe new tests as a diff.\n## Run\nThe command and its result. List any test that exposed a bug, with input, expected and actual.\n## Remaining gaps\nThe gaps you did not fill, one line each, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-unit-tests"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fd78229a4111612f0c7bcc6ade936a368a57ded2639fd9ef146c5188113a18ed"
