    {
      "id": "financial-checkup-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Yearly financial check-up",
      "description": "Runs a yearly personal finance check-up across net worth, cash flow, debt, emergency fund, insurance, retirement and goals, pausing between steps and ending with a ranked action list.",
      "category": "financial-planning",
      "tags": [
        "net-worth",
        "cash-flow",
        "emergency-fund",
        "annual-review"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "review",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "finances",
          "description": "Your household's money picture: take-home income, regular costs, savings and investment balances, pensions, debts with rates, property, insurance policies. Rough figures are fine; say who is included (you, a partner).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "Goals for the next 1, 5 and 20+ years with rough amounts and dates, such as a house deposit, a sabbatical, children's education or retiring at 60.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "country",
          "description": "Country of residence, so rules of thumb, account types and protections are framed for the right place.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "Runs this household's yearly money check-up the way a good financial planner runs an annual review: get an honest snapshot, test the foundations (debt and emergency buffer), check protection, check progress toward retirement and goals, then turn everything into a short, ranked action list. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse the approved figures instead of asking again.\n\n<finances>\n{{finances}}\n</finances>\n{{#goals}}\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{/goals}}\n{{#country}}\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n{{/country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only the figures the person gave or confirmed. Mark estimates as estimates and missing numbers as [X] with a question; never fill a gap with a typical figure without saying so.\n- Show the arithmetic so the person can check it and redo it next year.\n- Describe options and trade-offs; do not name specific products, providers, funds or lenders, and do not tell the person to buy, sell or cancel a specific investment or policy.\n- If no country is given, ask once in step 1 and keep country-specific points general until it is known.\n- If essentials or minimum debt payments cannot be covered, say so plainly in the step where it shows up and point to free, non-profit debt or money advice before continuing.\n- Do not ask for account numbers, logins or identity numbers, and tell the person to leave them out.\n- Keep a running list of open questions and of items for a professional (financial adviser, tax adviser, insurance broker), carried into step 5.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "snapshot",
          "stage": "discover",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": "financial-checkup/01-snapshot.md",
          "text": "# Step 1: Snapshot\n\n1. Net worth: table assets (cash, savings, investments, pensions, property at a cautious estimate) and liabilities (mortgage, loans, cards, overdrafts, family loans). Show liquid net worth separately from pensions and the home.\n2. Cash flow: monthly take-home income against spending, with annual and irregular costs converted to monthly. Give the surplus or shortfall and the savings rate (money saved or used to repay debt / take-home pay).\n3. Compare with last year if given; otherwise this is the baseline.\n4. Turn inconsistencies (debts without rates, savings growing while spending exceeds income) into questions.\n\nSections: Net worth, Cash flow, Savings rate, Changes since last year, Open questions. Stop for approval and answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "debt-and-buffer",
          "stage": "review",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": "financial-checkup/02-debt-and-buffer.md",
          "text": "# Step 2: Debt and emergency buffer\n\n1. Debt: table each debt with balance, rate, minimum, remaining term and fixed, variable or promotional. Flag expensive debt, promotions ending within 12 months and variable-rate exposure. Give debt payments as a share of take-home pay.\n2. Buffer: instant-access savings in months of essential spending, against the common three-to-six-month range adjusted for this household (more for single, variable or self-employed income and dependants).\n3. Order: apply the usual sequence (minimums, starter buffer, expensive debt, full buffer) to these numbers, with a monthly amount for each.\n\nSections: Debt table, Debt load, Emergency buffer, Suggested order, Open questions. Stop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "protection",
          "stage": "review",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": "financial-checkup/03-protection.md",
          "text": "# Step 3: Protection\n\n1. For each risk (earner's illness or death, job loss, home and contents, liability, car, serious health costs), record the cover in place: policies, employer benefits, bank or card cover, state support. Mark covered, partly covered, not covered or unknown; mark missing details [X] with where to find them.\n2. For gaps, show the money consequence (for example months of essentials until savings run out). Note overlaps paid twice.\n3. Check paperwork: a current will, beneficiaries on pensions and policies, and whether a partner knows where everything is.\n4. Do not recommend a policy, insurer or cover amount; list questions for an independent broker.\n\nSections: Risk map, Gaps, Overlaps, Paperwork, Questions for a broker. Stop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "retirement-and-goals",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "artifact": "financial-checkup/04-retirement-and-goals.md",
          "text": "# Step 4: Retirement and goals\n\n1. Retirement: summarise balances, personal and employer contributions and target age. Project a range at stated round real-return assumptions (for example 2%, 4%, 6% after fees), convert it to income with a cautious withdrawal assumption, and compare with the income they want. For state pensions say \"check your official forecast\"; never guess a figure.\n2. Note any employer match or tax relief that may be unused, as a question to check.\n3. Goals: per goal, the amount, date, monthly saving needed and whether they are on track. Ask for goals if none were given.\n4. Where goals compete, lay out the trade-off and let the person choose.\n\nSections: Retirement projection, Incentives to check, Goals, Trade-offs, Open questions. Stop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "action-list",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "none",
          "artifact": "financial-checkup/05-action-list.md",
          "text": "# Step 5: Action list\n\n1. Rank every action from steps 1-4: protect essentials and stop expensive debt first, then the buffer, protection gaps, long-term saving and optimisation.\n2. Keep at most seven top actions, each with the amount or target, a month, who does it and how they will know it is done.\n3. List the questions for professionals gathered along the way, by type (financial adviser, tax adviser, insurance broker, debt adviser, lawyer or notary for wills).\n4. Add a checklist for next year: figures to collect, documents to update and the date of the next check-up.\n\nSections: Top actions, For a professional, Next year's check-up, Assumptions."
        }
      ],
