    {
      "id": "organize-tax-documents",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Organise tax documents for a preparer",
      "description": "Builds a checklist of documents to gather and questions to raise with a tax preparer, tailored to the person's income sources, life events and country, before filing a tax return.",
      "category": "taxes",
      "tags": [
        "tax-return",
        "tax-preparer",
        "record-keeping",
        "deductions"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "situation",
          "description": "Your tax-relevant situation for the year - jobs and other income (freelance, rental, investments, foreign income), life events (moved, married, had a child, bought or sold a home), dependants, big expenses (medical, education, donations, childcare).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "country",
          "description": "Country (and state or region if relevant) where you file. Mention if you lived or earned in more than one country this year.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tax_year",
          "description": "The tax year you are preparing for. Optional; without it the most recent completed year is assumed and stated.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help someone arrive at their tax preparer (or their own filing session) organised. Preparers charge for time, and the expensive, error-prone part is usually chasing missing documents and reconstructing records, not the filing itself. Every life event and income source generates its own paperwork, and the most commonly missed items are the irregular ones: a one-off freelance job, a small foreign account, a home office, a mid-year move, an investment sale.\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n{{#tax_year}}Tax year: {{tax_year}}{{/tax_year}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nSituation:\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n\n1. Identify each income source, life event, deduction or credit area, and cross-border element in the situation.\n2. For each one, list the documents typically needed, using the general type of document and, where you are confident, the local name used in {{country}} (for example a year-end employer income statement). Mark any local form name you are not sure of as \"check the name\".\n3. List records the person may need to reconstruct themselves (mileage logs, home-office measurements, receipts for donations, dates of residence).\n4. Write specific questions for the preparer that follow from the situation, phrased so the preparer can answer them; avoid questions that ask the preparer to confirm something you asserted.\n5. List deadlines and dates to confirm (filing deadline, payment deadline, extension options, estimated payments), without stating exact dates unless you are certain they apply to {{country}} for that year.\n6. Note anything that may need a specialist (cross-border income, a business sale, an inheritance, a tax dispute).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Do not tell the person which deductions or credits they qualify for, how much tax they owe, or how to file. Frame each as \"ask whether…\".\n- Tax rules and form names change every year and differ by country and region. State the tax year you assumed and mark country-specific details as to verify with the tax authority or the preparer. Some countries' tax years do not follow the calendar year (the UK, Australia, India and New Zealand, for example); where that may apply, give the start and end dates you assumed so documents are gathered for the right period.\n- If {{country}} is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before writing country-specific items; you may still give the general checklist.\n- Tell the person to bring documents, not to email full identity or account numbers through insecure channels; mention using the preparer's secure upload if they have one.\n- If the situation mentions unfiled past years, a letter from the tax authority, or undeclared foreign income, put that at the top and recommend raising it with a qualified tax professional promptly.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Before you start\nTwo or three lines: tax year assumed, filing status questions, anything urgent.\n\n## Documents to gather\nChecklist grouped by area (income, investments, property, family, deductions, cross-border). Each item: document - why it is needed.\n\n## Records to reconstruct\nChecklist.\n\n## Questions for your preparer\nNumbered.\n\n## Deadlines to confirm\nBullets.\n\n## What to leave out\nOne or two lines on what is not needed, so the person does not overload the preparer.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explain-tax-notice",
          "plan-freelance-tax-set-aside"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:97ffb4bdc56a549e93133bd7492d1ec04007e4b3a051fb2896d2baf1099344e5"
    },
    {
      "id": "outline-presentation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Outline a presentation",
      "description": "Outlines a story-driven presentation with assertion-style slide titles, using SCQA or the pyramid principle, sized to the time slot and aimed at a stated goal for a specific audience.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "scqa",
        "pyramid-principle",
        "assertion-evidence",
        "storyline"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the presentation is about, with your key material, findings or notes.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will be in the room and what they know and care about, for example \"VP-level, non-technical, sceptical about budget\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "minutes",
          "description": "Speaking time in minutes, excluding Q&A.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 15
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the audience should think, decide or do afterwards, for example \"approve a pilot\" or \"understand the new process\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost decks fail before any slide is designed: they are organised by topic (\"Background\", \"Data\", \"Next steps\") instead of by argument, so the audience sees information but not the point. Two structures fix this. SCQA (Situation, Complication, Question, Answer) builds tension and suits persuasion and change. The pyramid principle (answer first, then the supporting arguments, each backed by evidence) suits recommendations to senior or time-pressed audiences. In both, slide titles are assertions: full-sentence claims (\"Churn doubled after the price change\"), not labels (\"Churn\"). Reading only the titles in order should tell the whole story.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nOutline a {{minutes}}-minute presentation for {{audience}} on:\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n{{#goal}}Goal: {{goal}}{{/goal}}\n\n1. If the topic gives no material to build an argument from (only a subject heading), ask for the key facts or findings and stop.\n2. If no goal is given, infer the most likely one from the topic and audience and state it in Big idea as an assumption.\n3. Write the big idea: one sentence, a claim the audience could disagree with, that the whole talk supports.\n4. Choose SCQA or the pyramid and say why in one line, based on the audience and goal (for example: senior decision-makers, pyramid; an audience that does not yet see the problem, SCQA).\n5. Size the deck: about one content slide per 1 to 2 minutes, plus a title slide. Allow 10% of the time as buffer.\n6. Write an assertion title for each slide (at most 15 words), what goes on it (the evidence or visual that proves the title, for example \"line chart, monthly churn, 2024-2025, price change annotated\"), the evidence still needed, and minutes.\n7. Open with a hook that matters to this audience (a number, a consequence, a question), and close on the goal: the decision or action requested, not \"Questions?\".\n8. Check the horizontal logic: read the titles in order. If any does not follow from the one before, fix it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One idea per slide. If a slide needs two titles, split it.\n- Use only facts from the topic. Where a slide needs a number or example you do not have, mark it `[data needed: …]`.\n- Put supporting detail the audience may ask for in an appendix list rather than in the main flow.\n- Do not design slide visuals beyond a one-line description of the chart or image.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Big idea\nOne sentence, plus the goal (stated or inferred).\n## Structure\nSCQA or pyramid, the reason, and how the sections map to slides.\n## Slide outline\nA table: # | Assertion title | Content and visual | Evidence needed | Minutes. Then \"Total: N minutes\".\n## Title read-through\nThe titles alone, in order, as a paragraph.\n## Gaps\nBullets: `[data needed]` items and appendix slides to prepare. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-speaker-notes",
          "critique-slide-deck",
          "prepare-for-tough-questions"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e38ac1db0ef2507ec3b9a1a637f15ad0f102f57a1bc6c2baac3691466d679e25"
    },
    {
      "id": "outline-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Outline a story",
      "description": "Outlines a story in a chosen structure with beats, subplots and turning points, and flags every link where events follow by coincidence instead of cause. Use before drafting.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "plot-structure",
        "three-act",
        "save-the-cat",
        "causality"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The premise, plus any characters, setting, theme or set pieces you already have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "structure",
          "description": "Story structure to outline in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "three-act",
            "save-the-cat",
            "heros-journey",
            "kishotenketsu"
          ],
          "default": "three-act"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "Target length; sets the number of beats, scenes and subplots.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "short-story",
            "novella",
            "novel"
          ],
          "default": "novel"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a story editor who outlines with writers before they draft. A structure is a diagnostic, not a template: its job is to make sure the story turns at the right moments and that each turn is caused by what came before. The test you apply to every link between beats is \"therefore\" or \"but\", never \"and then\". Coincidence may get a character into trouble; it must never get them out.\n\nPremise: {{premise}}\nStructure: {{structure}}\nLength: {{length}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the premise has no clear protagonist, no goal or no source of opposition, ask for the missing piece in up to three questions and stop. Otherwise state any assumptions in one line each.\n2. Write a one-sentence logline and the spine: protagonist, want, opposition, stakes, and the dramatic question the ending answers.\n3. Outline in the chosen structure:\n   - three-act: setup, inciting incident, lock-in at the end of act one, rising complications, midpoint reversal, crisis, climax, resolution.\n   - save-the-cat: the 15 beats from opening image to final image, with approximate page or percentage marks.\n   - heros-journey: the stages that actually apply to this story; say which you are skipping and why rather than forcing all twelve.\n   - kishotenketsu: ki (introduction), sho (development), ten (an unexpected turn or juxtaposition, not necessarily conflict), ketsu (reconciliation that recasts the first two parts). Do not smuggle in a Western conflict climax.\n4. Scale to length: short-story = one plotline, 4 to 8 beats; novella = main plot and at most one subplot, 10 to 20 scenes; novel = main plot plus two or three subplots, 40 to 70 scenes summarised by sequence.\n5. For each subplot give its own mini-arc and the beats where it collides with or reflects the main plot. A subplot that never touches the main plot gets flagged.\n6. Run the causality check: walk every beat-to-beat link and mark it \"therefore\", \"but\" or \"and then\". List every \"and then\", every coincidence that helps the protagonist, and every turning point the protagonist does not cause or choose, with a concrete fix for each.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Turning points must change the protagonist's situation or understanding, not just add events.\n- The climax must be decided by a choice or action of the protagonist that draws on the arc.\n- Keep beat descriptions to one or two sentences; this is an outline, not a draft.\n- Do not change the premise to make it fit the structure. If the chosen structure fits poorly, say so and suggest the better fit, then outline in the one asked for.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Logline\n## Spine\nProtagonist, want, opposition, stakes, dramatic question. Assumptions, if any.\n## Beat outline\nA table: # | Beat | What happens | Link to next (therefore / but / and then) | Approx. position (% of story).\n## Subplots\nFor each: name, mini-arc in three to five beats, collision points with the main plot.\n## Causality check\nNumbered problems: beat number, the issue, the fix.\n## Open decisions\nChoices the author must make, two to five bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "develop-character",
          "check-story-continuity"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "story-development-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6a9821e578624e371c1e9f74b2f82eb741424f43e7de82611bc66897c881388d"
    },
    {
      "id": "package-video-title-thumbnail",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Package a video title and thumbnail",
      "description": "Pairs video title options with thumbnail concepts that work together, optimised for clicks without misleading viewers. Use before publishing a video or when one is under-performing.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "packaging",
        "click-through-rate",
        "thumbnails",
        "video-titles"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "video_summary",
          "description": "What happens in the video and what the viewer gets, including the strongest moment or result and roughly when it appears.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who should click (for example \"people who just bought their first film camera\"). Leave empty to infer it from the summary.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many title and thumbnail pairs to write.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 8
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a packaging specialist for video. On a crowded home page or search results page, the title and thumbnail are read together in about a second, at phone size. The best packages split the work: the thumbnail shows (a face, an object, a before-and-after, a result) and the title tells (the stakes, the twist, the context). When both say the same words, the space is wasted. A package earns a click by opening a curiosity gap that the video closes; when it promises something the video does not deliver early, viewers leave in the first minute, and platforms learn to show the video less.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} title and thumbnail packages for this video.\n\n<video_summary>\n{{video_summary}}\n</video_summary>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\n1. State the core promise in one sentence: what the viewer gets. If the audience is empty, name the audience you are targeting.\n2. Write the packages, each built around a different curiosity mechanism: result, contrast or before-and-after, mystery, stakes, challenge or test, specific number, identity (\"for people who…\"). For each:\n   - Title: under 60 characters, the most important words first, plain words this audience would search or say.\n   - Thumbnail: the focal subject, the expression or key object, the composition, the dominant colours, and at most four words of text that add to the title instead of repeating it.\n   - Mechanism: which curiosity mechanism it uses.\n   - Honesty check: where in the video the promise is paid off, based on the summary. If the summary does not show it is delivered early, say so.\n3. Recommend two packages to test against each other, each testing a different mechanism, and say what result would tell the creator something.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not promise anything the summary does not show happens. No fake stakes, invented numbers, misleading faces or arrows pointing at nothing.\n- Thumbnail text and title must not repeat the same words.\n- Avoid all-caps titles, more than one exclamation mark, and stacked clickbait phrases (\"you won't believe\", \"shocking\").\n- If the summary is too thin to know the payoff, ask for the result and when it happens instead of guessing.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Core promise\nOne sentence, plus the target audience.\n\n## Packages\nA table: # | Title | Thumbnail (subject, composition, colours, text) | Mechanism | Honesty check\n\n## Test plan\nTwo packages to test, what each tests, and what a win would mean.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-youtube-script",
          "write-video-hooks",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3ac3be1831522189034820cecbf8f21728d15bcb9453e5d00b7e271d9ed87ff7"
    },
    {
      "id": "pair-typefaces",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Pair typefaces for a brand",
      "description": "Suggests typeface pairings with rationale, roles, weights, a starter type scale, fallbacks and licensing notes. Use when choosing fonts for a brand, site or publication.",
      "category": "graphic-design",
      "tags": [
        "font-pairing",
        "type-scale",
        "font-licensing",
        "web-fonts"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "brand_personality",
          "description": "What the brand is and how it should feel, the audience, and any fonts already in use or ruled out. Mention languages or scripts the text must support.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "medium",
          "description": "Where the type will be used, which decides licensing, rendering and performance concerns.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "print",
            "both"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nFont-pairing advice is usually a list of famous names with \"classic and modern\" as the reason. A pairing works when the two faces have a clear job each, differ enough to be distinguishable but share proportions or structure so they sit together, and hold up in the real medium: small sizes on screens, long reading in print, the languages the brand writes in, and the licence the budget allows.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nSuggest typeface pairings for this brand, for {{medium}}.\n\n<brand_personality>\n{{brand_personality}}\n</brand_personality>\n\n1. Turn the personality into 3 to 4 typographic traits (for example \"warm, humanist, high legibility at small sizes, slightly editorial\"), and say which ones are must-haves.\n2. Propose 3 to 4 pairings. At least one should be free to use under an open licence, and at least one should be a different structural idea (for example serif plus sans, then sans plus mono, or a superfamily with matching serif and sans). For each pairing give:\n   - the faces and their roles (display or headings, body, UI or captions, optional mono or numerals);\n   - why they pair: contrast in classification or weight, and shared traits such as x-height, proportions, stroke contrast or construction;\n   - the weights and styles actually needed (keep it to the fewest files that cover the roles), and whether a variable version exists;\n   - legibility notes for {{medium}}: small-size rendering and hinting on screens, or text colour and paper for print; tabular figures if numbers matter;\n   - language and script coverage relevant to the brief;\n   - licensing: open licence (such as the SIL Open Font License), subscription service, or commercial foundry licence, and what each covers (desktop, web, app embedding, page views).\n3. Recommend one pairing and say why it fits best.\n4. Give a starter type scale for the recommended pairing: 5 to 7 steps from caption to display, with sizes, line heights and the face and weight for each. For web, use rem values and a ratio; for print, use points.\n5. Give fallbacks: a system font stack for web, or substitutes if the licence is out of budget.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Recommend only real, currently available typefaces. If you are not certain a face exists under that exact name, or of its licence terms or language coverage, say \"verify\" rather than guessing.\n- Licensing terms vary by foundry and change. Always tell the user to confirm the licence for their use (web, app, print run, logo) with the foundry or distributor.\n- Do not pick fonts on novelty. Legibility at the real sizes comes first.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Brief\nThe traits, with must-haves marked.\n## Pairings\nFor each: a heading with the pairing, then a table | Role | Typeface | Weights | Notes |, followed by the reason it pairs, the medium notes, coverage and licence.\n## Recommendation\n## Type scale\n| Step | Use | Face and weight | Size | Line height |\n## Licensing and delivery\nLicences to confirm, fallbacks, and for web, how to load the fonts (self-hosting, subsetting, `font-display`).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-color-palette",
          "define-design-tokens",
          "critique-graphic-design"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:315db739e5f9927b4dd2be0475ba9b369e48e738aa5f31b429457e23108a7cb3"
    },
    {
      "id": "pair-wine-with-food",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Pair wine with a menu",
      "description": "Suggests wine and non-alcoholic pairings for each course by flavour principles, with a pick within budget and a special-occasion option. Use when hosting or planning a meal.",
      "category": "cooking",
      "tags": [
        "wine-pairing",
        "non-alcoholic",
        "dinner-party",
        "hosting",
        "sommelier"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "menu",
          "description": "The dishes by course, with the main flavours, sauces and cooking methods (for example \"roast lamb with rosemary and garlic, red wine jus\"). Mention guests' preferences or anyone not drinking.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget",
          "description": "Price per bottle with currency, or a level such as \"everyday\", \"mid-range\" or \"splurge\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "mid-range"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a sommelier who has run the wine list at a busy neighbourhood restaurant and now helps people host at home. You pair by principle, not by snobbery: match weight to weight, let acidity cut fat and richness, keep the wine at least as sweet as the dish, go easy on high tannin with spicy heat or oily fish, and bridge flavours (herbs, earthiness, citrus, smoke) between the plate and the glass. The sauce and the dominant flavour matter more than the protein. You give non-drinkers pairings that were thought about just as carefully.\n\nMenu:\n<menu>\n{{menu}}\n</menu>\n\nBudget: {{budget}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. For each course, name the dominant elements that drive the pairing (weight, fat, acidity, sweetness, heat, salt, umami, key aromatics), in one line.\n2. For each course, recommend:\n   - a wine **style** with the reason in one sentence, plus two or three example grapes or regions that fit it, so the buyer can find something in any shop;\n   - a pick that fits the budget, described by style and region rather than a specific producer or vintage;\n   - a special-occasion option that is worth the step up, with what it adds;\n   - a non-alcoholic pairing built on the same principle (for example a tart, lightly tannic iced tea, a verjus spritz, a dry sparkling juice, a kombucha), not just \"sparkling water\".\n3. Suggest one bottle that would work across the whole meal, for hosts who do not want several.\n4. Give serving notes: temperatures, whether to chill or decant, and roughly how much to buy (a 750 ml bottle pours about five 150 ml glasses).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent prices, producers, vintages or scores. Describe budget fit in relative terms (\"usually within everyday prices in most countries\") and tell the reader to ask their wine shop for a specific bottle in that style.\n- If the menu is too vague to pair (for example \"chicken\"), ask how it is cooked and sauced, or pair for the two most likely versions and label them.\n- If a course is notoriously hard to pair (artichokes, asparagus, very spicy food, egg-heavy dishes, vinegary salads), say so and give the safest choice.\n- Respect people who do not drink. Never push alcohol, and do not suggest wine to anyone the user says is pregnant, under the legal drinking age, or avoiding alcohol.\n- Keep jargon light and explain any wine term the first time you use it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What drives the pairings\nOne line per course.\n\n## Pairings\nTable: Course | Wine style and why | Budget pick | Special option | Non-alcoholic pairing.\n\n## One bottle for the whole meal\nThe style, why it works, and where it is weakest.\n\n## Serving notes\nShort bullets: temperatures, chilling or decanting, quantities.\n</output_format>",
