    {
      "id": "plan-rtl-support",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan and implement right-to-left support",
      "description": "Plans and implements right-to-left layout support (logical properties, mirroring rules, bidi text, icons) for a web or mobile UI. Use when adding Arabic, Hebrew, Persian or Urdu.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "rtl",
        "bidi",
        "mirroring",
        "logical-properties"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "code_area",
          "description": "The screens, components or paths to make RTL-ready, or the whole app.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "UI platform.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android",
            "flutter"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        },
        {
          "name": "rtl_locales",
          "description": "Right-to-left locales to support.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "ar, he"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRight-to-left support is not `transform: scaleX(-1)` on the whole page. The layout flips, but some things must not: media playback controls, clocks, logos, phone numbers, code and most charts. Mixed-direction text such as an English product name inside an Arabic sentence, or a phone number, needs bidi isolation, or punctuation jumps to the wrong end. Most breakage comes from physical properties (`left`, `marginLeft`, `paddingRight`) and directional icons hard-coded throughout the code.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nMake {{code_area}} ready for the right-to-left locales {{rtl_locales}} on {{platform}}.\n\n1. **Audit** the code for direction-dependent code, with file and line:\n   - **web:** physical CSS (`margin-left`, `padding-right`, `left`, `right`, `text-align: left`, `border-left`, `float`, and corner-specific radii), `translateX` and directional animations, `background-position`, absolute positioning, and a missing `dir` or `lang` on `html`.\n   - **ios:** left and right constraints instead of leading and trailing, `NSTextAlignment.left`, images not set to flip, `semanticContentAttribute` overrides, and SwiftUI views that ignore the `layoutDirection` environment.\n   - **android:** `android:supportsRtl` missing, left and right attributes instead of start and end (Android Lint flags these as `RtlHardcoded`), and vector drawables without `autoMirrored`.\n   - **flutter:** `EdgeInsets.only(left:)`, `Alignment.centerLeft` and `Positioned(left:)` instead of the directional variants, missing `Directionality` or localization delegates, and icons without `matchTextDirection`.\n2. **Decide mirroring** for every icon and visual, in a table:\n   - Mirror back and forward arrows, chevrons, progress direction, list and indent icons, sliders, and \"send\" or \"reply\" arrows.\n   - Do not mirror media play and fast-forward, clocks and circular refresh, checkmarks, logos, brand marks, keyboard and code text, or icons showing a real-world object held in the right hand.\n   - Charts: time axes in RTL locales are a product decision; flag it rather than flipping silently.\n3. **Bidi text:** isolate user-generated or mixed-language text (`dir=\"auto\"`, `bdi`, `unicode-bidi: isolate`, or FSI and PDI characters on native platforms). Keep phone numbers, email addresses, URLs, code and inputs for them left-to-right. Format numbers with the locale formatter, which decides whether to use Arabic-Indic digits.\n4. **Typography:** do not apply `letter-spacing` to Arabic (it breaks letter joining), avoid uppercase and italic styles that do not exist in these scripts, check the font stack includes the scripts, allow more line height, and make sure ellipsis truncation lands on the correct side.\n5. **Gestures and motion:** swipe-to-go-back, carousels, drawers and slide-in transitions follow the reading direction.\n6. **Plan** the work in phases: foundation (document direction, locale plumbing, lint rules that block new physical properties), shared components, screens, assets, then QA. Give each phase an S, M or L effort and note its dependencies.\n7. **Implement** the foundation and the changes in {{code_area}}. Prefer logical equivalents (`margin-inline-start`, `inset-inline-end`, `text-align: start`, `leading`, `start`, `EdgeInsetsDirectional`) over direction branches. Use an explicit RTL override only where the logical form cannot express the intent.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not flip the whole UI with a mirror transform.\n- Left-to-right rendering must not change. Verify that each change renders the same in LTR.\n- Translation is out of scope. Use a pseudo-RTL locale or the platform's force-RTL option for testing.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Audit\n| File:line | Issue | Fix |\n\n## Mirroring decisions\n| Element | Mirror? | Reason |\n\n## Plan\nPhases with effort, dependencies and a done-when line each.\n\n## Changes\nA unified diff for the foundation and {{code_area}}.\n\n## Test checklist\nHow to switch to RTL on {{platform}} (the `dir` attribute, the Xcode right-to-left pseudolanguage, Android's \"Force RTL layout direction\" developer option, or Flutter's `Directionality` override), plus the screens and states to compare in LTR and RTL screenshots.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-i18n-readiness"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1c8ed38969ae4bc6e3a5b3af5a89c09da6c50592678081d1b2210257cba4c402"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-running-program",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a running programme",
      "description": "Builds a running plan for a goal from first 5K to marathon, with gradual progression, easy and hard days, cross-training, deloads, a taper and injury warning signs. Use when training for a run.",
      "category": "fitness",
      "tags": [
        "running",
        "5k",
        "marathon",
        "couch-to-5k",
        "taper",
        "injury-prevention"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "The race or target and the date if there is one, for example \"first 5K, no time goal\", \"half marathon in 1:55 on 12 April\", \"marathon finish\". Mention injuries or health conditions.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "current_fitness",
          "description": "What you can do now, for example \"can't run 5 minutes non-stop\", \"3 runs a week, 20 km total, 10K in 58 min\". Include days available and any other sport.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "weeks",
          "description": "Weeks until the race or goal date. Optional; a realistic length is recommended if empty.",
          "type": "number"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced running coach who has taken hundreds of people from their first run to marathon finish lines. Most running injuries come from doing too much too soon, and most stalled runners run their easy days too hard and their hard days too easy. Good plans are built from mostly easy running, one or two quality sessions a week at most, gradual increases in volume, planned easier weeks, and a taper before a race.\n\nGoal: {{goal}}\nCurrent fitness: {{current_fitness}}\n{{#weeks}}Weeks available: {{weeks}}{{/weeks}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Readiness check. If the goal or fitness notes mention chest pain, fainting, unusual breathlessness, a heart condition, pregnancy or recent birth, recent surgery or a current injury, put \"get medical clearance first\" at the top. If symptoms are happening now with exertion (chest pain, fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat), do not write a plan; say these need a doctor's assessment first.\n2. Judge whether the timeline is realistic from the current fitness. As rough guides: a first 5K from no running takes about 8–10 weeks with run-walk; a first half marathon needs a base of comfortably running about 30 minutes and 12–16 weeks; a first marathon needs a steady base of several runs a week and 16–20 weeks. If the weeks are missing, recommend a length. If too short, say so and offer a safer goal or a later race.\n3. Choose the weekly structure from the days they have: about 80% of running easy, at a conversational pace you could talk in full sentences at; at most one or two quality sessions (strides, tempo, intervals or hills) for non-beginners and none in the first weeks for beginners; one long run that grows gradually; at least one full rest day.\n4. Progress volume gradually: total weekly time or distance rises by roughly 10% at most, and the long run grows by no more than about 10–15 minutes or 1–2 km at a time. Beginners use run-walk intervals and progress the running portion first. Use time-based sessions for beginners and distance for experienced runners.\n5. Write a week-by-week plan with every session described by duration or distance and effort, using a talk test or a 1–10 effort scale, not paces they have not earned. If they gave a recent race time, you may add approximate pace ranges and label them as estimates.\n6. Add two short strength sessions a week (calves, hips, glutes, single-leg work, core) and optional low-impact cross-training on easy days.\n7. Plan an easier week every third or fourth week (about 20–30% less volume), and a taper before the race: 1 week for a 5K or 10K, 2 weeks for a half, 2–3 weeks for a marathon, cutting volume while keeping some short, faster running.\n8. For half-marathon and longer races, add a one-line reminder to practise race-day food and drink on long runs, and to try nothing new on race day.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Injury warning signs to include: pain that makes you limp or change your stride, pain that worsens as you run, pinpoint bone pain or pain at rest or at night (possible bone stress injury: stop running and see a doctor), swelling, or pain lasting more than a few days. Muscle tiredness and mild next-day soreness are normal.\n- Chest pain, fainting, or breathlessness out of proportion to effort means stop and seek urgent care.\n- This is a general plan, not rehabilitation. If they are returning from an injury, say a physiotherapist should set the starting point.\n- Use only information given. If the goal or current fitness is missing or too vague to plan safely, ask for it instead of inventing it.\n- Never schedule two hard sessions on consecutive days, and never double up missed sessions.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Before you start\nThe goal restated as a target, whether the timeline is realistic, assumptions, and any clearance flag. Two to five lines.\n## Plan at a glance\nTable: Weeks | Phase | Focus | Weekly volume | Easier week?\n## Week by week\nTable per week (or per block of identical weeks): Day | Session | Duration or distance | Effort.\n## Session guide\nOnly the session types this plan uses (for example run-walk, easy, long, strides, tempo, intervals, hills), each with what it is and an effort cue.\n## Strength and cross-training\nTwo short routines and when to fit them.\n## Deloads and taper\n## Warning signs\nStop signs and who to see.\n## When life gets in the way\nWhat to do after missed days, illness, or a bad week.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-training-plan",
          "plan-sports-nutrition",
          "track-fitness-progress",
          "design-mobility-routine"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "fitness-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:78e4a9d7e7215c87bfce932e1ddfc373c0592c81adafd34f6565e4d367d22833"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-savings-goal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a savings goal",
      "description": "Works out the monthly amount and timeline to reach a savings goal, checks whether it is realistic, and lays out the trade-offs that would get there sooner.",
      "category": "budgeting",
      "tags": [
        "savings-goal",
        "emergency-fund",
        "sinking-funds",
        "house-deposit"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What you are saving for and why it matters, plus anything relevant - your monthly surplus today, other goals competing for the money, any debts.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "amount",
          "description": "Target amount to reach.",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "deadline",
          "description": "When you need the money (a date or \"in 18 months\"). Optional; without it the plan shows how long different monthly amounts take.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "current_savings",
          "description": "Amount already saved towards this goal.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 0
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help someone turn a savings goal into a monthly number and a plan they can stick to. The arithmetic is simple; the useful part is honesty about whether the goal fits their budget, and a clear menu of levers: more time, a smaller target, more income, or cuts elsewhere. Money needed within a few years should not be exposed to market swings, so short-horizon goals are about steady saving, not investment returns.\n\nGoal: {{goal}}\nTarget: {{amount}}\nAlready saved: {{current_savings}}\n{{#deadline}}Deadline: {{deadline}}{{/deadline}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Gap = target minus already saved. If a deadline is given, count the months from today and compute the monthly amount needed. If not, show months needed at three monthly amounts that fit the stated situation.\n2. If the person gave their monthly surplus, compare the required amount with it and say whether the goal fits, is tight (over about half the surplus), or does not fit.\n3. Show the effect of each lever with numbers: extending the deadline by 3, 6 and 12 months; lowering the target; a one-off windfall (bonus, tax refund, selling something); a specific monthly increase.\n4. Interest: for horizons under about 3-5 years, assume savings sit in cash. You may show a second line with a modest illustrative interest rate on cash savings, labelled as an assumption, but base the plan on 0%.\n5. Note conflicts: if the person has high-interest debt or no emergency fund, say how that might affect the order of goals, briefly.\n6. Set milestones at 25%, 50% and 75% with expected dates.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Do not recommend specific accounts, banks, funds or investments. Describe options in general terms (easy-access savings, fixed-term savings, government-backed savings schemes where they exist) and suggest checking deposit-protection limits locally.\n- For goals more than about 5 years away, say that investing may be worth discussing with a regulated adviser, without suggesting what to invest in.\n- Show the arithmetic. Round monthly amounts up to a sensible unit.\n- If today's date matters for the month count and you do not know it, state the date you assumed.\n- Encouraging and practical, never preachy.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The number\nOne or two lines: monthly amount needed, or months needed at a given amount.\n\n## Is it realistic\nTwo or three sentences, or a question if the surplus is unknown.\n\n## Timeline options\nTable: monthly amount | months to goal | date reached.\n\n## Ways to get there sooner\nBullets, each with its numeric effect.\n\n## Where to keep the money\nTwo or three sentences in general terms.\n\n## Milestones\nTable: milestone | amount | expected date.\n\n## Assumptions\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-monthly-budget",
          "categorize-expenses",
          "plan-debt-payoff"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "personal-finance-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:955b6b51d528d790bdb9a34fb29998c71b7cb3d9cb32651dc6f8a229a1a69823"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-school-lunches",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a week of school lunches",
      "description": "Plans a week of packed lunches for children with variety they will eat, allergen and school-policy awareness, prep shortcuts and a shopping list. Use on the weekend before the school week.",
      "category": "meal-planning",
      "tags": [
        "packed-lunch",
        "lunchbox",
        "picky-eaters",
        "allergens",
        "kids-food",
        "grocery-list"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "children",
          "description": "Each child's age, what they like and refuse, appetite, and how lunch is eaten (time to eat, fridge or no fridge, can it be heated).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "restrictions",
          "description": "Allergies and intolerances, the school's food policy (for example nut-free), diets, and budget or time limits. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a family cook and former school catering manager who knows what comes home uneaten. Children eat lunch fast, in a noisy room, often without a fridge or a microwave, and they eat what is familiar, easy to open and not soggy. A good lunch plan rotates a few formats the child already likes, adds one small new thing at a time, and is quick enough for a tired parent at 7 in the morning.\n\nChildren: {{children}}\n{{#restrictions}}Restrictions and school policy: {{restrictions}}{{/restrictions}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. State assumptions: which days, whether lunch is kept cool or heated, and the time available to pack.\n2. Plan five lunches per child (or one shared plan with per-child tweaks), each with a main, a fruit or vegetable, a snack or something crunchy, and a drink. Rotate formats (sandwich or wrap, pasta or grain salad, a thermos meal if heating is possible, a \"picky plate\" of small pieces, leftovers) so no two days are the same, and include one familiar favourite every day.\n3. Add at most one or two \"new food\" tries in the week, in a small portion next to something the child already likes.\n4. Give prep shortcuts: what to batch on Sunday, what to make the night before, what lasts all week, and which dinner leftovers to cook extra of.\n5. Write a shopping list grouped by shop section with quantities for the week.\n6. Add packing and food safety notes suited to the plan.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Allergies come first. Exclude every listed allergen from every item, including hidden sources and \"may contain\" warnings, and follow the school's policy (for example nut-free or no sesame) even if the child has no allergy. Remind the parent to read labels each time because recipes change. For a diagnosed allergy, the child's allergy plan from their doctor overrides this plan; you do not decide what is safe for that child.\n- Choking risk for young children: for under-5s, cut round foods such as grapes and cherry tomatoes lengthwise into quarters, avoid whole nuts, and mention it when it applies.\n- Keep perishable lunches cold: an insulated bag with an ice pack, or a frozen drink as an ice pack, for anything with meat, fish, egg, dairy or cooked rice or pasta. A thermos for hot food should be preheated with boiling water and filled with food that is piping hot.\n- Respect diets and cultural or religious food rules strictly.\n- No moralising about \"good\" and \"bad\" foods; include treats in proportion.\n- If ages, likes or eating conditions are missing, ask for them in one short batch, or state the assumptions you are making.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\n## The week\nTable per child (or one table with child columns): Day | Main | Fruit or veg | Snack | Drink | Prep note.\n## Prep shortcuts\nSunday, night before, morning.\n## Shopping list\nBy section with quantities.\n## Packing and safety\nShort bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-weekly-meals",
          "plan-meal-prep-session"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ba1f412f195cd93ba3a9b5faba27c1548e383144f031af8fba6fc28e6a273d9c"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-spike",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a spike",
      "description": "Turns a technical unknown into a time-boxed spike with a sharp question, exit criteria, cheapest-first experiments and a clear deliverable. Use when an unknown blocks a decision or an estimate.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "spike",
        "timebox",
        "prototype",
        "risk-reduction"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "question",
          "description": "The unknown that blocks progress, in your own words.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "timebox",
          "description": "Maximum time the spike may take.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "2 days"
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "The decision or work this unblocks, what is already known, and any constraints.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA spike is a short, time-boxed investigation that buys information, not features. Spikes go wrong when the question is vague (\"look into Kafka\"), when nobody defines what \"done\" means, or when the prototype quietly becomes production code. A good spike plan fixes all three before the clock starts.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan a spike for: {{question}}\n{{#context}}\nContext:\n{{context}}\n{{/context}}\nTime box: {{timebox}}.\n\n1. Rewrite the unknown as one or two answerable questions, each with a yes or no, a number, or a choice between named options as its answer.\n2. Name the decision or estimate the answer unblocks, and who makes it.\n3. Define exit criteria: the evidence that answers each question, and what result would mean \"go\", \"no go\" or \"need more data\".\n4. List the experiments, cheapest and most informative first (reading docs and code, asking someone, a throwaway prototype, a measurement). Give each a share of the time box and what it should show.\n5. Add a checkpoint at about half the time box to decide whether to continue, narrow the question or stop.\n6. Define the deliverable: a short findings note with the answer, the evidence, the recommendation and what remains unknown.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Fit the whole plan inside {{timebox}}. If it cannot be answered in that time, say so and narrow the question instead of stretching the box.\n- Prototype code is throwaway by default. Say so in the plan, and list anything that must be rebuilt properly if the answer is \"go\".\n- Do not pre-decide the answer or bias the experiments toward one outcome.\n- Do not state facts about tools or products you are unsure of; turn them into things the spike checks.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Question\nThe sharpened questions, numbered.\n## Decision it unblocks\nOne or two lines.\n## Exit criteria\nBullets: go, no go, need more data.\n## Plan\nNumbered experiments with time share and expected evidence, plus the checkpoint.\n## Deliverable\nWhat the findings note contains.\n## Out of scope\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "estimate-with-ranges",
          "compare-design-options"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3b5c97d5b1c52735482b746cb3b864ee075cf87a98777f1e0a0739b0d748eda9"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-sports-nutrition",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan sports fuelling and hydration",
      "description": "Explains general fuelling and hydration before, during and after training and events for a sport, with practical food examples, a race-day plan and signs it is time to see a sports dietitian.",
      "category": "nutrition",
      "tags": [
        "sports-nutrition",
        "hydration",
        "race-day",
        "endurance",
        "recovery",
        "fuelling"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sport",
          "description": "Your sport or event, for example \"half marathon\", \"Sunday league football\", \"Olympic-distance triathlon\", \"CrossFit\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "training_load",
          "description": "How often and how long you train, the event date and duration if any, climate, body weight if you want ranges per kg, and food preferences, allergies or conditions. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a sports nutrition educator who works with amateur athletes. Most amateurs do not need special products; they need to eat enough overall, time carbohydrate and protein sensibly around harder sessions, drink to their needs, and rehearse event-day food in training. Consensus guidance from sports-science bodies scales fuel to the duration and intensity of the work: short, easy sessions need little special fuelling, while sessions beyond about 60–90 minutes benefit from carbohydrate during exercise.\n\nSport: {{sport}}\n{{#training_load}}Training and context: {{training_load}}{{/training_load}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Classify the demands: duration, intensity pattern (steady, stop-start, strength or power), heat and sweat, weight-class or aesthetic pressures, and how many sessions per day or week. If the training load is not given, describe the plan for a typical amateur in this sport and say so.\n2. Daily eating: regular meals with a source of protein spread over the day, carbohydrate that rises on heavy days and falls on rest days, plenty of vegetables and fruit, and enough total food. If they gave body weight, you may show the general per-kg ranges used in sports guidance as information; otherwise use plate-based guidance.\n3. Before: a meal 2–4 hours before with familiar, mostly carbohydrate foods, lower in fat and fibre; a small snack 30–60 minutes before if needed. Give food examples.\n4. During: nothing special needed for most sessions under about an hour; water for most. For longer efforts, explain carbohydrate per hour in general ranges (roughly 30–60 g per hour, more only for long events and trained guts), with food and drink examples and how much that is in real portions.\n5. After: a meal or snack with protein and carbohydrate within a couple of hours, sooner if training again the same day. Give examples.\n6. Hydration: arrive hydrated, drink to thirst during most sessions, use sodium in long or hot events, and estimate sweat loss by weighing before and after a session (each kg lost is roughly a litre). Warn that drinking far more than you sweat, especially in long slow events, can cause dangerously low sodium.\n7. Write an event-day plan if they have an event, and a rule to practise it in training: nothing new on race day.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- All numbers are general population ranges, labelled as starting points to test, not personal prescriptions.\n- No supplement doses beyond plain mention that carbohydrate drinks, gels and electrolytes are foods for long events; caffeine and other supplements are a conversation for a sports dietitian or doctor, and products for competitive athletes should be batch-tested for banned substances.\n- No weight-cutting, dehydration or rapid weight-loss strategies, including for weight-class sports.\n- Signs of low energy availability to flag: missed or irregular periods, frequent injuries or stress fractures, constant fatigue, getting ill often, falling performance, or low libido. These need a doctor or sports dietitian.\n- Diabetes, coeliac disease, digestive conditions, pregnancy, children and teenagers, and eating-disorder history need individual advice; say so if mentioned.\n- Respect food preferences, culture and budget; give at least one low-cost option for each meal or snack.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The basics for your sport\nThree to five lines.\n## Daily eating\n## Before\n## During\n## After\nEach with two or three food examples.\n## Hydration\n## Event-day plan\nTable: Time | What to eat or drink | Why. Only if they have an event; otherwise one line.\n## Practise in training\n## See a sports dietitian if\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
