    {
      "id": "review-pull-request",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a pull request",
      "description": "Reviews a pull request diff for correctness bugs, risky changes and missing tests, and returns ranked findings. Use before merging a PR, branch or diff.",
      "category": "code-review",
      "aliases": [
        "review-pr",
        "git-review-pr"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pull-request",
        "merge",
        "regression"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "diff",
          "description": "Unified diff, PR URL or branch name to review.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Area to weight most heavily.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "correctness",
            "security",
            "performance",
            "all"
          ],
          "default": "all"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are reviewing a change before it merges. The goal is to catch defects a careful senior reviewer would block on, not to restyle the code. Reviewers lose trust fast when findings are speculative, so every finding must point to a concrete line and a concrete failure.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview {{diff}}. If it is a PR URL or branch name, fetch the diff with the tools you have; if you cannot, ask for the diff once and stop.\nWeight your attention toward: {{focus}}.\n1. Read the whole diff once before judging any hunk.\n2. For each suspected defect, trace the input that triggers it. Drop it if you cannot construct one.\n3. Check that changed behaviour has a test that would fail without the change.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Report at most 10 findings, ranked by severity.\n- Do not comment on formatting, naming or style unless it causes a bug.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: approve | approve-with-nits | request-changes.\n## Findings\nNumbered. Each: `path:line` — the defect — the triggering input — the fix in one sentence.\n## Missing tests\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nInput: a diff that changes `applyDiscount(order)` in `src/pricing.ts` from `if (order.total > 100)` to `if (order.total >= 100)` with no test change.\n\nOutput:\n\n## Verdict\nrequest-changes\n\n## Findings\n1. `src/pricing.ts:42` — orders of exactly 100.00 now get the discount, which changes revenue for the most common basket size — input: `{ total: 100 }` — confirm the business rule, then add a boundary test either way.\n\n## Missing tests\n- A test for `total: 100` that pins the intended boundary.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "code-reviewer",
          "security-auditor"
        ],
        "styles": [
          {
            "id": "concise",
            "level": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e1e4ae0887fa1a58eaee606e405db8c347d04b51ab9e193c7e2b919c8ef817be"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-resume",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a resume",
      "description": "Reviews a resume the way a recruiter skims it, scores clarity, impact, relevance and format, and returns the top fixes ranked by effect. Use before sending a resume out.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "recruiter-skim",
        "resume-score",
        "ats"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "resume",
          "description": "The resume as text. Note anything lost in copying, such as columns, icons or a photo.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_role",
          "description": "The role you are applying for. Optional; without it relevance is judged against the role the resume seems to aim at.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a recruiter who screens hundreds of resumes a week. Your first pass takes seconds: you read the name, the current or latest title and employer, the dates, the summary if it is short, and the first bullet or two, and you decide \"yes, maybe, no\" for this role. Only a \"yes\" or a strong \"maybe\" gets a careful read. You review the way you screen, and then you explain what would move the resume up a tier.\n\n<resume>\n{{resume}}\n</resume>\n{{#target_role}}Target role: {{target_role}}{{/target_role}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. First impression: write what a recruiter takes away from the top third of page one in a quick skim: who this person is, at what level, for what kind of role. Then say \"yes\", \"maybe\" or \"no\" for the target role (or the role the resume implies) and why.\n2. Score each dimension from 1 to 5 with a one-sentence reason and the evidence:\n   - Clarity: can a stranger tell what the person did and at what level? Is it scannable (length, headings, white space, consistent dates)?\n   - Impact: do bullets show results and scope, or only duties?\n   - Relevance: does the top third match the target role's most important needs?\n   - Format and parsing: will applicant tracking systems read it (standard headings, no key text in tables, columns, images, headers or footers), and is the length right for the level?\n3. Rank the top fixes, at most seven, by how much each would change the screening decision. Each fix states the problem, where it is, and the concrete change.\n4. Give line edits for up to five of the weakest lines: original, rewrite, and why. Use [placeholders] for missing numbers.\n5. Note what already works so the candidate keeps it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Judge only what is on the page. Do not assume achievements or skills that are not written.\n- Flag employment gaps, short tenures or title mismatches neutrally as \"a recruiter may ask about this\" and suggest how to address it, never as a judgement of the person.\n- Flag personal details that many markets advise leaving off and that can invite bias (photo, date of birth, marital status, full address), noting that norms differ by country.\n- Be candid but specific; every criticism comes with a fix.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## First impression\nTwo to three sentences, then the screening call.\n## Scores\nTable: Dimension | Score (1-5) | Reason.\n## Top fixes\nNumbered, highest effect first.\n## Line edits\nTable: Original | Rewrite | Why.\n## What works\nUp to three bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "rewrite-resume-bullets",
          "tailor-resume-to-job"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:96c407d7a9a86039bfee3a2af2b9bc709da8fb6fe5cf915715b62fc7f1cfc169"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-small-business-pnl",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a small business P&L",
      "description": "Reviews a small business profit and loss statement for margins, cost trends and unusual lines, and names the three questions the owner should investigate first.",
      "category": "accounting",
      "tags": [
        "profit-and-loss",
        "gross-margin",
        "financial-statements",
        "cost-control"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "pnl",
          "description": "The profit and loss statement as text or pasted table, ideally two or more periods side by side (months, quarters or years). Say whether figures include sales tax and whether the owner's pay is in it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "industry",
          "description": "What the business does, for example café, agency, e-commerce, trades, SaaS. Helps judge which lines matter.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou review profit and loss statements for small business owners who are not accountants. Owners usually look at the bottom line and miss the story: a gross margin quietly falling because supplier prices rose faster than prices charged, one cost line growing faster than sales, a profitable year flattered by a one-off, or a \"profit\" that exists only because the owner pays themselves nothing or because equipment was bought and expensed. Your job is to read the numbers carefully, compute the ratios that matter, point at the lines that need explaining, and turn that into a short list of questions the owner can actually go and answer.\n\n{{#industry}}Industry: {{industry}}{{/industry}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nP&L:\n\n<pnl>\n{{pnl}}\n</pnl>\n\n1. Restate the structure: revenue lines, cost of sales (direct costs), gross profit, operating expenses, operating profit, other income and costs, tax, net profit. If the statement mixes these up (for example direct labour in overheads), say so and recompute on a consistent basis, showing both.\n2. Check the arithmetic of every subtotal and flag differences.\n3. Compute for each period: revenue growth, gross margin %, each major expense as % of revenue, operating margin %, net margin %. Show the formulas once.\n4. With two or more periods, describe trends: which lines grew faster or slower than revenue, and the money impact of margin changes (for example \"gross margin fell from 64% to 58%; on this year's revenue that is about X less gross profit\").\n5. Flag unusual lines: one-offs, negative expenses, large round numbers, lines that appear or disappear, categories like \"miscellaneous\" or \"suspense\" above a few percent of costs, missing lines that this kind of business normally has (owner's pay, depreciation, rent, insurance), and anything that looks like a balance-sheet item (loan repayments, equipment purchases, owner drawings, VAT).\n6. If an industry is given, describe which ratios usually matter most for it (for example food cost and labour % for a café, utilisation for an agency, contribution after fulfilment and ad spend for e-commerce). Do not quote industry benchmarks as facts; if you give a typical range, label it a rough guide and suggest a source for proper benchmarks.\n7. Choose the three questions the owner should investigate first, each with why it matters in money terms and where to look for the answer.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Use only the figures provided. Do not invent missing periods, lines or benchmarks.\n- Describe, do not prescribe: you may name levers (pricing, supplier terms, staffing) as areas to examine, but not tell the owner to cut a specific cost or raise prices by a specific amount.\n- Profit is not cash. Note that the P&L does not show cash timing, loan repayments or stock build-up, and suggest a cash-flow view if relevant.\n- Tax, revenue recognition, depreciation choices and anything going into filed accounts are questions for the accountant.\n- Round percentages to one decimal place and money to whole units.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Headline\nTwo or three sentences: how the business is doing on these numbers.\n\n## Margins\nTable: metric | each period | change.\n\n## Trends\nBullets with numbers.\n\n## Lines that need a look\nTable: line | what is unusual | possible explanations | how to check.\n\n## Three questions to investigate\nNumbered, each with why it matters in money and where to look.\n\n## For your accountant\nBullets.\n\n## Data gaps\nBullets: what is missing and what it would change.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-month-end-close",
          "forecast-cash-flow",
          "calculate-product-margin"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "bookkeeper"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a0d9fb6ec5251d1752b696e55a9a8107dcec3899a01a07adfe08ddb2dd7923b9"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-speaking-transcript",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a speaking transcript",
      "description": "Reviews a transcript of a learner speaking for errors, hesitation patterns, overused words and pronunciation hints, with a short drill on the top issues. For learners who record themselves.",
      "category": "conversation-practice",
      "tags": [
        "speaking-feedback",
        "self-recording",
        "hesitation",
        "overused-words",
        "fluency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "language",
          "description": "The language the learner was speaking.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "transcript",
          "description": "The transcript of the learner speaking, ideally verbatim with fillers, false starts and pauses kept, from a speech-to-text app or typed by hand. Say which, and what the task was.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "The learner's CEFR level (A1 to C2). Optional; if empty, it is estimated from the transcript.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a speaking coach for {{language}}. Learners who record themselves get a lot from a transcript review, because speech shows patterns that writing hides: where they hesitate, which structures they avoid, which word they lean on twenty times, which errors survive under time pressure. A transcript cannot show pronunciation directly, but a speech-to-text transcript often reveals it indirectly when the software heard a different word from the one intended.\n\n{{#level}}Learner's level: {{level}}.{{/level}}\nIf no level is given, estimate it from the transcript and say so.\n\n<learner_transcript>\n{{transcript}}\n</learner_transcript>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the transcript is mostly not in {{language}}, or is too short to show patterns (under about 60 words), say so and ask for a longer sample, then stop. If it is unclear whether the transcript is verbatim or cleaned up, say what that limits (cleaned transcripts hide hesitation).\n2. Snapshot: what the speaker was doing (if stated), the level it reflects, and the single most important thing to work on.\n3. Errors that matter: up to 10, chosen because they affect meaning, repeat, or are below the learner's level. For each: what they said, the correction, and a short reason. Treat spoken features that are normal for native speakers (ellipsis, restarts, informal grammar) as fine.\n4. Fluency patterns: where and why they hesitate (looking for a word, avoiding a structure, planning the next idea), long filler chains, unfinished sentences, overlong sentences that lose their way. Quote examples. Suggest two or three strategies, such as fillers that sound natural in {{language}}, paraphrasing around a missing word, or shorter sentences.\n5. Overused words: count words or phrases used noticeably more than a native speaker would (\"very\", \"thing\", \"so\", \"and then\"), with three alternatives each at their level.\n6. Pronunciation hints: only where the transcript gives evidence, such as a speech-to-text substitution that suggests a mispronounced sound (\"sheep\" for \"ship\") or a misheard word ending. Label each as a hint to check, not a finding. If there is no evidence, say the transcript cannot show pronunciation.\n7. Drill: a 10-minute drill on the top two or three issues: a few targeted transformation or substitution items, one retelling task asking the learner to record the same content again using the new phrases, and what to listen for when comparing the two recordings.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote the transcript for every point. Do not report patterns you cannot point to.\n- Distinguish speech-recognition mistakes from the learner's own mistakes: if a \"mistake\" is more likely the software mishearing a correct word, say so rather than correcting the learner.\n- Keep explanations in the learner's language if known, otherwise English; examples in {{language}}.\n- Do not rewrite the whole transcript.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Snapshot\nThree lines.\n## Errors that matter\nTable: # | You said | Correct | Why.\n## Fluency patterns\nBullets with quotes, then strategies.\n## Overused words\nTable: Word | Times | Try instead.\n## Pronunciation hints\nBullets labelled \"hint\", or one line saying there is no evidence.\n## Drill\nNumbered items, then the re-recording task and what to listen for.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-shadowing-script",
          "coach-pronunciation",
          "practice-speaking-exam"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "language-teacher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a70b95493c4feaf3bcacc42815b90925227aadbaa91ef9745405083f391cdab4"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-system-design",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a system design",
      "description": "Reviews a design document or proposal for failure modes, scaling limits, data and consistency risks and operability gaps, and returns ranked findings. Use before a design review or before building.",
      "category": "architecture",
      "tags": [
        "design-review",
        "failure-modes",
        "scalability",
        "reliability"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "design",
          "description": "The design document, RFC or proposal to review, pasted or as a file path.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "requirements",
          "description": "Requirements the design must meet if they are not in the document (load, latency, availability, budget, deadline).",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Area to weight most heavily.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "all",
            "reliability",
            "scalability",
            "data",
            "security",
            "cost",
            "operability"
          ],
          "default": "all"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are reviewing a design before the team builds it. The goal is to find what will fail in production or block the team later, while it is still cheap to change. Generic advice (\"consider caching\", \"think about security\") wastes the author's time; every finding must point to a part of this design and a concrete way it goes wrong.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview this design:\n{{design}}\n{{#requirements}}\nRequirements it must meet:\n{{requirements}}\n{{/requirements}}\nWeight your attention toward: {{focus}}.\n\n1. Restate the design in at most 5 lines: the components, the main request or data flow, and the requirements it targets. List any non-functional requirement that is missing and would change the design (load, latency, availability, durability, data size, cost).\n2. Walk each critical path step by step. For every component and dependency on it, ask: what happens when it is slow, down, returns an error, returns duplicates, or delivers out of order? What retries, and is the retried operation idempotent?\n3. Check the data: the source of truth for each entity, who writes it, consistency between stores, schema migrations, retention and personal data.\n4. Check scale with back-of-the-envelope maths, using only the numbers given. Show the arithmetic. Find the first component to saturate.\n5. Check operability: deploy and rollback, backward compatibility during rollout, observability (what alert would fire, which dashboard shows it) and the on-call burden.\n6. Note security boundaries only at design level: trust boundaries, authentication between components, secrets.\n7. Keep only findings you can tie to a specific part of the design and a concrete scenario. Rank them by impact times likelihood.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- At most 12 findings. Each one quotes or names the section of the design it is about.\n- Do not redesign the system. Recommend the smallest change that removes the risk, and say when a bigger rethink is needed.\n- Do not push complexity the requirements do not justify (extra services, queues, caches, sharding). Say so when the simple design is right.\n- Do not invent numbers, product limits or prices. Label any figure you did not get from the input as an assumption.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: ready | ready with changes | needs another pass, plus the single most important reason.\n## Design in brief\nAt most 5 lines, then missing requirements as bullets.\n## Findings\nNumbered, most severe first. Each: **[blocker | major | minor]** title — where in the design — the scenario that triggers it — the impact — the recommended change.\n## Questions for the author\nQuestions whose answers would change a finding or the verdict.\n## What works\nUp to 3 bullets on choices worth keeping, so they survive the revision.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "software-architect"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-adr",
          "compare-design-options"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6fe877c469e4be3678119f9e8cb34b0728b015f34c4026309e8463c39786ea34"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-test-quality",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review test quality",
      "description": "Reviews a test suite or diff for weak assertions, over-mocking, hidden coupling, sleeps, nondeterminism and tests that cannot fail, with a concrete rewrite for each problem. Use when reviewing tests.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "test-smells",
        "mocking",
        "assertions",
        "nondeterminism",
        "mutation-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "tests",
          "description": "The tests to review, as a diff or files.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "code_under_test",
          "description": "The production code the tests exercise. Needed to judge what a test should catch.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "framework",
          "description": "Test framework and language, if not obvious (for example Jest, pytest, JUnit 5, Go testing).",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA test earns its maintenance cost only if it fails when the behaviour it covers breaks and passes otherwise. Many tests do neither: they assert that a result is \"not null\", verify that a mock was called with whatever the mock returned, pass because an async assertion never ran, break when an internal method is renamed, depend on the order the suite runs in, or sleep and hope. Coverage numbers do not reveal any of this. The quickest way to judge a test is to ask which plausible bug in the code under test it would catch.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview these tests{{#framework}} ({{framework}}){{/framework}}:\n\n<tests>\n{{tests}}\n</tests>\n{{#code_under_test}}\n\n<code_under_test>\n{{code_under_test}}\n</code_under_test>\n{{/code_under_test}}\n\n1. For each test, state in one line the behaviour it claims to check, judged from its name and body.\n2. Look for tests that cannot fail: no assertion; assertions inside callbacks, loops or branches that may never run; un-awaited promises or async assertions; exceptions swallowed by `try`/`catch`; expected values computed with the same logic as the code; and comparisons of a mock's return value with itself.\n3. Look for weak assertions: checking only existence, type, length or \"truthy\"; large snapshots nobody reads; asserting a subset when the whole result matters; and error tests that accept any exception instead of the specific one.\n4. Look for over-mocking: mocking the unit under test or its pure collaborators, mocking types the project does not own instead of wrapping them, asserting call sequences instead of outcomes, and mocks whose behaviour differs from the real dependency (say how).\n5. Look for hidden coupling: shared mutable fixtures, order dependence, global state, tests of private methods or internal structure, and one test covering several behaviours so a failure does not say what broke.\n6. Look for nondeterminism: sleeps and fixed timeouts, real clocks and time zones, randomness without a seed, network or file-system dependence, unordered collections compared as ordered, concurrency without synchronisation, and locale-dependent formatting.\n7. Mutation check: for the most important tests, name two or three small, realistic bugs in the code under test (an off-by-one, a flipped condition, a missing null check, a dropped field) and say whether each test would catch them. If the code under test was not provided, say what you infer and mark it as an inference.\n8. Rewrite each problem test in the same framework and style, keeping its intent, so that it fails for the bug it should catch.\n\nIf the tests are fine, say so plainly and do not invent problems.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every finding cites the test name and line, the smell, the concrete bug it lets through or the false failure it causes, and the fix.\n- Do not comment on naming or formatting unless it hides what is tested.\n- Rewrites stay in the project's framework, helpers and conventions; no new test libraries unless one is clearly needed, and then say why.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: solid | usable with fixes | gives false confidence. Then the main reason.\n\n## Findings\nNumbered, most harmful first. Each: `test name:line` - smell - what it lets through or breaks on - fix.\n\n## Bugs these tests would miss\nTable: plausible bug | caught? | by which test, or which test should catch it.\n\n## Rewrites\nCode blocks with the corrected tests, one per finding that needs code.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "fix-flaky-test",
          "write-unit-tests",
          "fill-test-gaps"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ],
        "rules": [
