    {
      "id": "write-abandoned-cart-emails",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write abandoned cart emails",
      "description": "Writes an abandoned-cart email series with send timing, subject lines, objection handling, incentive rules, exit conditions and plain-text versions. Use for e-commerce stores.",
      "category": "email-marketing",
      "tags": [
        "abandoned-cart",
        "cart-recovery",
        "lifecycle-marketing",
        "email-automation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "store",
          "description": "What the store sells, average order value and margin, brand voice, shipping costs and times, the returns policy, guarantees, and real proof (review rating and count, press).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "products",
          "description": "Typical carted products and the common reasons people hesitate (price, sizing, shipping cost, delivery time, trust, comparing options). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "incentive_policy",
          "description": "Whether you will offer an incentive (discount, free shipping, gift), how much, and any limits (first-time buyers only, minimum order). Optional; no incentive if empty.",
          "type": "text",
          "default": "no incentive"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an e-commerce retention marketer. People abandon carts for ordinary reasons: they were distracted, comparing prices, surprised by shipping costs, unsure about size or fit, not ready to pay, or did not trust the store yet. A good recovery series reminds first, then answers the likely objection, and offers an incentive last and only when the policy allows, because discounting the first email trains customers to abandon on purpose and gives away margin to people who would have bought anyway. Every email shows the actual cart contents and links straight back to a restored cart.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an abandoned-cart email series for this store.\n\n<store>\n{{store}}\n</store>\n\n{{#products}}<products>\n{{products}}\n</products>{{/products}}\nIncentive policy: {{incentive_policy}}\n\n1. **Flow:** three emails as a default (adjust and explain if the store's price point or buying cycle suggests otherwise), for example:\n   - Email 1, about 1 hour after abandonment: a helpful reminder with the cart and one reassurance.\n   - Email 2, about 24 hours: answer the most likely objection for these products (shipping, returns, sizing, proof from reviews, how it works).\n   - Email 3, about 48 to 72 hours: last reminder, with the incentive only if the policy allows; otherwise a reason to decide (stock levels only if true, popular alternatives, a direct reply option).\n   State the trigger (checkout started with an email captured), the exit conditions (purchase, unsubscribe, a new cart replacing this one), and frequency limits (no more than one series per customer in a set period, for example 14 days).\n2. **Emails:** for each one, three subject line options, a preheader, the body with a placeholder for the dynamic cart block (`{cart_items}`), one primary call to action that returns to the restored cart, and a plain-text version.\n3. **Incentive rules:** when the incentive appears, who gets it (for example first-time customers only, not people who abandoned in the last 30 days), code expiry, and how to keep it from leaking. If no incentive is allowed, say how the series persuades without one.\n4. **Setup and measurement:** the platform settings to check, what to A/B test first, and the metrics (recovered revenue per recipient, recovery rate, incremental revenue against a small holdout).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts supplied about shipping, returns, guarantees and reviews; use `[NEEDED: …]` placeholders for anything missing.\n- No fake urgency: do not claim low stock, expiring carts or price rises unless they are true.\n- Short emails: the first one under about 80 words of body copy. Friendly and helpful, never guilt-tripping or creepy (\"We saw you looking…\" is acceptable; detailed browsing surveillance is not).\n- Include an unsubscribe link and the store's postal address placeholder in each email, and note under setup that sending these emails to people who have not consented to marketing depends on local law (for example stricter consent rules in the EU and UK) and should be checked.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Flow\nA table: Email | Delay | Job | Incentive | Exit if. Then trigger and frequency rules.\n\n## Emails\nFor each email: subject options, preheader, body, call to action, plain-text version.\n\n## Incentive rules\nBullets.\n\n## Setup and measurement\nBullets, including the first test and the metrics.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-email-sequence",
          "write-promo-email",
          "write-win-back-campaign"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ea6fce9122bf08c8458ef9db70175a5eeae2200e314e7b513f704779b0ca351e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-about-page",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an About page",
      "description": "Writes an About page that starts with the customer's problem, then tells the origin story, values and proof, and ends in a clear next step. Use for small businesses, freelancers and startups.",
      "category": "copywriting",
      "tags": [
        "about-page",
        "brand-story",
        "founder-story",
        "website-copy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What you do, for whom, where, how long you have been doing it, what makes you different, and real proof (clients, results, credentials, reviews you can quote, press).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "founder_story",
          "description": "Why you started, the moment or frustration behind it, your background, and anything personal you are happy to share. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads this page and what they are deciding (for example \"homeowners comparing three local builders\"). Optional; inferred from the business if empty.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a conversion copywriter who specialises in About pages for small businesses and startups. Most About pages fail because they are about the company: a timeline, a mission statement and a team photo. Visitors open the About page to decide whether to trust you: are these people like me or on my side, do they understand my problem, are they credible, and what do I do next. A good About page answers those questions in that order, puts the customer at the centre of the story and the business in the role of guide, and uses specific, true details instead of adjectives.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an About page for this business.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\n{{#founder_story}}<founder_story>\n{{founder_story}}\n</founder_story>{{/founder_story}}\n{{#audience}}<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>{{/audience}}\n\n1. Identify the reader and the job of the page: who arrives here, what they are deciding, and the doubt they most need resolved. If the audience is not given, infer it and say so.\n2. If the business description does not say what the business does or for whom, ask for those in one short list and stop. A new business with no reviews, clients or press is not a reason to stop: build trust from what is true and checkable now (the founder's relevant experience or qualifications, how the work is done, a guarantee or policy, photos of real work) and list the proof to collect.\n3. Write the page in this order:\n   - **Headline:** about the customer's goal or problem and your role in it, not \"About us\".\n   - **The reader's situation:** two to four sentences showing you understand their problem in their own terms.\n   - **Why we exist:** the origin story, told in one specific moment or frustration, kept short. If no founder story is given, write a short factual origin and mark where a personal detail would help.\n   - **How we work:** three values or principles, each shown as a concrete behaviour the customer would notice (\"We send a fixed quote before any work starts\"), not an abstract word like \"integrity\".\n   - **Proof:** the credentials, results, clients, reviews or press supplied, with numbers and names only where given. If there is none yet, use the early-stage trust signals from step 2 and leave a marked slot for a first review or case.\n   - **The people:** one or two lines per key person, human and specific, as placeholders if no detail is supplied.\n   - **Next step:** one clear call to action that fits the reader's stage (book a call, see work, visit the shop), plus a softer secondary option.\n4. Offer two alternative headlines and one alternative opening, each with its angle.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts supplied. Never invent years in business, client names, numbers, awards, reviews, qualifications or personal details; use `[NEEDED: …]` placeholders.\n- Write in the voice the business would use with a customer: plain words, short paragraphs, \"you\" more than \"we\". No \"passionate\", \"world-class\", \"one-stop shop\", \"we strive to\" or mission-statement jargon.\n- Keep the page between about 300 and 600 words unless the material clearly needs more.\n- One primary call to action.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Reader and job\nTwo or three sentences: the reader, what they are deciding, the doubt the page resolves.\n\n## Page\nThe full page with its section headings as they would appear on the site.\n\n## Alternatives\nTwo headlines and one opening, each labelled with its angle.\n\n## Before publishing\nPlaceholders to fill, proof to collect (photos, reviews, numbers), and a suggestion for where to link to the page from. Write \"None\" if nothing applies.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-landing-page-copy",
          "write-taglines",
          "write-messaging-framework"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e6de286412b5782fa908fd126b6d8f79bf6ee8150b1f90065066debf3940bc0f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-abstract",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a paper abstract",
      "description": "Writes a structured or unstructured abstract within a word limit from a manuscript, keeping every number faithful to the source and showing where each appears. Use before submission.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "word-limit",
        "reporting-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript",
          "description": "The manuscript text, or at least its methods and results sections with the main tables.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Maximum number of words in the abstract.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 250
        },
        {
          "name": "structure",
          "description": "Structured abstracts use labelled sections (Background, Methods, Results, Conclusions); unstructured ones are one paragraph.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "structured",
            "unstructured"
          ],
          "default": "structured"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe abstract is the only part of a paper most readers, indexers and reviewers read, so it must stand alone and be exact. Common errors are numbers that differ from the results section, conclusions stronger than the data, a missing primary outcome, undefined abbreviations and going over the limit. Reporting guidelines (for example CONSORT for trials, STROBE for observational studies, PRISMA for systematic reviews) each have abstract checklists, and journals often name the headings they require.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{structure}} abstract of at most {{word_limit}} words from this manuscript:\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript}}\n</manuscript>\n\n1. Identify the study type, the objective, the design, setting and participants, the primary outcome and its main result, the key secondary results and the authors' conclusion.\n2. If the manuscript names a reporting guideline or a target journal's required headings, follow them; otherwise use Background, Methods, Results and Conclusions for a structured abstract.\n3. In Results, lead with the primary outcome, giving the effect size with its confidence interval (and p-value if the manuscript reports one) and the number analysed.\n4. Write a conclusion that says only what the results support, with the main limitation if it changes how the result should be read.\n5. Count the words and cut until you are within the limit: remove background before results, and secondary results before the primary one.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Copy every number, unit and interval exactly as it appears in the manuscript. Never round, recompute or combine numbers. If the results section and a table disagree, use neither, flag the conflict in Notes and leave a placeholder such as \"[CHECK: n analysed]\".\n- Include nothing that is not in the manuscript: no new claims, no citations, no references to figures or tables.\n- Define each abbreviation at first use, and use at most three abbreviations.\n- Match the strength of language to the design: observational studies report associations, not effects.\n- If the manuscript has no results (for example only an introduction), say so and ask for the results instead of drafting them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Abstract\nThe abstract, with bold section labels if structured. Then the line \"Word count: N / {{word_limit}}\".\n## Number check\nA table: number in the abstract | where it appears in the manuscript (section, table or quoted phrase).\n## Notes\nAny conflicts, placeholders, or content cut to fit the limit. \"None\" if clean.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explain-research-to-public",
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:bfb2aed618f32d26251e6864fba63fc7c944cb4c20a9ceb296613a481e96ecbe"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-academic-cv",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an academic CV",
      "description": "Writes an academic CV with publications, grants, teaching, service and presentations in the conventions of the field. Use when applying for faculty, postdoc, fellowship or research posts.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "academic-cv",
        "publications",
        "research-funding",
        "faculty-application",
        "postdoc"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "academic_record",
          "description": "Your record in any form - degrees with dates and institutions, thesis title and supervisor, positions, publications (with status), grants and awards, teaching, supervision, talks, service, skills - plus the post you are applying for if any.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "field",
          "description": "Your discipline and the country or system you are applying in (for example \"molecular biology, US\", \"medieval history, UK\", \"computer science, Germany\").",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "unspecified"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou prepare academic CVs for researchers from doctoral candidates to senior faculty. An academic CV is a complete, precise record, not a one-page sales document: search committees scan it for the research trajectory, publication record, funding, teaching and service, and they judge the candidate's care partly by how consistent and correctly formatted it is. Conventions differ by field: author order meanings, whether conference papers or journal articles carry more weight, how preprints and \"under review\" work is listed, and whether teaching or funding comes first. They also differ by country and post type (research-intensive faculty, teaching-focused, postdoc, fellowship, industry research).\n\nField and system: {{field}}\n\n<academic_record>\n{{academic_record}}\n</academic_record>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Conventions: state the conventions you will apply for this field, career stage and country, as general norms the candidate should check against their department's or the posting's expectations: section order, citation style, author-order notes, and whether to include a research statement summary.\n2. Build the CV with the sections that apply, in an order suited to the career stage and post:\n   - Contact details (placeholders only) and current position.\n   - Education: degree, institution, year; thesis title and supervisors for the doctorate.\n   - Academic appointments, reverse chronological.\n   - Research interests: one or two lines.\n   - Publications, split by type and status: peer-reviewed journal articles, conference proceedings, books and chapters, preprints, under review, in preparation (only with working titles and only if the field accepts it). Full citations in one consistent style, the candidate's name in bold, student or mentee co-authors marked if that is a convention, and a note explaining author order if the field needs it.\n   - Grants, fellowships and awards: funder, title, role (PI, co-investigator), amount if given, dates.\n   - Presentations: invited talks separated from contributed talks and posters.\n   - Teaching: courses with role (instructor of record, teaching assistant), level and enrolment if given; teaching development.\n   - Supervision and mentoring.\n   - Service: reviewing, committees, organising, outreach.\n   - Skills, languages, memberships, and references (or \"available on request\", per local norms).\n3. Check consistency: dates, citation format, name spelling, ordering within sections. List any inconsistencies you found in the input.\n4. Gaps and checks: missing information marked [X], items whose status is unclear (accepted or in press?), and anything that could be questioned.\n5. Tailoring: if a post was described, what to move up, expand or shorten for it, and what the cover letter or research statement should carry instead of the CV.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent publications, citations, DOIs, grant amounts, journal names, co-authors, dates or awards. Reproduce only what the record provides; where a citation is incomplete, mark the missing element as [X].\n- Do not upgrade the status of work: \"submitted\" is not \"under review\", and \"under review\" is not \"accepted\".\n- Do not add metrics such as impact factors or citation counts unless the candidate provided them and the field expects them.\n- Keep the visual format plain (headings, reverse chronological lists) so it converts cleanly to the template the institution requires.\n- If the field is unspecified, use broadly neutral conventions and ask for the field and country.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Conventions applied\n## CV\nThe complete CV in Markdown.\n## Gaps and checks\n## Tailoring for this post\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-resume",
          "write-cover-letter"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "resume-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ebc09b4b38228202270b8d123a2258ce6fb02e983ffb1899beee7433455de97c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-acceptance-criteria",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write acceptance criteria",
      "description": "Writes testable acceptance criteria for a user story or ticket, covering the main path, alternatives, validation, boundaries, permissions and empty states. Use before a story enters development.",
      "category": "product",
      "tags": [
        "gherkin",
        "bdd",
        "given-when-then",
        "definition-of-done"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
