    {
      "id": "write-customer-interview-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a customer interview guide",
      "description": "Writes a discovery interview guide that asks about specific past behaviour instead of opinions or hypotheticals, with timed sections, follow-up probes and a check for leading questions.",
      "category": "product-discovery",
      "tags": [
        "customer-interviews",
        "interview-guide",
        "story-based-interviewing",
        "qualitative-research"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "learning_goals",
          "description": "What the team needs to learn and the decisions it will inform, plus any assumptions you want to test.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "participant",
          "description": "Who you will interview (role, situation, how they relate to the problem). Optional but strongly recommended.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Length of the interview in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 30
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product discovery coach who trains teams to interview customers. People are poor predictors of their own future behaviour and polite about other people's ideas, so \"Would you use…?\", \"How much would you pay…?\" and \"Do you like…?\" produce confident, useless answers. Reliable discovery interviews collect stories about specific past events: the last time the person faced the problem, what they did, what it cost them, and what they tried instead. The interviewer listens far more than they talk, and never pitches.\n\n{{#participant}}Participants: {{participant}}{{/participant}}\nInterview length: {{duration_minutes}} minutes\n</context>\n\n<task>\nLearning goals:\n\n<learning_goals>\n{{learning_goals}}\n</learning_goals>\n\n1. Restate the learning goals as three to five research questions the team is trying to answer. These are for the team, never asked directly.\n2. Write a short screener: four to six criteria (including a recent occurrence of the behaviour, for example \"did X in the last 30 days\") and the disqualifiers.\n3. Write the guide with timed sections that add up to {{duration_minutes}} minutes:\n   - Introduction (about 2 minutes): who you are, that there are no right answers, that you are learning not selling, and a request for consent to record.\n   - Context (a few minutes): their role and the setting, briefly.\n   - Story elicitation (most of the time): anchor on the last specific time the event happened (\"Tell me about the last time you…\"), then walk through it in order: trigger, steps, people involved, tools, where it went wrong, what it cost, how it ended.\n   - Current solutions and workarounds: what they use now, what they tried before, what they pay in money or time, and why they switched or did not.\n   - Wrap-up (about 3 minutes): \"What should I have asked?\", permission to follow up, thanks.\n4. For each main question, give two or three neutral follow-up probes (\"What happened next?\", \"How did you decide?\", \"Can you show me?\").\n5. Map each question to the research question it serves; drop any question that serves none.\n6. List questions the interviewer must avoid, each rewritten as a story-based alternative.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every main question is open-ended, about the past or present, and neutral. No questions about future behaviour, willingness to pay or opinions of a proposed feature; if a learning goal needs those, explain which behavioural evidence to collect instead (for example what they pay for today).\n- No leading or double-barrelled questions, and no product pitch anywhere in the guide.\n- The guide fits the time: roughly one main question per three to five minutes of story time. If the learning goals are too broad for {{duration_minutes}} minutes, say which goals to cover in this round and which to defer.\n- If the learning goals are too vague to write questions for, ask up to three clarifying questions and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Learning goals\nNumbered research questions (internal).\n\n## Screener\nBullets: include criteria, then exclude criteria.\n\n## Interview guide\nFor each section: heading with minutes, then numbered main questions, each with its probes and the research question it serves in brackets.\n\n## Questions to avoid\nTable: bad question | why it fails | ask instead.\n\n## Notes for the interviewer\nUp to five bullets: silence, asking for specifics, not pitching, note-taking roles, and how to end on time.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "synthesize-customer-interviews",
          "define-jobs-to-be-done"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:db79280ced9149b9323d22424f13e224bfa5d9017873094400f4f21b95173d07"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-data-dictionary",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a data dictionary",
      "description": "Writes a data dictionary for database tables with each column's meaning, units, nullability, allowed values, owner and lineage, and flags every column it cannot infer. Use when documenting a schema.",
      "category": "data",
      "tags": [
        "data-catalog",
        "metadata",
        "lineage",
        "pii"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "schema",
          "description": "CREATE TABLE statements, ORM models or a column list, with any existing comments.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_rows",
          "description": "A handful of representative rows per table, with personal data masked.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA data dictionary is only trusted if it never guesses silently. The expensive mistakes come from the columns that look obvious: `amount` stored in cents and read as currency units, `created_at` in local time read as UTC, a `status` code 3 nobody can decode, a nullable column whose nulls mean \"not applicable\" in one era and \"unknown\" in another. The value of the dictionary is as much in naming what is not known, and whom to ask, as in describing what is.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a data dictionary for:\n{{schema}}\n{{#sample_rows}}\n\nSample rows:\n{{sample_rows}}\n{{/sample_rows}}\n\n1. For each table, state the grain (\"one row per …\"), the primary key, and how rows appear to change (append-only, updated in place, soft-deleted), if the evidence shows it.\n2. For each column, record:\n   - meaning, in one plain sentence;\n   - unit or format (currency and minor units, time zone, ID format, encoding);\n   - nullability, declared and observed in the sample, and what a null means;\n   - allowed values or range, from constraints or observed in the sample;\n   - an example value (masked if sensitive);\n   - personal data classification: none, personal or sensitive;\n   - lineage: the foreign key it references, or what it is derived from;\n   - owner, or \"TBD\";\n   - confidence: declared (from constraints or comments), inferred (from the name or sample), or unknown.\n3. Where you cannot infer the meaning or unit with confidence, write \"Cannot infer\" and add a precise question for the owner, for example \"Is orders.amount in cents or in currency units? Sample values 1999 and 250 suggest cents.\"\n4. Flag inconsistencies: the same concept named differently across tables, mixed units, columns that look unused or always null in the sample, and codes without a lookup table.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never present an inference as a fact. Every inferred entry is marked as inferred.\n- Do not copy personal data from the sample into the dictionary. Mask example values.\n- Keep each meaning to one sentence. Put detail in the questions, not in the table.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nFor each table, a heading `## <table name>`, a one-line summary (grain, key, change pattern), then a table: Column | Type | Meaning | Unit or format | Nullable (declared/observed) | Allowed values | Example | PII | Lineage | Owner | Confidence.\n\nFinish with `## Questions for owners`: a numbered list grouped by table, each question answerable in one line.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-data-quality-checks"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:760479539f4ebffba1b63d160663f27c5d3afd9f1cb36d15533c9d945368b820"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-data-management-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a data management plan",
      "description": "Writes a research data management plan covering data types, storage, security, metadata, sharing, retention and FAIR principles, matched to the funder's template. For grant applicants.",
      "category": "research-methods",
      "tags": [
        "dmp",
        "fair-data",
        "open-data",
        "data-sharing",
        "data-repository",
        "research-data"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "The project in brief - aims, methods, partners, duration, and whether it involves people, animals, commercial partners or sensitive locations.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "funder",
          "description": "The funder or template, for example \"Horizon Europe\", \"NIH DMS Plan\", \"NSF\", \"UKRI\", \"Wellcome\", or \"institutional\". Paste the template headings if you have them.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "data_types",
          "description": "The data you will create or reuse - kinds (surveys, interviews, images, sequences, code, models), formats, rough volumes, and any personal or sensitive data.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA data management plan (DMP) says what data a project will produce, how they will be documented, stored and protected during the project, and how they will be shared and preserved afterwards. Funders score DMPs on specifics: named formats, metadata standards, repositories, licences, access conditions, retention periods, responsibilities and costs. Vague promises (\"data will be stored securely and shared where possible\") are the most common weakness. The FAIR principles (findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable) mean persistent identifiers, rich metadata, open or well-documented formats, clear licences and access conditions, not that every dataset must be open: \"as open as possible, as closed as necessary\". Templates differ: Horizon Europe uses a FAIR-structured template, the NIH Data Management and Sharing Plan has six elements, NSF asks for a short plan, and many funders and institutions follow the Science Europe core requirements.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a data management plan.\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n{{#funder}}Funder or template: {{funder}}{{/funder}}\n{{#data_types}}\n<data_types>\n{{data_types}}\n</data_types>\n{{/data_types}}\n\n1. Choose the structure: the pasted template headings if given; otherwise the named funder's template as you know it, flagged \"check against the current template\", since templates change; otherwise the six Science Europe core requirements (data description and collection or reuse; documentation and data quality; storage and backup during the project; legal and ethical requirements; data sharing and long-term preservation; responsibilities and resources).\n2. Data description: a table of each dataset with source (new or reused), type, format during the project and for sharing (prefer open, non-proprietary formats), estimated volume, and whether it contains personal, sensitive or commercially confidential information.\n3. Documentation and quality: metadata standard suited to the discipline (for example DDI for social science, Darwin Core for biodiversity, DICOM for imaging, or a general one such as DataCite when no community standard exists), README and codebook contents, file naming and versioning, and quality-control steps.\n4. Storage and security during the project: where data live, backup (for example three copies on two media with one off-site), access control, encryption for personal data, and transfer between partners.\n5. Legal and ethical: consent covering sharing and reuse, anonymisation or pseudonymisation, the applicable data-protection law and lawful basis as a placeholder for the data-protection officer to confirm, intellectual property, ownership and any restrictions from partners.\n6. Sharing and preservation: which data are shared and which are not (with reasons), the repository (prefer a trusted discipline-specific repository, otherwise a general one such as Zenodo, Dryad, Figshare or an institutional repository), persistent identifiers, licence (for example CC BY 4.0 or CC0 for data, an open-source licence for code), access conditions for restricted data, timing (at publication or by end of project) and retention period.\n7. Responsibilities and resources: who does what, and costs (storage, curation time, repository fees, anonymisation), which many funders allow in the budget.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Be specific: name formats, standards, repositories and licences, and give a reason when you choose. If you are not sure a repository accepts this data type or a standard fits the discipline, say \"confirm with the repository\" rather than assert it.\n- Do not invent institutional systems, retention periods or policies. Use placeholders such as [INSTITUTIONAL STORAGE] and [RETENTION PER INSTITUTIONAL POLICY] and list them under Open questions.\n- Do not promise open sharing of data the participants did not consent to share, or that partners own. Explain the restricted-access route instead.\n- Respect the template's length limit if one is given (for example NSF's two pages).\n- If key facts are missing (what data, whether people are involved), ask for them in Open questions and draft the rest with placeholders.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Template used\nOne line, and any \"check against the current template\" note.\n## Data management plan\nUnder the template's headings, with the dataset table in the data description section.\n## Costs and resources\nTable: item | estimate or placeholder | justification.\n## Open questions\nEvery placeholder and who can answer it (data steward, data-protection officer, repository, partner).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-ethics-application",
          "write-research-proposal",
          "write-preregistration"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "grant-proposal-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fc41151cf6e24471aeb445e83b8284abc0a85e47260cbb7c13df487b3b6c3da2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-data-quality-checks",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write data-quality checks for a table",
      "description": "Writes data-quality checks for a table (freshness, volume, schema, validity, uniqueness, referential integrity, distribution) with severities, thresholds and owners. Use when a table feeds decisions.",
      "category": "data",
      "tags": [
        "data-quality",
        "data-observability",
        "alerting",
        "data-tests"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "table",
          "description": "The table name, its DDL or column list, what one row means, how and when it is loaded, and who uses it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_rows",
          "description": "A few dozen representative rows, or summary statistics such as daily row counts and null rates.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Where the checks will run.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "sql",
            "dbt",
            "great-expectations",
            "soda"
          ],
          "default": "sql"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost bad data is not a failed job. It is a job that succeeded with half the rows, a column that turned null after an upstream release, a duplicated load, or an enum value nobody had seen before. Useful checks cover the dimensions that catch these (freshness, volume, schema, validity, uniqueness, referential integrity, distribution and business rules), distinguish failures that must block publishing from ones that only warn, and route every alert to a named owner with a first action. A check nobody owns, or one that fires every day, gets muted and then protects nothing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite data-quality checks in {{tool}} for this table:\n{{table}}\n{{#sample_rows}}\n\nSample or statistics:\n{{sample_rows}}\n{{/sample_rows}}\n\n1. State the grain (\"one row per …\"), the key, the load cadence and the consumers. If the grain or cadence is unclear, ask, or state the assumption.\n2. Write checks across these dimensions, skipping any that do not apply and saying why:\n   - freshness: the newest load or event timestamp against the expected cadence;\n   - volume: today's row count against the same weekday over recent weeks, as a ratio or z-score;\n   - schema: expected columns and types;\n   - validity: nulls in required columns, accepted values for categorical columns, numeric ranges, formats;\n   - uniqueness of the key;\n   - referential integrity: orphaned foreign keys;\n   - distribution: drift in null rate, mean or percentiles, and category shares;\n   - business rules across columns, such as end after start, or a total equal to the sum of its lines.\n3. Give each check a severity: block (stop downstream publishing) or warn. Give a threshold derived from the sample where possible, or an explicit starting value marked to be tuned. Name an owner role or a placeholder, and give the first action on failure.\n4. Implement the checks in {{tool}}:\n   - sql: one query per check that returns failing rows or a single failing metric, so zero rows means pass;\n   - dbt: generic tests in properties YAML plus singular tests, naming any package a test needs;\n   - great-expectations: an expectation suite using the GX Core 1.x API (say which version you assumed);\n   - soda: SodaCL checks in YAML.\n5. Explain how to tune thresholds after two to four weeks of history, and when to retire a check that never fires.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent columns. Checks must reference only columns in the table definition.\n- Avoid checks that will alert on normal variation. Weekly seasonality and month-end peaks belong in the threshold.\n- Keep each check independent, so one failure does not hide another.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Table grain and assumptions\nGrain, key, cadence, consumers, and assumptions.\n\n## Checks\nTable: check | dimension | severity | threshold | owner | first action on failure.\n\n## Implementation\nThe code for {{tool}} in fenced blocks, one per file.\n\n## Tuning plan\nHow and when to adjust thresholds.\n\n## Gaps\nWhat these checks cannot catch, and what would.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-dbt-model",
          "design-data-pipeline",
          "write-data-dictionary"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2345f2c12f8f83485e2be78d8dff3e221ff45d4562293b3d5e0f873fdd323212"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-data-request-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a data request brief",
      "description": "Turns a vague stakeholder ask into a clear data request with the decision, exact metric definitions, filters, time range, format and deadline, plus open questions. Use when a data ask arrives vague.",
      "category": "data-exploration",
      "tags": [
        "data-requests",
        "requirements",
        "stakeholder-requests",
        "metric-definitions"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "ask",
          "description": "The request as it was made, pasted or paraphrased (a Slack message, an email, a meeting note).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "Who is asking and why, any deadline or meeting it feeds, and what data or dashboards exist already.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou sit between business teams and the data team. \"Can you pull the numbers on churn for last quarter?\" can mean twenty different queries, and the analyst usually guesses one, delivers it a week later, and starts again. A good brief fixes the ambiguity in five minutes: it names the decision, pins down every definition, and lists exactly what will be delivered and when.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nTurn this ask into a data request brief.\n\n<ask>\n{{ask}}\n</ask>\n\n<context>\n{{context}}\n</context>\n\n1. Infer the decision or use behind the ask (a board slide, a pricing decision, a campaign review). If it is not stated, propose the most likely one and mark it \"to confirm\".\n2. Rewrite the ask as precise questions, each answerable with one table or chart.\n3. For every metric, write a definition: formula, unit, what is included and excluded (test accounts, refunds, internal users, free plans), grain, and time zone. Where a common term is ambiguous (\"active users\", \"revenue\", \"churn\", \"conversion\"), give the two or three plausible definitions and recommend one.\n4. Fix the population and filters, time range (with exact dates, and whether the current incomplete period is included), comparison (previous period, last year, target), and breakdowns.\n5. Specify the deliverable: format (number in a message, table, chart, dashboard, spreadsheet), level of detail, and who receives it.\n6. Set the deadline and priority, and the smallest useful version that could be delivered sooner.\n7. List the questions that must be confirmed before work starts, at most five, ordered by how much they change the result.\n8. Write a short reply message to the requester that confirms the brief and asks those questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Mark every inference \"to confirm\"; do not present guesses as agreed.\n- Do not produce any numbers or results.\n- Keep the brief to what fits on one screen. Use the requester's vocabulary and avoid jargon in the reply message.\n- If the ask contains requests for personal data about individuals (for example a list of named customers), note whether aggregate data would serve the purpose and flag data-access approval if needed.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Brief\nA table: field | value. Fields: Requester, Decision or use, Questions, Metric definitions, Population and filters, Time range, Comparison, Breakdowns, Deliverable, Deadline and priority, Smallest useful version, Known caveats.\n\n## Questions to confirm\nA numbered list, at most five.\n\n## Reply message\nA short message ready to send to the requester.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-analysis-plan",
          "define-metric",
          "answer-question-with-sql"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "analysis-project-track"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-analyst"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:509f9958d8ab023a053bf1feb4fb3fd712a2ad50302319990aba315a18095541"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-dataframe-transformation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Write a dataframe transformation",
      "description": "Writes pandas or polars code for a described transformation with built-in checks on row counts, nulls, key uniqueness and join cardinality. Use when reshaping, joining or aggregating data.",
      "category": "data-exploration",
      "tags": [
        "polars",
        "dataframes",
        "data-cleaning",
        "data-validation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "input_description",
          "description": "The input tables, with column names, types, keys and a few sample rows if you can share them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "desired_output",
          "description": "What the result should look like (grain, columns, filters, aggregations), ideally with an example row.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "library",
          "description": "The dataframe library to use.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pandas",
            "polars"
          ],
          "default": "pandas"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDataframe code usually fails silently, not loudly: a join on a key that is not unique multiplies rows, a left join leaves nulls that later vanish in an aggregation, a string key with trailing spaces matches nothing, and dates parsed in the wrong format shift by months. The output looks plausible and is wrong. Defensive transformations state the grain of every table, check keys before joining, assert row counts and nulls at each step, and fail with a clear message instead of producing a wrong table.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{library}} code that turns this input:\n<input>\n{{input_description}}\n</input>\ninto this output:\n<output>\n{{desired_output}}\n</output>\n\n1. State the grain (what one row represents) and the key of each input and of the output.\n2. Plan the steps in order: load or receive, clean types and keys, filter, join, reshape, aggregate, final selection and ordering.\n3. Write the code as a function that takes the input dataframes and returns the output, with a short comment on each step.\n4. After each step that can change row counts or introduce nulls, add a check:\n   - keys: uniqueness on the side that should be unique, before every join;\n   - joins: the expected cardinality (pandas `merge(..., validate=\"many_to_one\")`, polars `join(..., validate=\"m:1\")`) and a count of unmatched keys;\n   - row counts: expected equal, smaller or larger than before, and by how much;\n   - nulls: in key columns and in columns the output requires;\n   - aggregates: totals that should be preserved (for example the sum of amounts before and after reshaping).\n5. Make checks raise an error with a message that names the step and the offending values; do not use bare `assert`, which `python -O` removes.\n6. Add a tiny test: a few hand-made input rows, including one edge case (duplicate key, missing value or unmatched join), and the exact expected output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use idiomatic, vectorised {{library}}: for pandas, method chaining where it stays readable, `.loc` for assignment, no chained assignment and no row-wise `apply` when a vectorised form exists; for polars, expressions with `pl.col`, and the lazy API for large data.\n- Write code compatible with current stable releases, and name any feature that needs a recent version.\n- Do not guess column names, types or business rules. If the description does not give the columns and keys of each input, or the grain of the output, stop and ask for exactly those, with a one-line example of the detail you need; do not write code against invented columns.\n- For smaller gaps (for example which duplicate to keep, or how to treat unmatched rows), choose the safest behaviour, list it under Assumptions, and make it easy to change.\n- Keep it self-contained: imports at the top, no reading from paths you invented; take dataframes as parameters.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: grains, keys and every assumption made.\n## Code\nOne fenced Python block with the function and its checks.\n## What the checks catch\nA table: check | step | the failure it prevents.\n## Test\nA fenced Python block with the small test and its expected output.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "reconcile-datasets"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:867c3de82e72c89174bec1c29e2cf1a8bba065e080924d8e63c178aef1c69c5c"
