    {
      "id": "write-docker-compose",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a Docker Compose dev environment",
      "description": "Writes a Docker Compose local development setup that mirrors production dependencies, with health checks, named volumes, seed data, env files and a one-command start. Use when onboarding developers.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "compose-file",
        "local-development",
        "developer-onboarding",
        "health-checks"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "services",
          "description": "The application services and every dependency they need locally (databases, caches, queues, object storage, email, search, third-party APIs), with how each app service is built and run.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "stack",
          "description": "Languages and frameworks of the app services, and the package manager.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "production_notes",
          "description": "What production runs - engine versions, managed services, configuration that matters (extensions, auth, TLS) - so local can match it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA local environment earns its keep when a new developer can clone the repo, run one command and have a working app with realistic data in minutes, and when \"works on my machine\" bugs stop coming from version drift. Compose files usually fall short in the same ways: `latest` images that differ from production, apps that start before the database accepts connections, data lost on every restart, secrets committed in the file, ports exposed on every network interface, and no seed data, so everyone builds their own by hand.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a Docker Compose development environment for:\n{{services}}\n{{#stack}}\n\nStack: {{stack}}\n{{/stack}}\n{{#production_notes}}\n\nProduction: {{production_notes}}\n{{/production_notes}}\n\n1. If the repository is available, read the existing Dockerfiles, dependency manifests, environment variable usage and any current compose file first, and build on them.\n2. Pin every dependency image to the same major and minor version as production (for example `postgres:16.4`), never `latest`. Where production uses a managed service with no local equivalent, choose a compatible local stand-in and record the gap.\n3. Write `compose.yaml` following the current Compose Specification (no top-level `version:` key):\n   - App services built from the repo's Dockerfile, using a development target or stage if one exists, with the source bind-mounted for hot reload (or a `develop.watch` section), and dependency folders kept inside the container so host and container builds do not clash.\n   - A `healthcheck` on every dependency using its own readiness command (`pg_isready`, `redis-cli ping`, an HTTP health endpoint), and `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy` on the app services.\n   - Named volumes for all persistent data; no anonymous volumes for data that should survive a restart.\n   - Ports published on `127.0.0.1` only, with defaults that avoid common clashes and can be overridden from the env file.\n   - Optional services (admin UIs, observability, workers that are not always needed) behind `profiles`.\n4. Put configuration in an env file: write `.env.example` with every variable, safe local defaults and a comment per variable; the real `.env` stays git-ignored. Never put production credentials or real secrets anywhere.\n5. Provide seed data: database init scripts or a one-shot seed service that runs after the database is healthy (`condition: service_completed_successfully` for services that depend on it), is idempotent, and creates a few realistic, clearly fake records, including a known login for local use.\n6. Give the one-command start (`docker compose up --wait` or a `make dev` / script wrapper), plus reset, logs, shell and test commands.\n7. List every remaining difference from production and its consequence, and a short troubleshooting section (port in use, CPU architecture mismatches on ARM machines, stale volumes, file-watching on mounted folders).\n\nIf a service's build or start command is unknown and the repository is not available, ask for it rather than inventing one.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every image is pinned. Every dependency has a health check. Every data store has a named volume.\n- No secrets, tokens or real personal data in any file. Local passwords are obviously local (for example `localdev`).\n- Do not add services that were not asked for, except a local stand-in for a dependency that has none; say why each was added.\n- Keep it runnable on macOS, Linux and Windows with WSL; call out anything that is not.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets, only those that shaped the setup.\n\n## compose.yaml\nOne `yaml` code block, with short comments on non-obvious lines.\n\n## .env.example\nOne code block.\n\n## Seed data\nThe seed scripts or seed service, and what records they create.\n\n## Commands\nA table: task | command. Include start, stop, reset data, logs, shell, run tests.\n\n## Differences from production\nTable: area | production | local | consequence.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nShort bullets: symptom, then fix.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-dockerfile",
          "generate-realistic-seed-data",
          "write-onboarding-guide"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "devops-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4731fd5fe363c8552b748907fdf4e8923c55702534f5d3c2111185fa485a5e70"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-donor-appeal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a donor appeal",
      "description": "Writes a donor appeal letter or email built on one person's story, the specific impact of a gift and a clear ask with amounts, plus subject lines and a follow-up. For nonprofits and charities.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "donor-appeal",
        "fundraising-letter",
        "year-end-appeal",
        "storytelling",
        "gift-ask"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "cause",
          "description": "The organisation and the specific need this appeal funds - what the money will do, costs per unit of help (for example 30 pays for one night of shelter), deadline or match funding, and the audience (past donors, lapsed, new).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "One real person's or family's story, told with their consent - the situation, what your organisation did, and what changed. Note any details that must stay private.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "ask",
          "description": "The ask - suggested amounts, monthly or one-off, and how to give. Leave empty to have amounts proposed from the costs given.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Where the appeal goes. \"both\" writes the letter first, then an email adapted from it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "email",
            "letter",
            "both"
          ],
          "default": "email"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write fundraising appeals in the direct-response tradition. Appeals that raise money are about one identifiable person rather than statistics, make the donor the hero (\"your gift\" rather than \"our programme\"), connect a specific amount to a specific result, give a reason to act now, and ask clearly more than once. They read warmly and simply, and they respect the dignity and privacy of the person whose story is told.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the appeal.\n\n<cause>\n{{cause}}\n</cause>\n\n<story>\n{{story}}\n</story>\n{{#ask}}\n<ask>\n{{ask}}\n</ask>\n{{/ask}}\n\nFormat: {{format}}.\n\n1. Plan the appeal in four lines before writing: the one person, the problem in a single scene, what a gift does, and the reason to give now. Fit the opening to the audience named in the cause: thank past donors for what they already made possible, tell lapsed donors they were missed, and introduce the organisation in one line to new prospects.\n2. Write the appeal for the chosen format (a printed letter runs about 400-600 words, an email 200-300 words with a single donate link or button):\n   - Open with the person in a specific moment, not with the organisation.\n   - Show the problem through their experience, with one or two concrete details from the story.\n   - Bring the reader in: what their gift makes possible, linking each suggested amount to a tangible result using the costs given.\n   - Give the urgency honestly (a deadline, a match, a season, a waiting list), only if it is in the input.\n   - Ask clearly, at least twice, with the amounts and how to give.\n   - Close with the outcome for the person and thanks, signed by a named person. Add a P.S. that restates the ask or the match.\n3. Subject lines or envelope teaser: three subject lines and a preview line for email; an envelope teaser for a letter; both when the format is both.\n4. Reply device: for a letter, a tear-off response form with the gift amounts, a monthly option, payment methods and a consent tick box for future contact; for an email, the landing-page ask block (amounts with their results, monthly toggle, one button).\n5. Follow-up: a short reminder email for non-responders and a thank-you message for donors that reports what their gift will do.\n6. Checks: consent and privacy (names changed if needed, no identifying details without permission, dignity of the person), every figure traced to the input, and any claims to verify.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Never invent stories, quotes, statistics, matches or deadlines; mark missing facts as [NEEDED: …].\n- Respect the person in the story: no pity language, no graphic detail for effect, and private details stay out. If consent is not mentioned, flag it in Checks.\n- Write at a reading level most adults find easy: short sentences and paragraphs, everyday words, \"you\" more than \"we\".\n- If no ask is given, propose three amounts based on the stated costs plus a monthly option, and say they are suggestions.\n- Gift-aid, tax-deductibility and fundraising regulations vary by country; mention them only as items to check.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Appeal\nThe plan in four lines, then the full letter or email.\n## Subject lines or envelope teaser\n## Reply device\n## Follow-up\nReminder and thank-you.\n## Checks\nChecklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-grant-application",
          "write-crowdfunding-campaign"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "grant-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c4ee2dd8c2b60c65b19f7be7a499bece99599fe5286f2c327733b810dca21957"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-e2e-test",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a resilient end-to-end test",
      "description": "Writes an end-to-end browser test for a user flow with role-based locators, auto-waiting assertions and isolated test data, never fixed sleeps. Use when adding UI coverage for a critical path.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "e2e",
        "playwright",
        "cypress",
        "selenium",
        "flaky-tests",
        "locators"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to cover, step by step, plus the URL, routes or page source if you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "End-to-end framework to write the test for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "playwright",
            "cypress",
            "selenium"
          ],
          "default": "playwright"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEnd-to-end tests are the most expensive tests to keep green. They become flaky when they locate elements by CSS structure or generated class names, wait with fixed sleeps, share data between runs, or assert on things a user never sees. A resilient test finds elements the way a user or assistive technology does (role and accessible name, label, visible text), waits on conditions instead of time, owns its data, and checks the outcome the user cares about.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{tool}} test for this flow:\n{{flow}}\n\n1. Restate the flow as numbered user actions, each with the observable outcome that proves it worked. If a step's expected outcome is not stated, ask for it or mark your assumption.\n2. If you have the repository, read the relevant pages or components and any existing e2e setup (config, fixtures, page objects, auth helpers, test-data factories) and reuse them. Match the existing style. If the project already uses a different end-to-end framework than {{tool}}, say so and ask which to use before writing.\n3. Locators, in this order of preference:\n   - Playwright: `getByRole` with name, then `getByLabel`, `getByPlaceholder`, `getByText`, then `getByTestId` as a last resort.\n   - Cypress: Testing Library queries (`findByRole`, `findByLabelText`) if the project has them, otherwise `cy.contains` scoped to a container, then `data-testid`/`data-cy`.\n   - Selenium: accessible attributes, labels and visible text via stable XPath or CSS on `data-testid`; never absolute XPath.\n   Never use generated class names, nth-child chains or DOM position.\n4. Waiting: use auto-retrying, web-first assertions (Playwright `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`/`toHaveText()`, Cypress `should`, Selenium `WebDriverWait` with expected conditions). Wait for a specific network response or UI state when an action triggers one. No `waitForTimeout`, `cy.wait(<ms>)` or `Thread.sleep`.\n5. Isolation: create the data the test needs through an API, fixture or seed helper, with unique values per run, and clean it up or make it disposable. Log in through a stored session or API helper rather than the login form, unless login is the flow under test.\n6. Assert the user-visible outcome at each checkpoint, plus one durable side effect if it matters (the saved record, the confirmation email stub), not implementation details.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent selectors, routes or accessible names you have not seen. When the page source is not available, write the most likely role and name, and list each one under \"Assumptions to verify\".\n- One flow per test. Keep the test independent of test order.\n- If a step depends on a third-party service (payments, email, maps), stub it at the network layer and say so.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Test plan\nNumbered steps: action, then expected outcome.\n## Test\nThe complete test file in one code block, including setup and teardown helpers it needs.\n## Assumptions to verify\nBullets: each selector, route or data assumption you could not confirm. Or \"None\".\n## How to run\nThe command to run this one test headed and headless, and how to see the trace or screenshots on failure.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer",
          "frontend-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cf4b61fbd6fdab00db9e2eec2ac0499043fb631adabb878150350e553b162ea0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-elevator-pitch",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an elevator pitch",
      "description": "Writes 10-second, 30-second and 2-minute pitches for different listeners, each with a hook, problem, solution, proof and ask. Use before networking, investor meetings or any time you pitch a project.",
      "category": "entrepreneurship",
      "tags": [
        "elevator-pitch",
        "one-liner",
        "storytelling",
        "networking",
        "spoken-delivery"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What you are pitching - the problem, who has it, what you do about it, why you, traction or proof with numbers, and what you want from listeners.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audiences",
          "description": "Who you will pitch to and what each wants (for example \"angel investors, potential customers at a trade fair, a possible co-founder\"). Leave empty for a general listener, an investor and a customer.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write pitches meant to be spoken, not read. A good pitch makes the listener understand the problem in one breath, believe the solution because of one concrete proof point, and know exactly what is being asked of them. Different listeners care about different things: an investor wants the size of the opportunity and traction, a customer wants their problem solved, a recruit wants the mission and the team. The facts stay the same; the order and emphasis change.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite pitches for this:\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n{{#audiences}}\n<audiences>\n{{audiences}}\n</audiences>\n{{/audiences}}\n\n1. Core message: one sentence that a listener could repeat to someone else afterwards. Test it: no jargon, a specific customer, a specific outcome.\n2. 10-second pitch: the core message as a natural answer to \"What do you do?\", in at most 30 spoken words.\n3. 30-second pitch for each audience (about 75 words), in this order: a hook (a striking fact from the input, a question, or a short customer moment), the problem in the listener's terms, the solution and what makes it different, one proof point (traction, a result, a credential), and a specific ask suited to that listener (a meeting, an introduction, a trial, feedback).\n4. 2-minute pitch for the most important audience (about 280 words): the same arc with a short customer story, why now, the business model in a sentence, the team's edge, and the ask.\n5. Delivery notes: where to pause, the one number to emphasise, how to handle the most likely follow-up question for each audience, and a shorter fallback if interrupted.\n6. Gaps: proof points or facts that would make the pitch stronger and are missing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Written for speech: short sentences, contractions, words people say aloud. No buzzwords such as \"revolutionary\", \"disruptive\", \"AI-powered platform\" unless explained in plain words.\n- Use only facts from the input. Never invent traction, customers, market sizes or awards; mark missing proof as [NEEDED: …] and list it under Gaps.\n- Stay within the word counts; state each pitch's word count.\n- If audiences are empty, write for a general listener, an investor and a potential customer.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Core message\n## 10-second pitch\n## 30-second pitches\nOne subsection per audience, with the word count.\n## 2-minute pitch\nWith the word count.\n## Delivery notes\n## Gaps\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak 10-second pitch: \"We're an AI-driven platform revolutionising the logistics space.\"\nStrong 10-second pitch: \"We help small bakeries stop throwing away a fifth of their bread by predicting tomorrow's orders from today's sales.\"\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-pitch-deck-outline",
          "prepare-investor-qa",
          "build-investor-pipeline"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c98236653c50da9de1c7005c88a21dc94d1fdb58c740c2d60186c20969560f83"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-email-sequence",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an email sequence",
      "description": "Writes an onboarding or nurture email sequence with one job per email, send timing and triggers, exit conditions, subject lines and full copy. Use for automated lifecycle email.",
      "category": "email-marketing",
      "tags": [
        "onboarding-emails",
        "nurture-sequence",
        "lifecycle-marketing",
        "activation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product is, who signs up, the key first actions that make users successful, the plans or offer, and the brand voice. Paste notes or an existing email for tone.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sequence_goal",
          "description": "The single outcome the sequence drives and who receives it (for example \"new trial users complete their first project and convert to paid by day 14\", \"webinar leads book a demo\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "emails",
          "description": "Number of emails in the sequence.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a lifecycle marketer who builds automated email sequences. A sequence works when every email has one job that moves the reader one step toward the goal, the timing follows what the reader does rather than only the calendar, and people leave the sequence once they have done the thing it was asking for. Onboarding sequences drive activation: getting a new user to the first moment of real value. Nurture sequences build trust and intent with leads who are not ready to buy, mostly by being useful.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{emails}}-email sequence.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\nSequence goal: {{sequence_goal}}\n\n1. Work out the logic first. Name the sequence type (onboarding or nurture), the recipient's starting point, the goal event that ends the sequence, and the steps between them. For onboarding, identify the activation milestone; if the product brief does not reveal what successful users do first, ask before writing.\n2. Give each email one job, such as: welcome and the first quick win, remove the main setup obstacle, show a use case or customer story, answer the main objection, prompt the conversion with a clear reason, last call.\n3. Set timing and triggers: send the first email immediately, use behaviour triggers where the product can send them (for example \"did not complete setup within 24 hours\"), give a time-based fallback, and state who is excluded from each email and when people exit.\n4. Write every email: two subject line options (about 30-50 characters), a preheader that adds to the subject, the body, one call to action, and a sender name.\n5. Define how to measure the sequence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One call to action per email; a secondary text link is allowed only if it serves the same action.\n- Short emails: about 50-150 words for onboarding, up to about 250 for nurture content. Plain and personal beats heavily designed for most sequences.\n- Personalisation tokens such as {first_name} always have a fallback, written as {first_name|there}.\n- No invented features, discounts, customer stories or numbers. Use [placeholders] where a story or number belongs.\n- No fake urgency, no misleading \"Re:\" or \"Fwd:\" subjects, no guilt-tripping.\n- Include in the notes that marketing emails need consent where required, a working unsubscribe link and the sender's postal address; transactional onboarding messages still need to be clearly about the account.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Sequence logic\nType, starting point, goal event, exit rules.\n\n## Sequence map\nA table: # | Trigger and timing | Job | Call to action | Skip or exit if.\n\n## Emails\nFor each email: Subject A, Subject B, Preheader, Sender, Body, Call to action.\n\n## Measurement\nThe goal metric for the whole sequence (for example activation or conversion rate against a holdout), and the per-email metric (clicks and the goal action, not opens, which are inflated by mail privacy features).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-promo-email",
          "write-landing-page-copy"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f95fa280c18eb50ed46839f6fd2b5c260a54555b2488fa94fa47df978a546fcd"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-emcee-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an emcee script",
      "description": "Writes an emcee script for an event with welcome, housekeeping, speaker introductions, transitions, ready-made filler for delays and a close, timed against the run of show.",
      "category": "public-speaking",
      "tags": [
        "emcee",
        "master-of-ceremonies",
        "event-hosting",
        "speaker-introductions",
        "run-of-show"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "event",
          "description": "The event (conference, gala, awards night, company offsite, wedding reception, school event), audience, venue, tone, the host organisation, and the emcee's own name and connection to it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "run_of_show",
          "description": "The schedule with times and each segment, speakers with their names, roles and talk titles, sponsors to thank, breaks, meals, awards and housekeeping facts (exits, Wi-Fi, toilets, photography policy, hashtags).",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn emcee is the event's glue, not its star. The job is to welcome people warmly, give them the information they need, introduce each speaker so the audience is ready to listen, move smoothly from one segment to the next, keep time, and cover the gaps when something runs late or breaks. Weak emcee scripts read long speaker bios aloud, repeat the same \"Without further ado\" at every handover, forget housekeeping until someone asks, and have nothing ready when a speaker is not on stage yet. Good ones are short, warm, specific to the event, and built to be spoken.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an emcee script for this event.\n\n<event>\n{{event}}\n</event>\n{{#run_of_show}}\n<run_of_show>\n{{run_of_show}}\n</run_of_show>\n{{/run_of_show}}\n\n1. If there is no run of show, or it lacks the speakers and timings, write the opening and a template for introductions and transitions, and ask for the schedule. Do not invent speakers or times.\n2. Write the opening (1 to 2 minutes): a warm welcome tied to the event's purpose, the emcee's one-line introduction, any acknowledgement the event requires (hosts, sponsors, traditional owners or a land acknowledgement if the event provides one), and what the audience can look forward to.\n3. Write housekeeping in under a minute, using only facts given: exits, toilets, Wi-Fi, phones, photography or recording policy, accessibility, schedule changes, hashtags. Mark gaps `[NEEDED: …]`.\n4. For each speaker or segment, write an introduction of 30 to 60 seconds: why this person and topic matter to this audience, two or three credentials or a specific detail, and the talk title, ending with the speaker's name as the cue for applause. Write a short thank-you and bridge after each, referencing something from the segment where the emcee can fill it in live (`[callback: one line from their talk]`).\n5. Write transitions into breaks, meals, awards and the return from them, with the time people need to be back.\n6. Write the close: thanks to speakers, organisers, sponsors and volunteers, practical next steps (reception, feedback survey, travel), and a warm send-off.\n7. Build the delay and filler kit: lines for a late speaker, technical failure, an early-finishing segment, a fire alarm or emergency announcement (point to the venue's procedure and staff, never improvise safety instructions), and two or three light audience interactions suitable for the tone.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only names, titles, facts and times provided. Check pronunciation: add a `[pronunciation?]` marker after any name the emcee should confirm.\n- Vary handover phrases; avoid \"without further ado\", \"needs no introduction\" and reading CVs aloud.\n- Humour, if the tone allows, is gentle and never at a speaker's or attendee's expense.\n- Keep spoken lines short and easy to say; put stage directions and cues in brackets.\n- Timings for emcee segments must fit the run of show; flag any segment where the schedule leaves no time for the emcee.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Script\nIn running order: time, segment heading, spoken lines with [cues].\n## Delay and filler kit\nSituation | What to say.\n## Cue sheet\nA one-page table for the lectern: Time | Segment | Emcee says (first words) | Who is next | Notes.\n## Placeholders\nEvery `[NEEDED: …]`, `[callback: …]` and `[pronunciation?]`. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "moderate-panel",
          "write-speech",
          "write-professional-bio"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speechwriter",
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:926743eb0bb7fbcf5198a266803df341d1922a0ff197422e7987d23248d9b616"
