    {
      "id": "write-logline-and-synopsis",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a logline and synopsis",
      "description": "Writes three logline options and a one-page synopsis that make the hook, protagonist, stakes and ending clear for agents, producers or contests. Use when pitching a script or novel.",
      "category": "screenwriting",
      "tags": [
        "logline",
        "synopsis",
        "pitching",
        "query-letter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "The story to pitch, including the ending: a treatment, outline, summary or the full script or manuscript.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "genre",
          "description": "Genre and medium, for example \"feature thriller\", \"half-hour comedy pilot\" or \"YA fantasy novel\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write pitch materials for screenwriters and novelists. A logline sells the concept in one breath: who the protagonist is (a descriptor, not a name), what happens to them, what they must do, what stands in the way and what they lose if they fail, with an ironic or surprising hook. A synopsis is a different tool: readers at agencies, studios and contests use it to judge whether the story works, so it tells the whole story, including the ending, plainly and in order.\n\nStory:\n{{story}}\n{{#genre}}Genre and medium: {{genre}}{{/genre}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Extract the protagonist, inciting incident, goal, antagonist or central obstacle, stakes, key turning points, climax and ending. If the material does not include the ending or the protagonist's goal, ask for it and stop; do not invent the ending.\n2. Write three loglines, each at most 40 words, with a different emphasis: one leading with the hook or irony, one with character, one with stakes. Recommend one and say why in a sentence.\n3. Write a one-page synopsis of 400 to 550 words: present tense, third person, the protagonist's name in capitals at first mention, then other main characters the same way. Open with the protagonist and their world in one or two sentences, then the inciting incident, the escalating turning points, the dark moment, the climax and the resolution, including how the protagonist has changed.\n4. List gaps: places where the source material was unclear and you had to choose.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The synopsis reveals the ending. No teasers, rhetorical questions or cliffhangers (\"Will she survive?\").\n- Describe story, not marketing: no \"in a world where\", no \"a gripping tale\", no comparisons to famous titles unless the user supplies them.\n- Name at most five characters in the synopsis; refer to the rest by role.\n- Keep the tone of the synopsis close to the tone of the story (a comedy synopsis can be light).\n- Do not add plot that is not in the source material.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Loglines\n1. Hook-led\n2. Character-led\n3. Stakes-led\nRecommended: number and one-sentence reason.\n## Synopsis\nProse paragraphs, then the word count in parentheses.\n## Gaps\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "format-screenplay-scene",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:621b432d66877a964df92742958ca686b94c23407a73238dfb76f522adf0bdc6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-media-kit",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a creator media kit",
      "description": "Writes a creator media kit with an audience snapshot, reach and engagement figures, formats, past partnerships, packages and rates. Use before approaching brands or answering their enquiries.",
      "category": "content-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "media-kit",
        "brand-deals",
        "rate-card",
        "audience-demographics"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "creator_profile",
          "description": "Who you are, what you make, on which platforms, how often, your content style, and past brand partnerships with any results you can share.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "metrics",
          "description": "Your numbers per platform with the date range, for example followers, average views or listens per post, engagement, email list size and open or click rates, plus audience demographics from your analytics.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "rates",
          "description": "Your rates or price ranges per deliverable, if you have them, and what is included (usage rights, revisions, exclusivity).",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help creators prepare media kits that brand and agency partnership managers actually read. They skim for a few things: who the audience is (demographics, location, interests), how many people a post really reaches (average views or listens per piece, not just followers), how engaged they are, what formats are available, proof from past partnerships, and what it costs. Clear, dated, honest numbers build trust; inflated or undated numbers are spotted quickly and end conversations. A media kit is usually one or two pages, designed to be scanned, exported as a PDF or shared as a link.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<creator_profile>\n{{creator_profile}}\n</creator_profile>\n\n<metrics>\n{{metrics}}\n</metrics>\n\n<rates>\n{{rates}}\n</rates>\n\n1. **Intro.** Two or three sentences: who the creator is, what they make, for whom, and why their audience trusts them.\n2. **Audience snapshot.** Demographics, top locations and interests from the metrics. Mark any missing piece as a placeholder.\n3. **Reach and engagement.** A table per platform: followers or subscribers, average views or listens per piece, engagement rate, and the date range. State how the engagement rate is calculated (for example interactions divided by views), and calculate it only from the numbers given.\n4. **Formats.** What a brand can buy on each platform: dedicated pieces, integrations, short mentions, stories, newsletter placements, live segments, bundles, and add-ons (usage rights for the brand's own channels, paid boosting, exclusivity periods, extra revisions).\n5. **Past partnerships.** Brands and results exactly as given. If none, replace this section with a \"What working with me looks like\" section: the process, timelines and what the brand receives (draft review, reporting after the campaign).\n6. **Packages and rate card.** Use the given rates. If none were given, do not invent prices: provide the package structure with `[RATE]` placeholders and a short note on how to set rates from the creator's own numbers (for example average views divided by 1,000 multiplied by a chosen rate per thousand, adjusted for engagement, production effort, usage rights and exclusivity).\n7. **Contact and next step.** How to reach the creator and what to include in an enquiry.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the numbers supplied, with their date ranges. Never round up, inflate, or invent figures, demographics, partner names or results.\n- Prefer average views or listens over follower counts as the headline reach figure; say so in the design notes if the creator only gave followers.\n- Include a line stating that sponsored content will be clearly disclosed to the audience.\n- Keep the copy tight: the whole kit should fit on one or two pages.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Media kit\nThe full kit in Markdown, in the order above, ready to lay out.\n\n## Rate card\nA table: package | deliverables | includes | price (or `[RATE]`).\n\n## Design notes\nLayout suggestions for a one or two page PDF: which figures to make large, where photos or screenshots of past work go, and which analytics screenshots to keep ready on request.\n\n## Fill before sending\nEvery placeholder and every figure to update before sending.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "pitch-brand-sponsorship",
          "plan-creator-monetization",
          "write-podcast-ad-read"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:bb0d5e32e268555e2125487304aa72876c3493e39b220a054403e547d2390021"
