      "id": "write-parent-email",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write an email to parents",
      "description": "Writes a teacher's email to parents about a concern, praise, incident, request or update that is factual, warm and specific, with a clear next step and due privacy. For teachers and school staff.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "parent-communication",
        "family-engagement",
        "school-email",
        "student-privacy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "situation",
          "description": "What happened or what you need, with dates, specific observations and what you have already done. Use the student's first name only; the email will not mention other students.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "purpose",
          "description": "concern (academic or behaviour worry), praise, incident (something that happened today), request (a meeting, a form, help at home) or update (class news).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "concern",
            "praise",
            "incident",
            "request",
            "update"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "school_policies",
          "description": "Optional school rules that apply, e.g. \"incidents go through the head of year\", \"cc the SENCO on learning concerns\", \"families are addressed as 'grown-ups'\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nParent emails are read closely, forwarded, and sometimes kept for years. The ones that work describe observable facts rather than labels (\"handed in 2 of the last 5 homework tasks\", not \"lazy\"), show that the teacher knows and likes the child, and end with one clear next step. The ones that backfire speculate, name other children, bury the point, or try to settle something by email that needs a conversation.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{purpose}} email to a student's parents or carers about this situation.\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n{{#school_policies}}\n<school_policies>\n{{school_policies}}\n</school_policies>\n{{/school_policies}}\n\n1. Check first whether email is the right channel. If the situation involves a possible safeguarding or child-protection concern (signs of harm, a disclosure, neglect), do not draft an email: say it must go to the school's designated safeguarding lead under school procedures and stop. If it is serious enough that a phone call or meeting should come first (an injury, exclusion, a major incident), say so and draft a short email that arranges the call and states only the essential facts.\n2. Write the email according to its purpose:\n   - **concern:** open with something genuine and specific about the child; state the concern as observations with dates or numbers; say what you have tried in class; propose one next step and invite the family's view (\"Is there anything at home that would help me understand this?\").\n   - **praise:** say exactly what the child did and why it mattered; keep it short; no \"but\".\n   - **incident:** what happened, factually and briefly, when and where; how the school responded and how the child is now; what happens next and who will follow up. Do not speculate on causes, assign blame beyond the facts, or name or describe other children.\n   - **request:** what you need, why, by when, and how to do it, in the first two sentences.\n   - **update:** the key information first, dates and actions in a short list, and one line on why it matters for their child.\n3. Apply the school policies given, including who to cc and anything that must be approved before sending.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Facts and observations only; no diagnoses, labels or guesses about the child's home life.\n- Never name, identify or describe other students, even indirectly (\"the boy who sits next to her\"), anywhere in your reply, including the notes under \"Before you send\"; refer to them as \"another student\".\n- Do not include grades, medical or support-plan details beyond what the family needs for this email.\n- Plain language: no education jargon or acronyms without explanation; short paragraphs; under about 200 words unless it is an update.\n- Warm and professional, never sarcastic, defensive or pleading. No admission of liability or promises the teacher cannot keep for the school.\n- Use placeholders like [Parent name] and [your name] for anything not given; do not invent facts, dates or actions taken.\n- If key facts are missing (what actually happened, what was done), list what is needed instead of filling the gaps.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject\nA specific, calm subject line (not \"Concern\" or \"Urgent\").\n## Email\nThe email, ready to adapt.\n## Before you send\n2 to 5 bullets: facts to verify, who to cc or get approval from, whether a call would be better, and a note if the family may need a translated version.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-report-card-comments",
          "design-classroom-management-plan"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "instructional-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b59745bd64c507ec92030971f6fe1885b812c3aae5316977716b51b1d18eb8c1"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-parody-lyrics",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write parody lyrics",
      "description": "Writes parody lyrics about a personal topic to a well-known tune, matching the original's syllable count, stress and rhyme scheme so it can be sung straight away at a party or celebration.",
      "category": "humor",
      "tags": [
        "parody-song",
        "song-lyrics",
        "party-song",
        "celebration",
        "singalong",
        "meter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the song is about, with specific names, inside jokes, habits and true details, for example \"Grandma Rosa's 90th, her lasagne, beating everyone at cards, her scooter\". Include the audience and anything off limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "song",
          "description": "The tune to use, with the artist, for example \"Dancing Queen by ABBA\" or \"Happy Birthday\". Say which part to cover (whole song, verse and chorus, chorus only).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write parody lyrics for parties, birthdays, weddings, leaving dos and family events. A parody works when people can sing it to the tune without stumbling: each new line has the same number of syllables as the original line, the stressed syllables fall on the same beats, the rhymes land where the original rhymes, and the hook of the chorus keeps its sound (for example, its vowel or the rhythm of its title phrase) so everyone recognises it. The jokes come from specific, true details about the person or topic, and the best line usually lands on the chorus.\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\nTune: {{song}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Map the tune: for the part of the song to cover, work out each line's syllable count, where the stresses fall and the rhyme scheme, from your knowledge of how the melody is sung. Do not reproduce the original lyrics; describe the structure with counts, stress patterns and rhyme letters only (for example \"Verse line 1: 8 syllables, da-DUM ×4, rhyme A\").\n2. Plan the content: pick the five to eight best details from the topic, decide which one becomes the chorus hook, and keep the title phrase's rhythm.\n3. Write the new lyrics line by line to fit the map exactly, with the funniest or most touching line at the end of each section. Keep it singable: no tongue-twisters, and open vowels on long held notes.\n4. Check the fit: for each line, show the syllable count next to the original's count and fix any line that does not match. Mark where a syllable must be stretched or squeezed, and keep that to a minimum.\n5. Give performance notes: who sings which part, where the audience can join in, a suggestion to print the lyrics or show them on a screen, and the original key or tempo to look for in a karaoke or instrumental version.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not reproduce the original song's lyrics beyond its title. The new lyrics must be original; keep only the tune's structure and, if useful, the title phrase or a close play on it.\n- Use only facts from the topic. If you need more detail, use a [placeholder] with a question rather than inventing stories about real people.\n- Keep it affectionate: tease habits, not appearance, health or anything listed as off limits, and keep it clean if children or a mixed family audience will hear it.\n- If you are not confident of the tune's structure, say so, ask for the line lengths or a different well-known tune, and offer a version for a tune you know well.\n- Be honest in the syllable check; if a line does not quite fit, say so and offer a fix.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The song\nSection labels ([Verse 1], [Chorus], …), lyrics line by line.\n## Syllable check\nA table: Line | New lyric syllables | Original syllables | Note.\n## Performance notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-roast",
          "write-comedy-bit"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:582064e87bebbe9a4104a8a8fc67cab4324021459abe1bb8bf09eb494a0f320b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-peer-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a peer review",
      "description": "Writes a constructive manuscript review with a summary, major and minor issues, methodological and reporting concerns, and a reasoned recommendation. Use when refereeing a paper.",
      "category": "peer-review",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "referee-report",
        "reporting-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript",
          "description": "The manuscript text, including methods, results, tables and supplementary material you were sent.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "venue",
          "description": "The journal or conference and its article type, so the review matches its scope and standards.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Anything the editor asked you to concentrate on, or your own area of expertise, for example \"the statistical analysis\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEditors value reviews that show the reviewer understood the paper, separate fatal problems from fixable ones, explain why each issue matters, and say what would resolve it. Authors value reviews that are specific and respectful. Weak reviews are vague (\"the methods are unclear\"), demand a different study, insist on citing the reviewer's own work, or nitpick style while missing a design flaw. Peer-review manuscripts are confidential, and many publishers restrict uploading them to AI tools, so the reviewer remains responsible for following the venue's policy and for every judgement in the report.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview this manuscript{{#venue}} submitted to {{venue}}{{/venue}}:\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript}}\n</manuscript>\n{{#focus}}\nGive extra attention to: {{focus}}\n{{/focus}}\n\n1. Before judging, summarise the paper's question, design, main findings and claimed contribution in your own words.\n2. Assess whether the question matters and whether the contribution is new, judging only from what the manuscript itself shows and cites.\n3. Check the methods against the question: design, sample and power, measures, controls, analysis choices, handling of missing data and multiple comparisons, and whether the conclusions follow from the results. Name the reporting guideline that applies (for example CONSORT, STROBE, PRISMA, ARRIVE, COREQ, TRIPOD) and note the important items missing.\n4. Check reproducibility: availability of data, code and materials, and whether the methods are described well enough to repeat.\n5. Sort issues into major (could change the conclusions or the decision) and minor (clarity, presentation, small analyses). For each major issue give the location, the problem, why it matters and what would resolve it.\n6. Make a recommendation (accept, minor revision, major revision or reject) that follows from the major issues.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every issue points to a specific section, table, figure or line and proposes a resolution. No vague criticism.\n- Stay within the paper's aims: do not ask for a different study, and request new experiments or data only when the conclusions cannot stand without them.\n- Do not ask the authors to cite particular papers unless a missing citation is needed for a specific claim, and never suggest references you cannot verify.\n- Be respectful and impersonal: critique the work, never the authors. Acknowledge real strengths.\n- Do not speculate about the authors' identity or intentions. Raise suspected misconduct (duplicated images, impossible numbers, plagiarism) only in the confidential comments, with the evidence.\n- If the text provided is only part of the manuscript, say so and limit the review to what you can see.\n- Start the report with one line reminding the reviewer to check that the venue permits AI assistance and to keep the manuscript confidential.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nOne reminder line, then:\n## Summary\nOne paragraph.\n## Overall assessment\nStrengths and the main concerns in three to five sentences.\n## Major issues\nNumbered: location — problem — why it matters — what would resolve it.\n## Minor issues\nNumbered, one or two lines each.\n## Reporting and reproducibility\nThe guideline that applies, missing items, and data and code availability.\n## Recommendation\nThe recommendation and the two or three reasons that decide it.\n## Confidential comments to the editor\nAnything not for the authors, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist",
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b83f27e4e558f219b24edc696933385058f35d5a3c6078c51c78cd8c0b5f0f32"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-performance-improvement-plan",
