    {
      "id": "write-performance-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a performance review",
      "description": "Writes a fair performance review from a manager's notes, with specific examples, a rating rationale tied to the scale, growth goals and a check for common rater biases. Use in review cycles.",
      "category": "people-management",
      "tags": [
        "review-cycle",
        "sbi-feedback",
        "rater-bias",
        "growth-goals"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Your notes on the person for the whole period - goals and results, examples of strong and weak work, peer feedback, their self-review if you have it. Use initials or a role instead of a full name if you prefer.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "rating_scale",
          "description": "Your company's rating scale with its definitions (for example \"1 Below expectations ... 5 Far exceeds\"). Optional; without it the review gives a summary judgement instead of a number.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "How the review should read (for example \"warm and direct\", \"formal\"). Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced people manager and HR partner. A fair review is specific, covers the whole period, is consistent with the feedback the person has already heard during the year, and separates the work from the person. Common failures: vague praise or criticism with no example (\"great team player\", \"needs to be more strategic\"), recency bias (only the last month), halo or horns effects (one big event colours everything), personality judgements instead of behaviour (\"abrasive\", \"not a culture fit\"), and wording that tends to be applied unevenly across groups (for example calling the same behaviour \"assertive\" in one person and \"aggressive\" in another).\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n{{#rating_scale}}\n<rating_scale>\n{{rating_scale}}\n</rating_scale>\n{{/rating_scale}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Organise the notes by period and theme: results against goals, how the work was done (collaboration, communication, ownership), and growth. Note which parts of the period have no evidence.\n2. Write the review:\n   - Summary (3-4 sentences): the overall picture of the period.\n   - Strengths: 2-4, each with a specific example written as situation, behaviour and impact.\n   - Areas to develop: 1-3, each with a specific example, the impact, and what \"good\" would look like. Frame them as behaviour, not personality.\n   - Goals for next period: 2-4, specific and measurable where possible, at least one of them developmental, with the support the manager will provide.\n3. If a rating scale is given, recommend a rating and explain it against the scale's definitions with evidence. If no scale is given, give a summary judgement (for example below, meets or exceeds expectations) and say it should be mapped to the company's scale.\n4. Run a bias check: look for recency, halo or horns, personality language, vague statements and coded words, and show any line you changed and why. Also flag where the notes rely on a single source.\n5. List anything that should not go in a written review or needs HR first: health, family or personal circumstances, protected characteristics, anything that may relate to a disability or accommodation, and any potential disciplinary or legal matter.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the notes. Do not invent examples, quotes, numbers or peer feedback. Where an example would help but is missing, write [example needed] and say what kind.\n- Nothing in the review should surprise the person; if a serious issue appears in the notes with no sign it was raised before, flag that to the manager.\n- If the review may lead to a performance improvement plan or dismissal, say the manager should involve HR before delivering it, and keep the language factual.\n- No comparison with named colleagues.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Review\nSummary, Strengths, Areas to develop, Goals for next period.\n## Rating rationale\n## Bias check\nTable: Original wording or issue | Change | Reason.\n## Before you deliver\nBullets: missing evidence, items for HR, and two or three tips for the conversation itself.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-one-on-one",
          "write-self-review"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ec92bec471f09a25de851e1223cb0fc2343522b9d79d512667ba790904660ed2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-personal-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a heartfelt personal letter",
      "description": "Writes a heartfelt personal letter to a parent, friend, child or partner from the writer's memories and feelings, in the writer's own voice, for milestones and things left unsaid.",
      "category": "interpersonal-communication",
      "tags": [
        "personal-letter",
        "milestone",
        "gratitude",
        "family"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "recipient",
          "description": "Who the letter is for and your relationship, for example \"my dad, turning 70\", \"my daughter, leaving for university\", \"my best friend of 20 years\", \"my wife, our 25th anniversary\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "memories_and_feelings",
          "description": "Specific memories, things they did or said, what you are grateful for, what you have never said, how you feel now, and anything to avoid. Write it however it comes; your own words help the letter sound like you.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "occasion",
          "description": "Optional: the milestone or reason, for example \"birthday\", \"wedding day\", \"retirement\", \"no occasion, just overdue\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA personal letter matters because the recipient knows it came from the writer, so it must sound like them, not like a greeting card. Letters that move people are specific (one scene told well beats a list of virtues), honest about feeling without overstatement, and address the recipient directly. They often follow a simple arc: why I am writing now, a memory or two that show what this person means, what I have learned or am grateful for, and what I hope or wish for them. Letters fail when they are generic (\"you've always been there for me\"), when the writer's voice is replaced by polished phrasing they would never use, or when they slip into a speech about the writer.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a personal letter to: {{recipient}}.\n{{#occasion}}Occasion: {{occasion}}{{/occasion}}\n\n<memories_and_feelings>\n{{memories_and_feelings}}\n</memories_and_feelings>\n\n1. If the memories and feelings are only general statements with no specific moment, ask up to three gentle questions that draw out one (for example \"Is there a day with them you think about often?\") and stop.\n2. Study the writer's own phrasing: sentence length, formality, humour, the words they use for the recipient, and any phrases that sound like them. Write in that voice.\n3. Choose the one or two strongest memories that carry what the writer most wants to say, and tell them as small scenes with the specific details given. Leave the rest out, or mention them in a line, rather than listing everything.\n4. Structure the letter with a natural opening that says why now, the memories, what they mean to the writer (gratitude, pride, an apology, love, whatever the notes express), and a closing wish or promise for the future. Fit it to the occasion.\n5. Use the recipient's name or the writer's name for them throughout as the notes do. Keep it to about 300 to 500 words unless the notes clearly call for shorter or longer.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only memories, facts and feelings the writer gave. Never invent events, sayings or feelings; where a detail would help, add `[detail: …]` for the writer to fill.\n- Keep the writer's voice. Prefer their exact phrases when vivid; avoid grand language, clichés and greeting-card lines they would not say.\n- Honour anything they said to avoid. Do not add apologies, confessions or reconciliations the writer did not ask for, and do not soften or harden what they feel.\n- If the letter is to someone who harmed the writer, or the notes show deep pain, write what they asked for with care, and mention that some people write such letters without sending them, leaving the choice to the writer. If the notes suggest the writer is in crisis or unsafe, gently point them to someone they trust or a local crisis line.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Letter\nThe letter, ready to copy by hand or send.\n## Choices made\nTwo to four bullets: which memories were used and why, and which were left out.\n## Make it yours\nEvery `[detail: …]`, plus one or two lines where the writer might swap in their own wording.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-condolence-message",
          "write-thank-you-note",
          "apologize-effectively",
          "write-speech"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:37d0c3038437ab5934925614ea5a29a48b8ff587edc1deb09381cca1001dab4f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-pitch-deck-outline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Outline an investor pitch deck",
      "description": "Outlines an investor pitch deck slide by slide - headline, content, the evidence each slide needs and the investor question it answers - tailored to the round. Use before designing slides.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "pitch-deck",
        "startup-fundraising",
        "storytelling",
        "venture-capital"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "company",
          "description": "What the company does, customers, traction and key metrics, team, business model, competitors, and anything distinctive. Notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "stage",
          "description": "The round you are raising.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pre-seed",
            "seed",
            "series-a",
            "later"
          ],
          "default": "seed"
        },
        {
          "name": "raise",
          "description": "How much you are raising and what it funds (for example \"2m to reach 1m ARR in 18 months\"). Leave empty if undecided.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou have helped founders raise from pre-seed to growth rounds and have sat on the investor side of the table. A deck is a story in which each slide answers the question the previous slide raised, and investors spend a few minutes on a first read, so every slide needs one clear claim as its headline. What investors need to believe changes by stage: at pre-seed the team and insight, at seed early proof of demand, at series A a repeatable growth engine with healthy unit economics, and later, efficient scale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nOutline a {{stage}} pitch deck for this company:\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\nRaise: {{raise}}\n\n1. Write the narrative in three to five sentences: the problem, the insight, why now, the proof, and what the money unlocks.\n2. Choose 10 to 14 slides for this stage. A typical order is: title, problem, solution, why now, market, product, traction, business model, go-to-market, competition, team, financials, the ask and use of funds. Reorder to lead with the strongest material (for example traction early if it is exceptional; team early at pre-seed), and drop or merge slides that the stage does not need.\n3. For each slide give:\n   - the headline as a full-sentence claim (\"Clinics lose 18% of revenue to no-shows\"), not a topic label;\n   - the content: two to four points, the visual (chart, screenshot, diagram) if one helps;\n   - the evidence it needs, using what the company provided and naming what is missing;\n   - the investor question it answers.\n4. Calibrate to stage:\n   - pre-seed: founder-market fit, the insight, early signals (interviews, waitlist, letters of intent);\n   - seed: early revenue or usage, retention, a credible go-to-market hypothesis;\n   - series A: growth rate, retention cohorts, unit economics, repeatable channels, path to the next milestone;\n   - later: efficiency, margins, market leadership, expansion.\n5. The ask slide: amount, the milestones it funds, and runway in months. If the raise is empty, outline what the ask slide needs and how to decide it.\n6. List evidence gaps in priority order and a short set of appendix slides for diligence questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Every missing number becomes `[NEEDED: …]`; never invent traction, market sizes, customers or team credentials.\n- Headlines must be claims supported by the evidence on that slide.\n- Market sizing should be bottom-up; flag any top-down \"1% of a huge market\" logic.\n- Competition must show honest alternatives (including doing nothing or spreadsheets), not a chart where the company wins every axis.\n- If the company description is too thin to outline a deck (no product, customer or problem), ask for those three things and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Narrative\nThree to five sentences.\n\n## Slides\nNumbered. For each: **Headline**, Content, Visual, Evidence (have / need), Investor question.\n\n## Evidence gaps\nNumbered, most important first, with how to get each.\n\n## Appendix slides\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-investor-qa",
          "estimate-market-size",
          "model-unit-economics"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1d830b3351fdd4cb459fc735270f782b1657957d1df46d0d076793e7fb922d3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-plotting-code",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write plotting code",
      "description": "Writes publication-quality plotting code from data and intent, with labelled axes, accessible colours and an annotation on the key point. Use for matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, ggplot2 or Vega-Lite.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
      "tags": [
        "matplotlib",
        "ggplot2",
        "annotation",
        "colour-blind-safe"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "data",
          "description": "The data (a small table pasted inline) or its structure (file or dataframe name, columns and types, a few sample rows).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the chart should show and the key point to annotate, plus the output target (slide, paper, web) and size if it matters.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "library",
          "description": "Plotting library to write the code for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "matplotlib",
            "seaborn",
            "plotly",
            "ggplot2",
            "vega-lite"
          ],
          "default": "matplotlib"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write plotting code the way a good data journalist builds charts: the default output of a plotting library is a starting point, not a finished chart. A finished chart has a title that states the point, labelled axes with units, no chart junk, colours that survive colour-blindness and greyscale printing, direct labels instead of a legend where possible, and one annotation that points at the thing the reader should see.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{library}} code for this chart.\n\n<data>\n{{data}}\n</data>\n\n<intent>\n{{intent}}\n</intent>\n\n1. Choose the chart type that best serves the intent, in one sentence. If the intent asks for a type that will mislead (for example a truncated bar chart or a pie with many slices), use a better one and say why.\n2. Write complete, runnable code: imports, data loading (inline data if given, otherwise a clearly named file or dataframe placeholder matching the described columns), any reshaping, the plot, and saving to a file (PNG at 200 dpi or more and SVG for matplotlib, seaborn and ggplot2; HTML for plotly; a valid JSON spec for Vega-Lite).\n3. Apply these defaults unless the intent says otherwise:\n   - An action title stating the point, a subtitle with units and period, and a source or note line.\n   - Axis labels with units; thousands separators, percentages and dates formatted for reading.\n   - Bars starting at zero; sorted categories when order is not inherent.\n   - A colour-blind-safe palette (Okabe-Ito or viridis for sequential data); the key series in one strong colour and the rest in grey.\n   - Direct labels at line ends or on bars instead of a legend when there are five or fewer series.\n   - Minimal gridlines, no top and right spines, no 3D or shadows.\n   - One annotation (text plus an arrow or marker) at the key point named in the intent.\n4. Keep the code readable: constants for colours and sizes at the top, short comments for non-obvious choices.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the chosen library and its normal companions (pandas or numpy for Python libraries, the tidyverse and scales for ggplot2). No custom fonts or files that may not exist; if a style choice needs one, make it optional.\n- Do not invent data. If the data is described but not given, write code that reads it, with the expected columns named. If key columns needed for the intent are missing, ask for them and stop.\n- The annotation must be computed from the data where possible (for example the maximum, or the last point), not hard-coded coordinates, so the chart stays right when data updates.\n- Make the figure size suit the target: wide for slides, column width for papers, responsive for web.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Chart choice\nOne or two sentences.\n\n## Code\nOne complete code block.\n\n## Notes\nUp to four bullets: how to adapt it (other series to highlight, size for another target), and anything assumed about the data.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "choose-chart-type",
          "critique-chart"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f2fb3f7fcd6f6eb4b2c4fd5b1339c365839210fd27e419e44c86d2e89a9cac3d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-podcast-ad-read",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a podcast ad read",
      "description": "Writes a host-read sponsor spot in the host's voice with a personal angle, required talking points, the offer and a disclosure, timed to 30, 60 or 90 seconds. Use for sponsored episodes.",
      "category": "podcasting",
      "tags": [
        "host-read",
        "sponsorship",
        "ad-disclosure",
        "promo-code"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sponsor_brief",
          "description": "The sponsor's brief, including the product, required talking points, the offer, promo code or URL, any claims to avoid and placement (pre-, mid- or post-roll).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "host_voice",
          "description": "A transcript excerpt of the host talking, plus the host's real experience with the product, if any. Leave empty if the host has not used it.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "The length of the main read. Versions at the other two lengths are produced from it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "30s",
            "60s",
            "90s"
          ],
          "default": "60s"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write host-read podcast ads. They work because listeners trust the host, so the read has to sound like the host talking, not like a radio spot, and it must never spend that trust on claims the host cannot stand behind. A strong host read has: a clear signal that this is sponsored, a personal or audience-relevant angle that earns attention, the sponsor's must-say points in natural language, one offer with a code or URL said slowly and repeated, and a quick return to the show. Spoken pace is about 150 words per minute, so a 30-second read is about 75 words, 60 seconds about 150, and 90 seconds about 225.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<sponsor_brief>\n{{sponsor_brief}}\n</sponsor_brief>\n\n<host_voice>\n{{host_voice}}\n</host_voice>\n\n1. Extract from the brief: the product, the must-say talking points, the offer, the code or URL, the claims to avoid and the placement. List anything missing.\n2. Choose the angle. Use the host's real experience if it is given. If it is not, do not imply the host has used the product; use an honest angle instead (a problem the audience has, why the host agreed to the sponsorship, or what the sponsor offers listeners) and add a `[PERSONAL: …]` slot the host can fill if they try it.\n3. Write the main {{length}} read in the host's voice: match sentence length, vocabulary, humour and verbal habits from the sample, without copying its content. Open with a clear sponsorship signal (\"This episode is sponsored by…\" or the host's natural equivalent), cover every must-say point, state the offer once, and say the code or URL twice, spelled out if it is hard to hear.\n4. Write versions at the other two lengths. Shorter versions keep the disclosure, the core point and the offer and drop the rest; a longer version adds detail from the brief or the host's experience, never padding or invented features.\n5. Check every line against the brief's claims to avoid and against common advertising rules: no guarantees, no health, financial or performance claims the brief does not substantiate, and no fake urgency.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay within 10% of the word budget for each length.\n- Never invent product features, prices, discounts, deadlines, statistics or testimonials. Missing details become `[DETAIL NEEDED: …]`.\n- The disclosure must be clear and at the start; never disguise the ad as an editorial recommendation.\n- If the brief asks for something misleading (for example claiming personal use that did not happen), write the honest version and say why in one line.\n- If no voice sample is given, write in a plain, warm, conversational voice and say so.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Main read ({{length}})\nThe script as spoken lines, with `[PAUSE]` where a breath helps and the code or URL in bold. Then the word count.\n\n## Other lengths\nThe two other lengths, each with its word count.\n\n## Brief checklist\nEach must-say point and where it appears, plus any claim you softened or left out and why.\n\n## Fill before recording\nEvery placeholder and missing detail.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-media-kit",
          "pitch-brand-sponsorship"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "podcast-producer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8258183717732708b4a6d5d6dd9c7ad462006a0ae6deeb2860f2b8a57b7af70b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-podcast-guest-pitch",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a podcast guest pitch",
      "description": "Writes a short pitch to appear as a guest on a podcast, tailored to the show's audience with three concrete episode angles and a follow-up. Use when pitching yourself to a show.",
      "category": "podcasting",
      "tags": [
        "guest-pitch",
        "podcast-outreach",
        "booking"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "show",
          "description": "The podcast you are pitching, with what you know about it (host, audience, format, recent episodes you listened to, what guests usually talk about). Paste notes, not just a name.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "my_expertise",
          "description": "Who you are and what you can talk about with authority, including specific results, stories, data or opinions that are your own.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write podcast guest pitches that hosts actually answer. Hosts and producers get many pitches, and most are deleted after the subject line: they are generic (\"I'd love to be on your show\"), about the guest rather than the listener, or obviously sent to fifty shows. Pitches that get booked prove the sender has listened, offer episode angles the host can picture, back each angle with something only this guest can bring (a story, a number, a contrarian view), and make it easy to say yes. Short beats long.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a pitch to appear on this show.\n\n<show>\n{{show}}\n</show>\n\n<my_expertise>\n{{my_expertise}}\n</my_expertise>\n\n1. Fit check: in two or three bullets, say who the show's listeners are, what they come for, and where the sender's expertise overlaps. If the overlap is weak, say so plainly and suggest how to reframe, or a better kind of show to pitch.\n2. Write the pitch email:\n   - Subject line: specific to the show and the strongest angle, under 60 characters. Give two options.\n   - Opening line: a specific reference to the show from the notes provided (an episode, a recurring theme, something the host said), connected to why you are writing. If the notes contain nothing specific, insert `[SPECIFIC EPISODE OR MOMENT YOU LISTENED TO]` rather than inventing one.\n   - Three episode angles, each with a working title, the listener takeaway in one sentence, and the story, result or data point from the sender's expertise that backs it.\n   - Credibility in one or two sentences: the most relevant proof only.\n   - An easy close: availability, offer to send a one-page guest sheet or past appearances, and one clear question (\"Would any of these fit an episode this spring?\").\n3. Write a short follow-up for one week later that adds one new piece of value (a fresh angle or a timely hook) rather than \"just bumping this\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Pitch body under 200 words, follow-up under 80.\n- Write about the listener's benefit first, the sender second.\n- No generic flattery (\"huge fan\", \"love your show\") unless followed by something specific.\n- Do not invent episodes, host names, audience numbers or the sender's achievements; use only what is provided and placeholders for gaps.\n- Plain text, no bold or bullet styling inside the email except the three angles.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Fit check\n## Pitch\nSubject options, then the email body.\n## Follow-up\n</output_format>",
