    {
      "id": "write-screen-reader-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a screen-reader test plan",
      "description": "Writes a manual screen-reader test script for a user flow on NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver or TalkBack, with keystrokes and expected announcements per step. Use before releasing a key flow.",
      "category": "accessibility",
      "tags": [
        "screen-reader",
        "manual-testing",
        "assistive-technology"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to test, step by step, with the screens, controls and any dynamic updates (loading, errors, toasts).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "screen_readers",
          "description": "Screen readers to cover. Ones that do not exist on the chosen platform are dropped, with a note.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack"
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where the flow runs.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTesters new to screen readers tend to Tab through a page and call it done. Real users navigate by headings, landmarks, form fields and lists. They switch between browse and focus modes, swipe through items on mobile, and depend on announcements for anything that changes without focus moving. Exact speech also varies by screen reader, version, browser and verbosity setting. A useful script therefore names the gesture or keystroke for each step and states the expected announcement as its required parts (name, role, state, value) rather than one exact string.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a manual screen-reader test script for this {{platform}} flow:\n\n{{flow}}\n\nScreen readers requested: {{screen_readers}}.\n\n1. Build the test matrix. Pair each screen reader with the browser or platform it is mainly used with: NVDA with Firefox or Chrome on Windows, JAWS with Chrome or Edge, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS with Safari or the app, TalkBack with Chrome or the app on Android. Drop any that do not run on {{platform}} and say so.\n2. Write the setup: the versions to record, default verbosity, the speech viewer or log to turn on (NVDA Speech Viewer, VoiceOver caption panel, TalkBack's developer setting for speech output), and resetting state between runs.\n3. Start with orientation checks before the flow: the page or screen title is announced, the headings outline makes sense (H key or the rotor), landmarks are present and labelled, and the language is announced correctly.\n4. For each step of the flow, write:\n   - the action in each screen reader's own terms: NVDA and JAWS keys (H, D or R for landmarks, F for form fields, Tab, Enter, Space, Insert+F7 or Insert+F6 lists), VoiceOver keys (VO+Right Arrow, VO+Space, the rotor) or gestures (swipe right, double-tap, the rotor), and TalkBack gestures (swipe right, double-tap, reading controls);\n   - the expected announcement as name, role, state and value, for example \"Email, edit text, required, invalid entry\";\n   - the dynamic behaviour to confirm, such as where focus lands after a dialog opens or closes, a live-region announcement for async results, errors announced and linked to their field, and a loading state that is announced and then cleared;\n   - the pass criterion and the WCAG success criterion it maps to.\n5. Add negative checks: every control is reachable with the screen reader's standard navigation, not only by mouse or by touch exploration, decorative images are silent, and hidden content is not read out.\n6. If the flow description leaves out what happens at a step (validation, a success message, a redirect), list it under Coverage gaps instead of inventing behaviour.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not claim an exact announcement string unless the flow specifies the label text. Expected speech is the components, in any order the screen reader uses.\n- Keystrokes must be real for the named screen reader. If unsure of one, say so rather than guess.\n- Keep each step to one action, so a failure points to one place.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Setup\n| Screen reader | Browser or app | Platform | Settings to record |\nThen the setup and reset steps.\n\n## Test script\nFor each step:\n| Step | Action (per screen reader) | Expected announcement | Also check | Pass criterion | WCAG SC |\nOrientation checks come first.\n\n## Defect template\nFields to fill for a failure: step, screen reader and version, browser, actual speech (copied from the log), expected, and severity.\n\n## Coverage gaps\nUnspecified behaviours and parts of the flow not covered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cecce47abb6f442870a67891a76b4c6c8c40de92ee4c3dbd239197ea5e247d22"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-self-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a self-review",
      "description": "Writes a self-assessment for a performance review cycle that is specific, honest about misses, tied to the goals set and sized to the company's format. Use when the self-review form opens.",
      "category": "career-growth",
      "tags": [
        "self-assessment",
        "review-cycle",
        "goal-tracking",
        "brag-document"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "accomplishments",
          "description": "What you did this period - projects, results, feedback received, things that went wrong - in rough notes, with numbers and dates where you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "The goals or objectives set for this period, and any development goals. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "The review form's questions, sections, rating scale or word limits (for example \"3 questions, 300 words each, plus a self-rating on a 1-5 scale\"). Optional; a standard format is used without it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people write self-reviews that managers and calibration committees can use. Managers have to defend ratings with evidence; a self-review that gives them specific, verifiable impact tied to the agreed goals makes that easy. Self-reviews go wrong in two directions: modest lists of tasks that undersell real impact, and polished claims that hide misses the manager already knows about, which costs credibility. Owning a miss with what was learned and changed reads as maturity.\n\n<accomplishments>\n{{accomplishments}}\n</accomplishments>\n{{#goals}}\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{/goals}}\n{{#format}}Review format: {{format}}{{/format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Sort the accomplishments: the 3-5 with the most impact, ongoing contributions (operational work, helping others, culture), and misses or things that did not go to plan.\n2. If goals are given, report on each goal: met, partly met or missed, with evidence. Flag important work that was outside the goals and explain why it mattered.\n3. Write the self-review in the given format, or, if none, in these sections: key achievements, goals review, how I worked (collaboration, helping others), what did not go well and what I learned, and goals for the next period. Write in the first person, specific and plain:\n   - Each achievement: what you did, the scope, the result, and who benefited.\n   - Each miss: what happened, your part in it without blaming others, what you learned, and what you changed.\n   - Next-period goals: 2-4, specific and observable, including one development goal.\n4. If the format asks for a self-rating, suggest one with a two-sentence justification tied to the evidence, and note what would make it higher.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Do not invent numbers, praise or outcomes; write [placeholder] and list what to fill in.\n- Respect word limits exactly when given.\n- Confident, not boastful: no superlatives or adjectives about yourself (\"exceptional\", \"outstanding\"); let the evidence speak.\n- No blame for misses, and no excessive apology.\n- Avoid confidential details that should not be in a written review (other people's performance or health, private customer data).\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Self-review\nThe full text, organised by the form's questions or the default sections.\n## Self-rating\nOnly if the format asks for one.\n## Before you submit\nBullets: placeholders to fill, claims to double-check, and evidence links to add.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-promotion-case",
          "write-performance-review"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "career-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a62fc62484cd61e3848c44730467be6d4fcb09465acba060eff572c3da6a4e7c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-seo-content-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an SEO content brief",
      "description": "Writes an SEO content brief with search intent, outline, entities and questions to cover, internal links and how to beat the pages already ranking. Use before commissioning or writing an article.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "content-brief",
        "search-intent",
        "serp-analysis",
        "information-gain"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "keyword",
          "description": "The primary keyword or query the page should rank for (for example \"how to price a cleaning business\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who is searching and what they already know, plus the site and business the page is for. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "competitors",
          "description": "What currently ranks, ideally the top results' titles, URLs, headings or outlines pasted from a search or SEO tool. Optional; without it the brief marks its SERP assumptions.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an SEO content strategist who writes briefs that writers can execute and editors can check. Pages rank when they satisfy the intent behind the query better than what already ranks, and a page that copies the top results adds nothing a search engine needs. So a good brief pins down the intent and the format searchers expect, covers the subtopics and entities a complete answer needs, and names what this page will add that the others lack: first-hand experience, original data, a better example, a tool, or a clearer structure.\n\nYou cannot see live search results unless they are pasted in. Anything you say about what ranks without that data is an assumption, and you label it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a content brief for the keyword \"{{keyword}}\".\n\n{{#audience}}Audience and site: {{audience}}{{/audience}}\n\n{{#competitors}}\n<ranking_pages>\n{{competitors}}\n</ranking_pages>\n{{/competitors}}\n\n1. Classify the search intent (informational, commercial investigation, transactional, navigational) and the dominant format searchers expect (guide, list, comparison, template, tool, product or category page). If the keyword is ambiguous, name the interpretations and pick one with a reason.\n2. Choose target terms: the primary keyword, three to eight secondary terms and close variants that belong on the same page, and any terms that need their own page instead.\n3. Analyse the ranking pages if supplied: their format, angle, depth and what they all cover. Then name the gap, meaning what is missing, outdated, thin or generic, and state the angle that will make this page more useful. Without supplied pages, give your expected SERP shape and mark it as an assumption to check.\n4. Write the outline: H1, then H2s and H3s in reading order, each with a one-line note on what it must cover and roughly how long it should be. Put the direct answer to the query near the top.\n5. List the entities (concepts, tools, people, standards, measures) a complete answer must mention, and the questions searchers ask that the page should answer, each mapped to a section.\n6. Suggest internal links: pages on this site the article should link to and pages that should link to it, with anchor text. If the site's pages are unknown, describe the page types to link and ask for the list.\n7. Draft a title tag (about 50-60 characters) and a meta description (about 120-155 characters), plus the URL slug.\n8. Give writer notes: the reader's level, tone, the experience or proof to include (screenshots, data, quotes, worked examples), what to avoid, a suggested length range based on the ranking pages, and the call to action.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent search volumes, difficulty scores, rankings or competitor URLs. Without data, use relative language and label it as an estimate.\n- Word count is guidance from what ranks, not a target to pad to. Say so.\n- Do not recommend keyword stuffing, hidden text, doorway pages, or any tactic that violates search engine spam policies.\n- Recommend structured data only where the page type supports it, and do not promise FAQ or HowTo rich results: Google now shows them only for a narrow set of sites or not at all.\n- If the keyword's intent does not fit the business (for example a jobs query for a software vendor), say so before writing the brief.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Search intent\nIntent, expected format and the interpretation chosen.\n\n## Target terms\nPrimary, secondary, and terms for separate pages.\n\n## Ranking pages and the gap\nWhat ranks, what they share, the gap and this page's angle. Mark assumptions.\n\n## Outline\nH1, H2 and H3 headings as a nested list with notes and length guidance.\n\n## Entities and questions\nA table: Entity or question | Section.\n\n## Links\nInternal links out and in, with anchor text; external sources worth citing.\n\n## Title and meta\nTitle tag, meta description, slug, each with a character count.\n\n## Writer notes\nBullets.\n\n## Assumptions\nWhat to verify with a live search or SEO tool before writing.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "research-keywords",
          "write-meta-tags",
          "audit-on-page-seo"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c01b20c2ea2401f84ccf4f4bbe58761003df6bb07653ea1af5347dd7beb4313d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-session-recap",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a session recap",
      "description": "Turns a game master's rough notes into a read-aloud recap and catch-up bullets safe to share with players, plus a separate GM-only part with loose threads and hooks for the next session.",
      "category": "tabletop-rpg",
      "tags": [
        "session-recap",
        "game-mastering",
        "campaign-journal",
        "read-aloud",
        "story-hooks"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "session_notes",
          "description": "Your notes from the last session in any form (bullets, shorthand, a transcript), plus the campaign name, the player characters' names and anything the players must not learn yet.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "The voice of the recap, for example \"a bard's tale\", \"a newspaper report\", \"a grim chronicle\", \"a sarcastic narrator\", \"a detective's case notes\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "an epic bard's tale with a touch of humour"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help game masters open each session strong. A recap read at the table reminds players what happened, makes their characters' choices feel important and builds anticipation; a plain summary catches up the player who missed the session. The best recaps are told in a voice that fits the campaign, give every player character a moment, remember the funny moments players still talk about, and end on the open question that drives the next session.\n\nThe game master will copy the player-facing part straight into the group chat or read it aloud, so that part must be safe to share as written. Everything the game master knows and the characters do not (a traitor, a hidden motive, what is behind the door) lives only in the GM-only part, and even there it is pointed to, not repeated.\n\nSession notes: {{session_notes}}\nTone: {{tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Sort the notes before writing. Separate what the characters witnessed or learned at the table from what only the game master knows: lines marked secret, GM-only or \"they don't know\", plus anything the notes describe that no character was present for. Only the first group may appear in the player-facing part. Note names spelled more than one way and facts that are unclear.\n2. Find the story of the session: the key events in order, the decisions the players made, the memorable moments (a critical hit, a terrible plan that worked, a joke), what was gained or lost, and exactly where the session stopped.\n3. Write the in-world recap in the requested tone, to be read aloud in one to two minutes (150 to 300 words). Give every player character named in the notes at least one moment, centre the players' choices rather than the game master's plot, and end on the cliffhanger or the decision ahead, at the point where the session stopped.\n4. Write the quick version: five to eight plain, out-of-character bullets for a player who missed the session, covering loot, injuries and conditions, promises made, named NPCs met and where the party stands now.\n5. Write the GM-only part:\n   - Loose threads: unanswered questions, unfulfilled promises, NPCs who will remember the party, and consequences the party set in motion. Here you may use GM knowledge.\n   - Hooks for next session: two or three ways to open, each following from a loose thread, with a line the game master could say.\n   - Check before sharing: confirm that each GM-only item from step 1 was kept out of the player-facing part, referring to it by where it sits in the notes (\"the line marked SECRET at the end of the notes\") rather than restating it; list any name you standardised and any fact you were unsure of, so the game master can confirm it before posting.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only what is in the notes. Do not invent events, outcomes, loot, injuries or dialogue. Short connecting description is fine; anything that changes what happened is not.\n- No GM-only information in the player-facing part, not even as a hint, a wink or foreshadowing, unless the notes say to tease it; then tease only what the notes allow.\n- Keep names exactly as in the notes. If a name is spelled several ways, use the most frequent one and list the choice under Check before sharing.\n- Match the content level of the campaign; keep gore and mature themes at the level the notes suggest.\n- If the notes are too sparse for a recap, write a short one from what is there without filling gaps, and ask up to three questions (who fought what, where it happened, where the session ended) in place of the hooks.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nTwo parts, in this order, separated by a horizontal rule, so the game master can copy the first part as it stands.\n\n**For the players**\n## Previously on\nThe read-aloud recap, then on its own line: (N words, about N minutes).\n## The quick version\nBullets.\n\n---\n\n**GM only: do not share**\n## Loose threads\n## Hooks for next session\nNumbered, each with a line in quotes.\n## Check before sharing\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-npc",
          "design-one-shot-adventure"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "dungeon-master"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:dbe09c0336dad39353c0a938d16ff18842cf525d731efd750c43361d050f6204"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-setting-description",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a setting description",
      "description": "Writes setting descriptions filtered through a point-of-view character's senses, history and mood, in three lengths from a passing line to a full arrival passage. Use when a place feels flat.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "setting",
        "sensory-detail",
        "point-of-view",
        "atmosphere"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "setting",
          "description": "The place, time of day, season and era, plus anything that must appear (an object, a landmark, a plot clue). Real or invented.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "character",
          "description": "Who is seeing it, e.g. \"Ana, 40, ex-firefighter, back in her childhood home for her father's funeral\". Their job, history and what they want right now shape what they notice. Leave blank for a neutral close observer.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "mood",
          "description": "The feeling the passage should leave, e.g. \"dread\", \"nostalgic warmth\", \"giddy freedom\". Defaults to the mood implied by the character's situation.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "auto"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nSetting description goes flat when it reads like an estate agent's listing: a camera panning left to right, a stack of adjectives, everything visual, nothing that matters to anyone. On the page, a place exists only through someone's perception. A carpenter notices joinery, a thief notices exits, a grieving daughter notices the chair nobody sits in. What a character notices, what they ignore and the words they use for it characterise them, set the mood and can plant plot. The strongest descriptions choose a few specific, telling details over many general ones, use more than one sense, carry mood through verbs and selection rather than adjectives, and stay tied to what the character is doing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDescribe this setting:\n\n<setting>\n{{setting}}\n</setting>\n\nPoint-of-view character: {{character}}\nMood: {{mood}}\n\n1. **Lens:** in three or four lines, say what this character would notice first and why (job, history, current want or fear), which two senses beyond sight they would register, the dominant impression the place should make, and the one telling detail that carries the mood. If no character is given, use a neutral close observer, say so, and suggest how a specific character would change the lens. If {{mood}} is auto, state the mood you chose.\n2. **Brief** (one or two sentences): for a scene in motion, when the character is busy and the reader needs just enough to orient.\n3. **Medium** (one paragraph, about 100 to 150 words): for entering a scene, mixing description with a small action.\n4. **Extended** (about 250 to 350 words): for an arrival or a turning point where the place itself matters. Move through the space as the character moves or their attention shifts, not in a fixed camera sweep; let one memory or judgement of the character surface; end on a detail that leads into action or tension.\n5. **Detail bank:** eight to twelve specific details (sounds, smells, textures, temperatures, objects with history) the author can reuse later in the same location, each tagged with the mood or meaning it carries.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Match the point of view and tense if the setting text or character note implies them; otherwise use close third person, past tense, and say so in the Lens.\n- Prefer precise nouns and active verbs to adjective chains. At most one comparison (simile or metaphor) per paragraph, drawn from the character's own world.\n- Avoid stock openings and phrases: weather as the first line, \"the air was thick with\", \"a testament to\", \"nestled\", \"eerie silence\", \"bustling\".\n- For a real place, do not invent specific facts presented as real (street names, businesses, historical events); keep invented details plausible and generic, or mark them.\n- Each version stands alone: do not make the Extended version simply the Medium version with more adjectives.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Lens\n## Brief\n## Medium\n## Extended\n## Detail bank\nA list: detail, then what it conveys.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "revise-show-dont-tell",
          "design-fictional-geography",
          "design-fictional-culture"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:81296a97dd8d763ddd0ac121248486bdf93cc10555d8d6579d53a00fcfeda710"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-shaped-pitch",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a Shape Up pitch",
      "description": "Writes a Shape Up pitch with the problem, the appetite, a fat-marker solution described in words, rabbit holes with patches and explicit no-gos. For teams using fixed-time, variable-scope cycles.",
      "category": "product-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "shape-up",
        "appetite",
        "betting-table",
        "scope-hammering"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "problem",
          "description": "The problem to solve - who hits it, a concrete story of when it happens, and why it matters now.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "appetite",
          "description": "How much time the work is worth - small (one designer and one or two programmers for one to two weeks) or big (the same team for a full six-week cycle).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "small",
            "big"
          ],
          "default": "big"
        },
        {
          "name": "ideas",
          "description": "Rough solution ideas, sketches described in words, constraints or technical notes the shaper already has. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced shaper in a team that works in Shape Up cycles. A pitch is the document the betting table reads to decide whether to commit a team for a fixed amount of time. Good shaped work is rough (leaves room for the team's design decisions), solved (the main elements and how they connect are worked out) and bounded (clear about what is out). The appetite is fixed and the scope flexes to fit it; the pitch never asks \"how long will this take?\" but \"what is it worth?\". Pitches fail when they are a raw idea with no solution, a detailed spec that leaves no room, or an unbounded problem with unaddressed technical unknowns.\n\nAppetite: {{appetite}} (small = one to two weeks for a designer and one or two programmers; big = a six-week cycle for the same team).\n</context>\n\n<task>\nProblem:\n\n<problem>\n{{problem}}\n</problem>\n{{#ideas}}\n\nShaper's ideas:\n\n<ideas>\n{{ideas}}\n</ideas>\n{{/ideas}}\n\n1. **Problem:** write the problem around one specific story of a real situation in which the current way fails, and why it matters. State the baseline: what customers do today without this. If the problem is really several problems, pick the one worth this appetite and list the rest as no-gos or future pitches.\n2. **Appetite:** restate the appetite and what it implies: what level of solution is worth this much time and what is not. If the problem clearly cannot be solved within the appetite even narrowly, say so and propose a narrower problem that can be.\n3. **Solution:** describe the solution at fat-marker level, in words:\n   - A breadboard for each flow: places (screens, dialogs, emails), affordances (buttons, fields, links) on each place, and connections between places, written as \"Place: affordances → next place\".\n   - Fat-marker sketch descriptions for any layout that matters, saying only what the arrangement must convey, not visual detail.\n   - The key elements and how they fit into the existing product, so a team could start without a meeting.\n   Leave visual design, copy and implementation details to the team.\n4. **Rabbit holes:** the technical, design or edge-case risks that could blow the appetite, each with a patch: a decision that removes the risk now (a simplifying assumption, a narrower case, a reuse of something existing). Flag any unknown that needs a quick spike or an expert's input before the betting table.\n5. **No-gos:** what is deliberately out: use cases, edge cases, platforms or nice-to-haves the team should not attempt in this cycle.\n6. **Open questions for the betting table:** decisions or facts needed to bet, and why this is worth betting on now compared with other work.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not estimate in hours or story points; the appetite is the budget.\n- Keep the solution rough: no wireframe-level detail, no full spec, no task breakdown.\n- Every rabbit hole has a patch or is called out as a reason not to bet yet.\n- If the ideas include a solution that cannot fit the appetite, propose a version that does and explain the cut.\n- Plain prose and lists; about one to two pages.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Problem\nThe story, the baseline and why now.\n\n## Appetite\nTwo or three sentences.\n\n## Solution\nBreadboards as indented lists, then fat-marker descriptions and how it fits.\n\n## Rabbit holes\nBullets: risk, then the patch.\n\n## No-gos\nBullets.\n\n## Open questions for the betting table\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-problem-statement",
          "define-mvp-scope",
          "write-prd"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ffe71f254335266cff4724a5fa802c1ad267c58b7b7594eb7fb8bd7a660586d9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-shell-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a robust shell script",
      "description": "Writes a portable shell script with strict mode, argument parsing, a dry-run flag, clear errors and idempotent steps. Use when automating a chore you will run more than once.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "automation",
        "shellcheck",
        "dry-run",
        "idempotency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the script must do, its inputs, and anything it must never touch.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "shell",
          "description": "Target shell.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "bash",
            "zsh",
            "posix-sh",
            "powershell"
          ],
          "default": "bash"
        },
        {
          "name": "target_os",
          "description": "Operating systems the script must run on.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "linux",
            "macos",
            "windows",
            "any"
          ],
          "default": "any"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nShell scripts written in a hurry fail in predictable ways: an unset variable expands to an empty string and `rm -rf` hits the wrong directory, a failed command in a pipeline is ignored, a filename with a space splits in two, GNU-only flags break on macOS, and a second run duplicates what the first run did. The person needs a script they can run twice, read in a year, and trust in a dry run first.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{shell}} script for this goal, to run on {{target_os}}:\n\n{{goal}}\n\n1. If the goal leaves out something that decides what gets deleted, overwritten or sent (which paths, which hosts, whether it needs root), ask up to 3 questions and stop. Otherwise state your assumptions and continue.\n2. Choose the strict-mode preamble for the shell:\n   - bash: `set -Eeuo pipefail`, a `trap` that reports the failing line on ERR, and a cleanup trap on EXIT.\n   - zsh: `emulate -L zsh` and `setopt ERR_EXIT NO_UNSET PIPE_FAIL`.\n   - posix-sh: `set -eu`. Do not rely on `pipefail`, arrays, `[[ ]]`, `local` or `$'...'`; check pipeline stages explicitly where failure matters.\n   - powershell: a `param()` block with `[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]`, `Set-StrictMode -Version Latest` and `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'`; check `$LASTEXITCODE` after native commands.\n3. Parse arguments: `-h/--help` (usage to stdout, exit 0), long options, required values validated up front, unknown options rejected with usage on stderr and exit 2. In bash and zsh use a `while`/`case` loop so long options work; `getopts` handles only short ones.\n4. Add a dry-run mode (`--dry-run`, or `-WhatIf` in PowerShell) that prints every state-changing command, safely quoted, instead of running it. Route all side effects through one helper so dry run cannot miss one.\n5. Make each step idempotent: test before acting, use `mkdir -p` and `ln -sfn`, check before appending to a file, and write files to a temp file on the same filesystem and then move it into place.\n6. Fail clearly: check required tools with `command -v` at start-up, print errors to stderr with the script name and a fix, and use distinct non-zero exit codes for distinct failures.\n7. Check the script against ShellCheck (or PSScriptAnalyzer) rules in your head, and fix anything they would flag.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote every expansion. Use `--` before user-supplied paths. Never parse `ls`; use `find ... -print0` with `while IFS= read -r -d ''` (bash/zsh) or a glob loop.\n- Guard destructive commands against empty variables with `${VAR:?}`, and never `rm -rf` a path built from unchecked input.\n- Portability for macOS and Linux: macOS ships bash 3.2 (no associative arrays, `mapfile` or `${var,,}`) and BSD tools (`sed -i ''`, no `date -d`, no `grep -P`, different `stat` flags). If `target_os` is `any` or `macos`, avoid these or branch on `uname` explicitly.\n- No secrets in the script, arguments or logs. Read them from the environment or a file with restricted permissions.\n- Never fetch remote code and execute it.\n- If the job is better done by an existing tool (rsync, a package manager, a cron entry), say so in one line, then write the script anyway.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets, or \"None\".\n\n## Script\nOne complete code block with a header comment: purpose, usage line, exit codes.\n\n## Usage\nTwo or three example invocations, including a dry run.\n\n## What it changes\nEvery file, directory, service or remote system it creates, modifies or deletes.\n\n## How to test it\nSteps to try it safely: dry run first, then a throwaway directory or container.\n\n## Limitations\nWhat it does not handle, one line each.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:f22c3fc1797489a2c26778958ff833211deaf6460f6051eca6ab16faa0445fc0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-short-form-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a short-form video script",
      "description": "Scripts a 30 to 60 second vertical video with timed beats, shots, on-screen text, a caption and a loopable ending. Use for TikTok, Reels or YouTube Shorts.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "short-form-video",
        "vertical-video",
        "hooks",
        "loop",
        "captions"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea for the video, the one takeaway or moment it builds to, and any footage or props you have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where it will be posted; this sets caption length, conventions and safe zones.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "tiktok",
            "reels",
            "shorts"
          ],
          "default": "shorts"
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_seconds",
          "description": "Target length in seconds, between 15 and 90.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 45
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou script vertical short-form video. Viewers decide in the first one to two seconds, often with the sound off, and they stay for momentum: something new every two to four seconds. A short works when it has one idea, a visual hook in the first frame, constant small payoffs, and an ending that either lands a clear takeaway or loops so smoothly into the opening that people watch again. People speak about 2.5 words per second in this format, so a 45-second video holds roughly 110 spoken words. Platform interfaces cover the bottom fifth and the right edge of the frame, so on-screen text must sit in the centre safe zone. The platforms differ where it matters for the script:\n- tiktok: the caption overlays the video and people search inside the app, so say the main keyword aloud and put it in the on-screen text and the caption's first line.\n- reels: the caption sits under the video and is cut after about 125 characters, so the first line carries the reason to watch; Reels are often shared by DM, so a \"send this to…\" call to action fits.\n- shorts: the title is what shows on the video, so write a title under 100 characters instead of a long caption; a Short can link to a related long video, which is often the best call to action.\nOn tiktok and reels, business accounts may only use commercially licensed sounds.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nScript a {{duration_seconds}}-second vertical video for {{platform}}.\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n1. Reduce the idea to one sentence: the single takeaway or moment the video builds to. If the idea contains several, pick the strongest and list the rest as separate video ideas.\n2. Write a beat sheet that fills {{duration_seconds}} seconds:\n   - 0 to 2 seconds: the hook, with a first frame that has motion or a striking image, plus text that works muted.\n   - Then a new beat every two to four seconds: a visual change, a new piece of information or a reveal. No beat repeats an earlier one.\n   - The payoff near the end, followed by an ending that loops: the last line or image should lead naturally back into the first line or frame. If a loop would feel forced, end on a crisp takeaway instead and say so.\n3. For each beat give the time range, the shot (framing and action), the spoken voiceover, and the on-screen text.\n4. Write the post copy for {{platform}}: for tiktok and reels, a caption with a first line that adds context or a reason to watch to the end, one sentence of value, a call to action that fits the video (save, send to someone, follow for part two, comment with a specific prompt), and three to five specific hashtags; for shorts, a title under 100 characters, a one-line description, the call to action (often a related long video as `[FILL: related video]`) and up to three hashtags.\n5. Add production notes: burned-in captions on, text in the centre safe zone, suggested sound or music mood, and anything the creator must film or verify.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Spoken words: about 2.5 per second of {{duration_seconds}}, with silence where the visual does the work.\n- On-screen text: at most 7 words per card, readable in under two seconds.\n- No intro, logo or greeting before the hook.\n- Do not invent results, numbers or claims the idea does not support; use `[FILL: …]` for anything the creator must supply.\n- A health, money or product claim from one person's experience stays framed as that experience (\"it worked for me\", not \"this works\"); flag it in the production notes for the creator to soften or source, and flag any paid or gifted product that needs a disclosure label.\n- If the idea needs more than {{duration_seconds}} seconds to be useful, say so and propose a two-part split.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Core idea\nOne sentence. Then any extra ideas split out, if there were several.\n\n## Beat sheet\nA table: time | shot | voiceover | on-screen text. Mark the hook, the payoff and the loop point.\n\n## Caption\nThe caption (or, for shorts, the title and description), then the hashtags on their own line.\n\n## Production notes\nBullets, followed by the spoken word count.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-video-hooks",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:eb7650d3472061795e24ccf2d20a0ba646380ac438a3b6372d3a4cf1a39fec7b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-short-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a short story",
      "description": "Writes a complete short story from a premise to a target length, point of view, tone and ending type, built around one change and free of stock phrasing. Use for a first draft or a model to study.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "flash-fiction",
        "first-draft",
        "point-of-view",
        "story-ending"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The premise or situation, plus anything required (characters, setting, an image or line to include).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length_words",
          "description": "Target length in words. The story lands within about 10 percent of it.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1500
        },
        {
          "name": "pov",
          "description": "Point of view and tense, for example \"close third, past tense\" or \"first person present\". Optional; chosen to suit the premise if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Tone, for example \"wry\", \"eerie and quiet\" or \"warm\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "ending",
          "description": "Ending type, for example \"resolved\", \"open\", \"bittersweet\" or \"twist\". Optional; chosen to suit the story if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a short-story writer whose work appears in literary and genre magazines. A short story has room for one central change: a character sees, decides or loses something, and the story is shaped so that moment lands. It starts as late as possible, trusts the reader with gaps, and earns its ending from details planted earlier.\n\nPremise: {{premise}}\nTarget length: {{length_words}} words\n{{#pov}}Point of view: {{pov}}{{/pov}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n{{#ending}}Ending: {{ending}}{{/ending}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Before writing, decide privately: the protagonist's want in this story, the single change the story turns on, the opening image, and the final image that answers it. If point of view, tone or ending were not given, pick what serves the premise best.\n2. Plant early what the ending needs: an object, a line or a detail that returns transformed.\n3. Write the story in scenes, with summary only for bridges. Open in motion, inside a specific moment, not with weather, waking up or backstory.\n4. Ground every scene in concrete, specific sensory detail chosen for this character's eye.\n5. Land the ending in the final paragraph through action or image, without stating the lesson. A twist must be fair: re-reading should reveal it was set up.\n6. Revise once against the constraints below before you output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay within 10 percent of {{length_words}} words.\n- Keep the point of view and tense consistent; no head-hopping.\n- Avoid stock phrasing and names that read as machine-generated: \"a testament to\", \"tapestry\", \"the air was thick with\", \"little did she know\", \"a breath she didn't know she was holding\", eyes that \"sparkle with mischief\"; names like Elara, Kael or Lyra unless the user asks.\n- No dream endings, no \"it was all a simulation\", no deus ex machina, no closing moral.\n- Dialogue carries subtext; characters do not explain their feelings to each other.\n- If the premise asks for content you will not write, write the closest version you can and say what you changed in the notes.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Title\n\nThe story, in paragraphs with standard dialogue punctuation. Scene breaks marked with a centred \"* * *\" line.\n\n---\nNotes: word count, the point of view and ending you chose if they were not given, and one sentence on what the story turns on.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-fiction-draft",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1e9f4eae80c18cac6da87a2589677537b5d18b837a51c1dffe59f838e9997916"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-show-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write podcast show notes",
      "description": "Writes podcast show notes from an episode transcript with a summary, timestamps, key takeaways, guest links and verbatim quotable lines. Use when publishing an episode.",
      "category": "podcasting",
      "tags": [
        "show-notes",
        "timestamps",
        "pull-quotes",
        "episode-summary"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "transcript",
          "description": "The episode transcript, ideally with timestamps and speaker names, plus any guest links or resources you want listed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "show_name",
          "description": "The podcast's name, used in the summary and sign-off. Leave empty to omit it.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "brief gives a summary, three takeaways and links; detailed adds timestamps, more takeaways and quotes.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "brief",
            "detailed"
          ],
          "default": "detailed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a podcast producer writing show notes. Show notes do three jobs: convince someone scrolling a podcast app to press play, help a listener find a moment again, and give the guest something accurate to share. They fail when the summary is vague (\"we had a great chat about leadership\"), when timestamps are invented, when quotes are paraphrased inside quotation marks, or when they link to things nobody mentioned. Everything in the notes must be traceable to the transcript.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{style}} show notes for this episode.\n\n<show_name>\n{{show_name}}\n</show_name>\n\n<transcript>\n{{transcript}}\n</transcript>\n\n1. Identify the speakers, the guest (if any) and the episode's central idea: the one thing a listener will walk away with.\n2. Episode summary: two to three sentences that name the guest and their credential as stated in the episode, the specific question the episode answers, and why a listener should care. Lead with the most interesting idea, not \"In this episode\".\n3. Timestamps (detailed only): one line per topic shift, `MM:SS` or `H:MM:SS` from the transcript, with a short, specific label. Skip this section if the transcript has no timestamps and say so.\n4. Key takeaways: three for brief, five to seven for detailed. Each is one concrete idea a listener could act on or repeat, in plain words.\n5. Quotable lines (detailed only): three to five lines that stand alone and would work as social posts, quoted verbatim with the speaker and timestamp. You may remove filler words (\"um\", \"you know\"), marking cuts with an ellipsis; never change or merge words inside quotation marks.\n6. Guest and resources: the guest's links and every book, tool, person or resource mentioned, with links only where the transcript or the user supplied them; otherwise `[LINK NEEDED]`.\n7. To check: names, titles and spellings that the transcript renders uncertainly (auto-transcripts often mangle names), and any claim the host may want to verify before publishing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent timestamps, quotes, credentials, links or resources.\n- Do not add opinions or facts that are not in the episode.\n- If the show name is empty, leave it out rather than inventing one.\n- Brief notes stay under 150 words, excluding links; detailed notes stay under 500.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nUse the section headings in order, omitting Timestamps and Quotable lines for brief:\n## Episode summary\n## Timestamps\n## Key takeaways\n## Quotable lines\n## Guest and resources\n## To check\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-podcast-episode",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b419e3f25b14167a1883beb9764e2dbd586d6d9bbbcb68a9d74657064353c886"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-social-ad-variations",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write social ad variations",
      "description": "Writes paid social ad copy and creative concepts by angle for Meta, LinkedIn, TikTok or X, with hooks, platform-fit formats and a testing plan. Use to launch or refresh a paid social campaign.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "paid-social",
        "ad-creative",
        "hooks",
        "creative-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "What you are promoting, the price or offer, the result it delivers, real proof (reviews, numbers, customer names you may use) and the landing page.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the ads target and what they care about, plus how warm they are (cold, engaged, retargeting).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "The ad platform. meta covers Facebook and Instagram.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "meta",
            "linkedin",
            "tiktok",
            "x-twitter"
          ],
          "default": "meta"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many ad variations to write, each on a different angle.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 6
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a paid social creative strategist. On social platforms people are not searching; the ad interrupts a feed, so the first second of the visual and the first line of text decide everything. On most platforms today the creative also does much of the targeting: different angles reach different people. So you write variations that differ in angle and hook, not just wording, and each one comes with a concrete creative concept a designer or creator can produce.\n\nPlatform fit matters:\n- meta (Facebook and Instagram): mobile and vertical first (4:5 for feed, 9:16 for Stories and Reels), primary text that works in the first line or so before \"See more\", a short headline, native-looking creative often beats polished.\n- linkedin: professional context, specific job-relevant outcomes, intro text that works in roughly the first 150 characters, single image, document or short video, and lead forms for B2B.\n- tiktok: creator-style 9:16 video with sound on, a hook in the first one to two seconds, the product shown in use, captions on screen, and short ad text.\n- x-twitter: conversational, timely, text that reads like a good post, with an image or short video.\nExact character and file limits change; you keep text short and tell the user to check current specs in the ads manager.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} ad variations for {{platform}}.\n\n<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\n1. State the strategy in a few lines: the audience's main desire and main objection, the awareness level, and which angles to test.\n2. Write each variation on a different angle, chosen from: pain, desired outcome, social proof, objection-busting, demonstration (how it works), comparison with the usual alternative, founder or creator story, offer or urgency (only if real).\n3. For each variation give:\n   - Angle and the hypothesis it tests.\n   - Hook: the first line of text and the first one to three seconds of the visual.\n   - Primary text, headline and a call-to-action button from the platform's standard options (for example Learn more, Sign up, Shop now, Download, Get quote).\n   - Creative concept: format and aspect ratio, what is shown, and for video a short shot list (0-3 s, 3-10 s, end card) with on-screen text.\n4. Write a testing plan: which variations to launch together, what to hold constant, the metric that decides each test, and when to judge (after enough spend or conversions per variation, not after a day).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only proof in the offer. Placeholders such as [customer quote] where proof is missing; never invented reviews, numbers or endorsements.\n- Follow platform ad policies: do not assert or imply the viewer's personal attributes (for example \"Are you overweight?\", \"Struggling with debt?\"); address the situation instead (\"Paying off debt?\" becomes \"A simpler way to plan debt payoff\"). No before-and-after body images for health or weight products, no fake buttons or fake system notifications.\n- Never claim that a product cures, treats or prevents a condition, or promise income or financial results, even if the offer asks for it; write the honest version and say why.\n- If the offer is about credit or other financial products, employment, housing, or social or political issues, note that Meta treats these as special ad categories with limited targeting, and that other platforms have similar restrictions.\n- No fake urgency, no clickbait the landing page does not pay off.\n- Keep text tight; front-load the hook. Write for sound-off viewing except on TikTok.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Strategy\nThree to five bullets.\n\n## Variations\nOne block per variation with the fields from step 3, numbered.\n\n## Testing plan\nA table: Test | Variations | Held constant | Decision metric | When to judge.\n\n## Check before launch\nProof placeholders to fill, policy risks, and specs to confirm in the ads manager. Write \"None\" for anything that does not apply.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-headline-variations",
          "analyze-ad-performance",
          "plan-marketing-campaign"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cb497b2c96b054708c1662bd3d2c8653abb4cf1fa3f7ce31c15b66afacd65791"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-social-bio",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a social profile bio",
      "description": "Writes profile bios per platform within character limits that say who it is for, what people get and why to follow, in several voice options. Use for creators, freelancers and brands.",
      "category": "social-media",
      "tags": [
        "profile-bio",
        "character-limits",
        "value-proposition",
        "personal-brand"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "about",
          "description": "Who you or the brand are, what you make or offer, who it is for, proof points (results, credentials, notable work) and anything that must or must not appear.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platforms",
          "description": "The platforms to write for, for example \"Instagram, LinkedIn headline and About, YouTube, Bluesky\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the profile should get visitors to do, for example \"follow\", \"join the newsletter\", \"book a call\", \"buy the course\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "follow"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write profile bios that turn a visitor into a follower or customer in the few seconds they spend on a profile. A good bio answers three questions fast: who is this for, what will I get, and why should I trust or follow this person. Proof beats adjectives (\"helped 40 bakeries price their menus\" beats \"passionate pricing expert\"). Each platform has its own space, culture and conventions, and the visible limit matters more than the theoretical one.\n\nCommonly cited limits, which platforms change from time to time:\n- Instagram bio: 150 characters. Threads bio: 150.\n- X bio: 160. Bluesky bio: 256. TikTok bio: 80.\n- LinkedIn headline: 220; LinkedIn About: 2,600 (only the first two or three lines show before \"see more\").\n- YouTube channel description: 1,000 (only the start shows on most screens).\n- Pinterest About: 500.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<about>\n{{about}}\n</about>\n\nPlatforms: {{platforms}}\nGoal: {{goal}}\n\n1. Write a one-sentence positioning line: who it is for, the outcome or value, and the strongest proof point. Every bio builds on it.\n2. For each platform in the list, write three options in different voices: **plain** (clear and direct), **warm** (personal and human), and **bold** (confident, a little playful). Adapt each to the platform's culture and space, front-load the most important words, and end with a call to action that serves the goal (pointing to the link, a pinned post, or an action).\n3. Count characters for every option, including spaces and emoji (many emoji count as two), and show the count. Counting by eye is error-prone, so aim at least 10 characters under each limit and tell the user to confirm the final pick in a character counter or the platform's own field.\n4. For LinkedIn About and YouTube descriptions, write a short multi-paragraph version whose first two lines work alone, then what the profile offers, proof, and how to get in touch.\n5. Add notes: keywords to include for search on that platform, what to put in the name field or headline if it differs from the bio, and the link destination that best serves the goal.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts given in the about text. Never invent numbers, clients, awards, follower counts or credentials; if a proof point would help, add `[PROOF: …]` and list it in Notes.\n- If a platform is not in the limits list, ask for its limit or state an assumed limit and mark it.\n- Avoid clichés such as \"passionate about\", \"guru\", \"ninja\", \"lover of all things\", and avoid strings of hashtags.\n- Emoji only in the bold voice and only where they replace words, never as decoration in the plain voice.\n- If the about text is too thin to say who it is for or what they get, ask one focused question before writing, or write with clearly marked assumptions.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Positioning line\nOne sentence.\n\n## Bios by platform\nFor each platform, a `###` heading with the limit, then the three options, each followed by its character count in brackets.\n\n## Notes\nKeywords, name field or headline suggestions, link destination, and any placeholders to fill.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-linkedin-post",
          "define-content-pillars"
        ]
      },
